PG.notes("l277", "<p class=\"summary\">Overview of what React is, why it is worth learning and the core ideas behind it: reusable components, a component tree, mixing markup/style/logic per component, and efficient re-rendering through diffing.</p>\n<h3>What React is</h3>\n<ul><li>React describes itself as a JavaScript library for building user interfaces; in practice it is treated as a front-end framework.</li><li>Goal: build UIs faster and with less repeated code, e.g. listing grids like Airbnb's where every tile shows the same kinds of fields (rating, badge, price) with different data.</li><li>Popularity is cited via Stack Overflow surveys (most loved/wanted), Google Trends overtaking Angular and Vue, and job listings that commonly ask for React plus Node. Used by companies such as Airbnb, Uber, Facebook and Netflix.</li></ul>\n<h3>Components and the component tree</h3>\n<ul><li>A component is a reusable, customisable piece of UI. Write it once, then render it many times with different data.</li><li>A whole app is modelled as a tree: App contains NavBar and ToDoList; ToDoList contains many ListItem components; each ListItem contains Checkbox and ItemText.</li><li>Components are used like custom HTML elements, so page structure reads as a clean nested layout instead of long Bootstrap-style HTML blocks.</li><li>Benefits: less code, modular and scalable structure, easier to debug since each piece is isolated.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mixing HTML, CSS and JS per component</h3>\n<ul><li>Earlier modules kept HTML, CSS and JS in separate files. React instead bundles a small amount of each into every component: its own markup, its own styling and its own behaviour.</li><li>Because each component manages itself, one part of a page can update independently (an infinite-scroll feed loading more posts, a post receiving a new comment) without the rest of the page refreshing.</li></ul>\n<h3>Efficient re-rendering (diffing)</h3>\n<ul><li>When data or user input changes, React compares the new desired DOM tree with the current one, finds the differences, and updates only the affected parts (for example one checkbox going from unchecked to checked).</li><li>This is why React apps feel fast and interactive.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> This comparison mechanism is commonly called the virtual DOM and reconciliation.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l278", "<p class=\"summary\">Preview of the module: several small React practice apps plus the main Keeper project, and the React and ES6 topics that will be covered along the way.</p>\n<h3>Projects in the module</h3>\n<ul><li>Small apps: a registration screen, a real-time clock, an emoji dictionary and a to-do list.</li><li>Main project: Keeper, a clone of Google Keep where the user adds notes for new ideas.</li></ul>\n<h3>React topics ahead</h3>\n<ul><li>JSX, components, props, containers, the virtual DOM, state management, declarative programming and hooks such as useState.</li></ul>\n<h3>ES6 (ES2015) topics ahead</h3>\n<ul><li>Destructuring, the spread operator, map and filter, and import/export modules; the module doubles as a deeper JavaScript lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l279", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces CodeSandbox as the browser-based editor used for the React module, explains why it replaces local VS Code here, and outlines the learn-then-practice rhythm of the lessons.</p>\n<h3>What CodeSandbox offers</h3>\n<ul><li>Browser-based development environment with instant hosting: every project gets a shareable URL for friends, colleagues or a portfolio.</li><li>Designed with React in mind: no manual React install, built-in npm dependency management, and live collaboration.</li><li>Free to use. Sign-up (via the link in the course resources) works with GitHub, Google or Apple accounts.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why not local VS Code for this module</h3>\n<ul><li>React releases change details frequently and the course cannot be re-recorded each time, so a shared environment keeps everyone on identical React versions and avoids deprecation surprises.</li><li>Provided starter and final sandboxes mean everyone sees the same code and tooling, so learning focuses on fundamentals (components, hooks, functional style) instead of setup problems.</li><li>Local development is still possible; the next lesson is a text guide for it, but it is not recommended until the basics are solid. Docs cover any later version changes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Module structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Pattern: a lesson teaches one skill in a demo sandbox, then a separate practice sandbox lets the learner apply it.</li><li>After enough skills are built, the Keeper app project is introduced in several parts, each applying earlier skills.</li></ul>\n<h2>The task: Create a CodeSandbox account</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Link to codesandbox.io in the lecture resources</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Be able to open and fork the course's starter sandboxes</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open the CodeSandbox link from the course resources.</li><li>Click sign in.</li><li>Choose GitHub, Google or Apple to register.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Logged-in CodeSandbox account ready for the following lessons.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> CodeSandbox's UI and templates change over time, and the reference repo uses local Vite projects instead, so sandbox links may differ from what the repo shows.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l280", "<p class=\"summary\">Optional text guide for running the module's React projects locally with Node, VS Code and Vite instead of CodeSandbox.</p>\n<h3>Prerequisites</h3>\n<ul><li>Node.js (LTS version) and VS Code installed, and basic comfort with a terminal. The instructor still recommends CodeSandbox for following the videos; local work means adapting differences from the video code.</li><li>Each lesson ships a downloadable zip you can use for the starter code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Local workflow with Vite</h3>\n<ul><li>Scaffold the project with npm create vite@latest, pick the React framework and the JavaScript variant, then cd into the project.</li><li>npm install fetches dependencies into node_modules; npm run dev starts the Vite dev server, which recompiles and reloads on every save.</li><li>Open the printed local address (typically http://localhost:5173/) in the browser.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Create and run a local React app with Vite</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">npm create vite@latest my-react-app -- --template react\ncd my-react-app\nnpm install\nnpm run dev\n# open http://localhost:5173/</pre>\n<h2>The task: Optional local React setup</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Node LTS and VS Code installed</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Run a Vite React app locally</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open a terminal in the folder where the project should live.</li><li>Run the Vite scaffold command, accept the install prompt, choose React then JavaScript.</li><li>cd into the new folder and run npm install.</li><li>Run npm run dev and open the local URL shown.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Default Vite + React page served at localhost:5173 that hot-reloads on edits.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer npm versions need the extra -- before --template for the flag to reach the template; without it npm may swallow the flag and the CLI asks for a framework interactively.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's later lessons are Vite projects (src/main.jsx, src/App.jsx, index.html with a div#root), matching this setup.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l281", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through the first React code: the single-div HTML shell, rendering an h1 into the root div, what JSX is, how Babel compiles it to plain JavaScript, import vs require, and why render accepts only one top-level element.</p>\n<h3>Starter sandbox and project layout</h3>\n<ul><li>The lesson uses a CodeSandbox starter linked in the resources. Fork it (GitHub login) to keep your own copy; free accounts have a limit on draft projects, so download finished projects as a zip.</li><li>Files: public/index.html (the only one with code at first), src/index.js (empty) and src/styles.css (empty).</li><li>index.html is a bare skeleton: a head linking the stylesheet, and a body with a single div whose id is root, followed by a script tag loading index.js.</li><li>By convention a React app always has one div with id root; everything React builds is injected inside it. After the first setup this HTML file is not edited again (except the title), all work happens in JavaScript.</li></ul>\n<h3>Installing and loading React packages</h3>\n<ul><li>In CodeSandbox, dependencies are added by searching for them in the Dependencies panel and clicking, instead of npm install in a terminal. Needed here: react and react-dom.</li><li>Installing is not enough; each file that uses a package must load it. Old style: const React = require('react'); const ReactDOM = require('react-dom') (module names lowercase).</li><li>Modern style: import React from 'react'; import ReactDOM from 'react-dom'. import/export (ES6 modules) organise dependencies better; a later lesson covers them in depth.</li></ul>\n<h3>Rendering with ReactDOM.render (lesson's React version)</h3>\n<ul><li>ReactDOM.render(what, where, callback?) takes three arguments: the element to show, the DOM node to put it in, and an optional callback run when rendering completes (not needed here).</li><li>What to show: &lt;h1&gt;Hello world!&lt;/h1&gt; written directly as JSX with no quotes.</li><li>Where to show it: document.getElementById('root') (plain DOM API returning the root div).</li><li>Result: an h1 appears on the page although index.html was never changed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In React 18+ (including the Vite reference repo) ReactDOM.render is replaced by createRoot(container).render(element), imported from 'react-dom/client'. Same idea: choose a container, render an element.</li></ul>\n<h3>What JSX is and how Babel fits in</h3>\n<ul><li>JSX is HTML-like syntax written inside a JavaScript file, with no special delimiters such as EJS tags.</li><li>A compiler converts JSX to ordinary JavaScript before it reaches the browser. That compiler is Babel, which tooling in the React setup runs for you.</li><li>Demo: removing the React import breaks the page with React is not defined, because compiled JSX calls React functions (createElement).</li><li>Babel is a JavaScript compiler that turns next-generation JS (ES6 and later, arrow functions, map, etc.) into code every browser understands, JSX included. Its website has a Try it out pane showing input and compiled output side by side.</li><li>Compiled output for &lt;h1&gt;Hello world!&lt;/h1&gt; is a React.createElement('h1', null, 'Hello world!') call.</li><li>Equivalent vanilla DOM code needs several lines: createElement, set innerHTML, getElementById('root'), appendChild. JSX gives the same result in one readable line.</li><li>Because Babel handles compatibility, ES6+ features can be used freely in React code, which is why the module teaches many of them.</li></ul>\n<h3>Only one root element</h3>\n<ul><li>render accepts a single element. Placing an h1 and a p side by side causes a syntax error.</li><li>Fix: wrap siblings in one parent such as a div; anything nested in a div still counts as one element, so any number of children can be rendered.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Modern React also allows a fragment (&lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;) as the wrapper to avoid adding an extra div to the DOM; the reference repo App.jsx uses it.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Lesson code (older React API), src/index.js</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom&quot;;\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello world!&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;This is a paragraph.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Same idea in the Vite reference repo (React 18+), src/main.jsx and App.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// main.jsx\nimport { StrictMode } from 'react'\nimport { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client'\nimport App from './App.jsx'\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(\n  &lt;StrictMode&gt;\n    &lt;App /&gt;\n  &lt;/StrictMode&gt;,\n)\n\n// App.jsx\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This is Introduction to jsx and babel&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/&gt;\n  )\n}\nexport default App</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Roughly what Babel produces from JSX</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">React.createElement(\n  &quot;div&quot;, null,\n  React.createElement(&quot;h1&quot;, null, &quot;Hello world!&quot;),\n  React.createElement(&quot;p&quot;, null, &quot;This is a paragraph.&quot;)\n);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Render your first JSX</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter sandbox with the root div in index.html and empty index.js</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Display an h1 and a paragraph using React</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Change the page title in index.html to JSX.</li><li>In index.js, add react and react-dom as dependencies and import both.</li><li>Call ReactDOM.render with an h1 reading Hello world! and the root element from getElementById.</li><li>Add a p element and observe the error, then wrap both in a div.</li><li>Open Babel's Try it out page and compare JSX with its compiled output.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Page shows a heading and a paragraph generated entirely from JavaScript.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Two sibling top-level elements in render cause a syntax error; wrap them.</li><li>Removing the React import (older setups) breaks JSX with React is not defined.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo is a Vite app with React 18+/19, so index.js becomes main.jsx and ReactDOM.render is no longer used.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l282", "<p class=\"summary\">Practice challenge: build a React app from scratch that renders a heading and an unordered list of three items, then a walkthrough of the solution including the single-parent rule and how the editor's auto-format handles empty elements.</p>\n<h3>Setup of the challenge</h3>\n<ul><li>Fork the provided CodeSandbox so changes are saved to your account. index.html already contains the div#root; index.js has been emptied so the whole React app is written from scratch.</li><li>The code comments in the sandbox list the steps to complete one by one.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Import React from 'react' and ReactDOM from 'react-dom' (both packages are already installed in the sandbox, but must be imported before use).</li><li>Call ReactDOM.render with two minimum arguments: what to show and where to show it. Start with an h1 heading, with document.getElementById('root') as the target.</li><li>To add a ul next to the h1, wrap both in a div, because render takes one element only; the div can have any number of children.</li><li>Add three li elements inside the ul with content (bacon, noodles, a third food).</li><li>Saving (Cmd/Ctrl+S) runs the formatter (prettifier). An li with nothing inside gets rewritten as a self-closing tag (&lt;li /&gt;), like img or link; adding text content avoids that rewrite.</li></ul>\n<h3>Learning approach</h3>\n<ul><li>Repeating the from-scratch render steps builds muscle memory. A completed sandbox is linked in the resources for comparison.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution in the lecture's older React API</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom&quot;;\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Bacon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Noodles&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Mushrooms&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Equivalent from the reference repo (Vite, App.jsx; author's extra content stripped)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Bacon&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Noodles&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Jamon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: JSX code practice: favourite foods list</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Forked CodeSandbox with empty index.js and a div#root in index.html</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a React app from scratch showing an h1 and an unordered list with three list items</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Fork the sandbox.</li><li>Import React and ReactDOM.</li><li>Call ReactDOM.render with a wrapper div as the first argument, containing an h1 heading and a ul.</li><li>Add three li elements with text to the ul.</li><li>Pass document.getElementById('root') as the second argument.</li><li>Save and check the preview.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Page with a heading and a bulleted list of three favourite foods.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom&quot;;\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Bacon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Noodles&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Mushrooms&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Rendering a heading and a list as siblings without a wrapper throws a syntax error.</li><li>Empty elements are rewritten as self-closing tags by the formatter; give list items text.</li><li>The reference repo's App.jsx goes beyond the task (variables, extra ol, notes text); the core requirement (h1, ul, 3 li) is satisfied.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l283", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to embed JavaScript inside JSX with curly braces, why only expressions (not statements) are allowed, and several ways to build a full name including ES6 template literals.</p>\n<h3>Curly braces inject JavaScript into JSX</h3>\n<ul><li>Text placed inside a JSX element is treated as a literal string. Writing Hello name prints the word name, not the variable.</li><li>Wrapping something in {} switches back to JavaScript: &lt;h1&gt;Hello {name}&lt;/h1&gt; outputs the value stored in the const name.</li><li>JSX therefore works in both directions: HTML inside a JS file, and JS inside that HTML.</li></ul>\n<h3>Any expression works</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside the braces you can put any JavaScript expression: a variable, maths such as {3 + 9}, or a function call like {Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)}, which yields a random whole number from 0 to 9 and changes on each page refresh.</li><li>Math.random() gives a decimal in [0,1); multiply by 10 to scale it, and Math.floor rounds down.</li></ul>\n<h3>Expressions vs statements</h3>\n<ul><li>An expression evaluates to a value. A statement (if/else, loops) performs work and does not produce a value, so it is not allowed in the braces; an if (name === ...) block there is a syntax error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> For conditional output inside JSX use the ternary operator or &amp;&amp; instead; these are covered in a later lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Building a full name: three approaches</h3>\n<ul><li>Concatenation inside one pair of braces: {fName + &quot; &quot; + lName}.</li><li>Two separate brace pairs with a literal space between them: {fName} {lName}. Multiple brace pairs in one element are fine.</li><li>ES6 template literal: backticks with ${} placeholders, for example <code>${fName} ${lName}</code>. Wrapped in JSX braces this nests three levels (string inside template inside JSX inside JS), so the simpler options are generally clearer.</li><li>Any approach that gives the right output is acceptable.</li></ul>\n<h3>Rules of thumb</h3>\n<ul><li>Multiple top-level elements still need a single wrapper such as a div.</li><li>The completed sandbox is in the resources to experiment with.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">All the variants from the lesson in one render call</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const fName = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nconst lName = &quot;Yu&quot;;\nconst num = 7;\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello {fName + &quot; &quot; + lName}&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello {fName} {lName}&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello {`${fName} ${lName}`}&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Your lucky number is {num}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Random: {Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Lucky number and full name</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter sandbox rendering one h1 from a name const</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Display values from JavaScript variables and expressions inside JSX</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Replace the hard-coded text with {name} to render a const.</li><li>Create a const num set to your lucky number and add a paragraph with the text Your lucky number is plus {num}.</li><li>Wrap the h1 and p in a div so render receives one element.</li><li>Experiment with expressions such as {3 + 9} and a random number using Math.random and Math.floor.</li><li>Create fName and lName consts and show the full name in an h1 in at least two different ways.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Heading showing the full name, paragraph showing the lucky number (or a random/calculated value).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const fName = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nconst lName = &quot;Yu&quot;;\nconst num = 7;\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello {fName + &quot; &quot; + lName}&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello {fName} {lName}&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello {`${fName} ${lName}`}&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Your lucky number is {num}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Random: {Math.floor(Math.random() * 10)}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting braces prints the variable name as plain text.</li><li>Statements such as if/else inside braces cause syntax errors; only expressions are allowed.</li><li>Sibling elements without a parent wrapper break render.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l284", "<p class=\"summary\">Challenge to render two paragraphs, a credit line from a name constant and a copyright line with a dynamically computed current year, followed by the solution using new Date().getFullYear().</p>\n<h3>Challenge requirements</h3>\n<ul><li>Build a React app from scratch in a forked sandbox: two p elements.</li><li>First paragraph: Created by plus a name stored in a const. Second: copyright text with the current year, computed by JavaScript rather than hard-coded.</li><li>Mixes React rendering, JSX braces and plain JavaScript (Date API); looking up the Date method online is encouraged.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution steps</h3>\n<ul><li>Import React and ReactDOM, then call ReactDOM.render with a wrapper div (single root) holding both paragraphs, and document.getElementById('root') as the target.</li><li>Declare const name and a placeholder year const (e.g. 2019) to confirm braces work: &lt;p&gt;Created by {name}&lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>To get the real year: create a Date object with new Date() (current date and time), then call its getFullYear() method, which returns the four-digit year as a number.</li><li>Logging the date object to the console helps confirm what it holds before extracting the year.</li><li>Use the result in JSX either through a const (const year = new Date().getFullYear()) or directly inline as {new Date().getFullYear()}, since the call evaluates to a value.</li></ul>\n<h3>Common mistake: missing parentheses</h3>\n<ul><li>getFullYear without () is a reference to the function, not its result. React logs that functions are not valid as a React child, and nothing useful renders.</li><li>The parentheses call the method so the expression resolves to a number.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Lecture solution (older React API)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom&quot;;\n\nconst name = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nconst currentDate = new Date();\nconst year = currentDate.getFullYear();\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Created by {name}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Copyright {year}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);\n// Inline alternative: &lt;p&gt;Copyright {new Date().getFullYear()}&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo equivalent (Vite App.jsx); the student's extra lorem-ipsum paragraphs and h2/h3 swapped for the lecture's two p elements</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const name = &quot;Your Name&quot;;\nconst currYear = new Date().getFullYear();\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Created by {name}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Copyright {currYear}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Dynamic copyright footer</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Practice sandbox with comments describing the steps and index.html containing div#root</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Display Created by &lt;name&gt; and Copyright &lt;current year&gt; where the year updates automatically</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Fork the sandbox.</li><li>Import React and ReactDOM.</li><li>Create const name with your name.</li><li>Obtain the current year using new Date().getFullYear() (look it up if needed).</li><li>Call ReactDOM.render with a div containing two paragraphs that use braces for name and year.</li><li>Render into the element returned by document.getElementById('root').</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Two paragraphs: Created by YOURNAME and Copyright with the current year (no hard-coded value).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom&quot;;\n\nconst name = &quot;Angela&quot;;\nconst currentDate = new Date();\nconst year = currentDate.getFullYear();\n\nReactDOM.render(\n  &lt;div&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Created by {name}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Copyright {year}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;,\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)\n);\n// Inline alternative: &lt;p&gt;Copyright {new Date().getFullYear()}&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Omitting the parentheses on getFullYear.</li><li>Rendering the two paragraphs without a wrapper element.</li><li>Hard-coding the year defeats the purpose of the exercise.</li><li>Reference repo diverges: it uses an h2/h3 and extra paragraphs, not the two p elements the lecture requested.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l285", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how HTML attributes carry over into JSX with two changes: names become camelCase (class becomes className) and values can be JavaScript expressions in curly braces. Also covers styling React output with an ordinary CSS file, self-closing tags, and alt text.</p>\n<h3>Styling React output with a normal stylesheet</h3>\n<ul><li>React renders ordinary HTML elements, so everything known about CSS still applies: define a class (e.g. .heading { color: red; }) in styles.css and attach it to an element in the JSX.</li><li>Element selectors (ul { color: blue; }), class selectors and id selectors all work against React-rendered DOM exactly as on a static page.</li><li>Blanket element selectors are rarely a good idea in a real app because they restyle every matching element everywhere; classes give targeted control.</li><li>The React docs prefer styling through classes in a CSS file, applied via className, over inline styles (inline styles come in the next lecture).</li></ul>\n<h3>class becomes className</h3>\n<ul><li>JSX is compiled to JavaScript calls, and the DOM property for an element's classes in JavaScript is className, so JSX uses className instead of class.</li><li>Writing class often still works because React tolerates it, but the browser console logs a warning about an invalid DOM property and it can cause subtle problems, so always use className.</li><li>CSS class names follow kebab-case (food-img), while the attribute name itself is camelCase (className).</li></ul>\n<h3>Telling the tooling the file contains JSX</h3>\n<ul><li>In the CodeSandbox set-up of the time, a warning about an unexpected angle bracket in index.js meant the script was being treated as plain JavaScript. The fix was to set the script tag's type attribute in index.html so the file is handled as JSX rather than plain JS.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In a modern Vite project (as in the reference repo) none of this is needed: files ending in .jsx are compiled automatically and index.html loads src/main.jsx as a module.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> main.jsx uses createRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(&lt;StrictMode&gt;&lt;App /&gt;&lt;/StrictMode&gt;) to mount the app.</li></ul>\n<h3>Multi-word attributes are camelCased</h3>\n<ul><li>HTML attributes are all lowercase (contenteditable, spellcheck); in JSX the convention follows JavaScript naming, so they become contentEditable and spellCheck.</li><li>Editor autocomplete in a JSX file suggests the camelCase forms, which is a quick way to find the right spelling.</li><li>contentEditable={true} makes the element editable in the browser (click the h1 and type); {false} turns it back off. Using the all-lowercase HTML spelling breaks the behaviour.</li><li>spellCheck={false} stops Chrome drawing red spelling underlines in an editable element.</li><li>Single-word attributes (src, href, alt, id) are unchanged.</li></ul>\n<h3>Expressions as attribute values</h3>\n<ul><li>Instead of a quoted string, an attribute can take a JavaScript expression wrapped in curly braces: src={img} where img is a const holding a URL.</li><li>Any expression works, such as concatenation: src={imgURL + '?grayscale'} appends a query string that makes the Picsum placeholder service return a greyscale image.</li><li>Quotes mean a literal string; braces mean evaluate this JavaScript. Do not combine them (src='{img}' would be the literal text).</li><li>Lorem Picsum (picsum.photos/size) returns a random photo for a given size, handy for placeholder images.</li></ul>\n<h3>Self-closing tags are mandatory</h3>\n<ul><li>HTML forgives a missing slash on void elements like img; JSX does not. An element with no children must be written &lt;img ... /&gt; or JSX raises an 'Unterminated JSX contents' style error.</li><li>The same applies to every void element: br, hr, input, and so on.</li></ul>\n<h3>Alt text and linter warnings</h3>\n<ul><li>The sandbox ran an ESLint-based linter that warns when an img has no alt attribute.</li><li>alt should describe the picture for screen-reader users (e.g. alt='bacon'); adding it clears the warnings and improves accessibility.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">CSS used in the lesson (class for the heading and fixed-size images)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.heading {\n  color: red;\n}\n\n.food-img {\n  height: 100px;\n  width: 100px;\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">JSX with className, an expression-valued src, and alt text (based on the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const img = &quot;https://picsum.photos/200&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot; contentEditable={true} spellCheck={false}&gt;\n        My Favourite Foods\n      &lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Bacon&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Jamon&lt;/li&gt;\n        &lt;li&gt;Noodles&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n      &lt;div&gt;\n        &lt;img className=&quot;food-img&quot; src={img} alt=&quot;random&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;img className=&quot;food-img&quot; src={img + &quot;?grayscale&quot;} alt=&quot;random, greyscale&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Disable spellcheck, then build an image row</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A forked CodeSandbox (or Vite project) rendering an h1 and a list of three foods, with styles.css linked.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Practise JSX attributes by turning off spellcheck on an editable heading, and replacing the list with three equally sized images.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Make the h1 editable with contentEditable={true}, then look through the HTML global attributes list to find the one that disables spellcheck.</li><li>Convert that attribute to camelCase (spellCheck) and set it to {false}; type nonsense into the heading to confirm no red underline appears.</li><li>Delete the ul and replace it with a div containing three img elements whose src values are image URLs found via image search.</li><li>In styles.css create a class (e.g. .food-img) setting height and width to 100px.</li><li>Add className=&quot;food-img&quot; to each image (multi-cursor editing with Alt/Option-click speeds this up).</li><li>Store a Picsum URL in a const and use it as an image's src with curly braces; then append '?grayscale' via an expression.</li><li>Give every img an alt value to clear the linter warnings.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Three (or four) 100x100 images in a row below a red, editable heading with no spellcheck underlines, and no console or linter warnings.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">&lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot; contentEditable={true} spellCheck={false}&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;div&gt;\n  &lt;img className=&quot;food-img&quot; src={img} alt=&quot;random&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;img className=&quot;food-img&quot; src=&quot;https://example.com/bacon.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;bacon&quot; /&gt;\n  &lt;img className=&quot;food-img&quot; src=&quot;https://example.com/noodles.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;noodles&quot; /&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using class instead of className produces a console warning.</li><li>Writing contenteditable or spellcheck in lowercase silently loses the behaviour in JSX.</li><li>Forgetting the self-closing slash on img is a compile error in JSX.</li><li>Quoting an expression (src=&quot;{img}&quot;) passes the literal text instead of the variable.</li><li>The reference repo's styles.css targets li (blue) rather than ul, and its alt texts are all just 'img'; use descriptive alt text instead.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l286", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains inline styling in JSX: the style attribute takes a JavaScript object (not a string), which produces the double-curly-brace syntax. Properties are camelCased, values are strings, and the main benefit is that styles can change at runtime by updating the object.</p>\n<h3>style takes an object, not a string</h3>\n<ul><li>In plain HTML, style=&quot;color: red&quot; is a string. In JSX, a string value triggers a console error saying style expects a mapping of properties to values.</li><li>The value must be a JavaScript object: { color: &quot;red&quot; }. Keys behave like property names (unquoted), values are usually strings, and pairs are separated by commas rather than semicolons.</li><li>Because a JSX attribute needs curly braces to hold JavaScript, and the object literal has its own braces, the result is style={{ color: &quot;red&quot; }}. The outer pair means 'JavaScript expression here'; the inner pair is the object itself.</li></ul>\n<h3>Property names and values</h3>\n<ul><li>CSS properties written in kebab-case must be converted to camelCase as object keys: font-size becomes fontSize, background-color becomes backgroundColor.</li><li>Values are strings, including multi-part values such as &quot;1px solid black&quot; for border. Spaces are preserved inside the string.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Plain numbers are allowed for many properties and React appends px (fontSize: 20 gives 20px), but unitless-capable properties such as lineHeight and opacity are left as numbers.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pulling the style into a variable</h3>\n<ul><li>Move the object into a const (customStyle) declared above the component, then use style={customStyle}. This keeps the JSX readable and is the form used when the style needs to change.</li><li>The object can be changed before render, e.g. customStyle.color = &quot;blue&quot;, and the element picks up the new value the next time it renders, with no change to the JSX itself.</li><li>This is the headline reason to use inline styles: styles driven by data such as user actions, the time of day or app state. In later lessons this combines with state so the UI restyles itself.</li></ul>\n<h3>Class-based versus inline</h3>\n<ul><li>The React docs recommend classes in a stylesheet applied with className as the default approach; inline styles are the less common option reserved for dynamic values.</li><li>Inline styles cannot express pseudo-classes (:hover), media queries or keyframes, which is another reason classes stay the default. (Extra)</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Style object stored in a const, then applied (from the reference repo, typos fixed)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const customStyle = {\n  color: &quot;red&quot;,\n  fontSize: &quot;20px&quot;,\n  border: &quot;1px solid black&quot;\n};\n\n// Changing the object changes the rendered style on the next render\ncustomStyle.color = &quot;blue&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 style={customStyle}&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Inline form with double curly braces</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">&lt;h1 style={{ color: &quot;red&quot; }}&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Passing a string to style raises an error in React.</li><li>Using font-size (kebab-case) as a key is a syntax error; write fontSize or quote the key.</li><li>Separating declarations with semicolons inside the object is invalid; use commas.</li><li>Forgetting to quote values such as &quot;20px&quot; or &quot;1px solid black&quot; causes a syntax error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Mutating a plain const object only shows up when the component re-renders; later lessons use state to trigger that properly.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l287", "<p class=\"summary\">A challenge that combines JSX expressions, className and inline styles: render one h1 whose text and colour depend on the current hour. The solution reads the hour with Date, branches with if/else, and builds a style object.</p>\n<h3>What the exercise combines</h3>\n<ul><li>Embedding a JavaScript variable inside JSX text using curly braces.</li><li>Applying a ready-made CSS class with className.</li><li>Choosing an inline style object at runtime so the colour follows the data.</li><li>Plain JavaScript logic (if / else if / else) placed above the return statement of the component.</li></ul>\n<h3>Getting the current hour</h3>\n<ul><li>new Date() creates a Date for the current moment; calling .getHours() on it returns the hour as a number from 0 to 23 in local time (17 means 5 PM).</li><li>Logging the value to the console first is a quick way to confirm it before building on it.</li><li>The sandbox allowed setting a fixed date such as new Date(2019, 0, 1, 10) (month is zero-indexed) to test each branch without waiting for the real time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Branching on the hour</h3>\n<ul><li>Declare a greeting variable first, then fill it from the hour: below 12 is morning, from 12 up to (not including) 18 is afternoon, everything else is evening/night.</li><li>Because else-if is only reached when earlier conditions fail, each later branch only needs an upper bound.</li><li>The variable is then dropped into the heading with {greeting}.</li></ul>\n<h3>Class plus dynamic inline style</h3>\n<ul><li>The supplied .heading class (larger, bold, underlined with a bottom border) is applied with className=&quot;heading&quot;.</li><li>A customStyle object with a color key is declared before the branches. Each branch sets customStyle.color to red, green or blue along with the greeting.</li><li>The h1 receives style={customStyle}. Class and inline style combine; the inline colour wins over any colour in the class if both exist (inline styles have higher specificity). (Extra)</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution (reconstructed from the lecture and the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function App() {\n  const currentTime = new Date().getHours();\n  const customStyle = { color: &quot;&quot; };\n  let greeting = &quot;&quot;;\n\n  if (currentTime &lt; 12) {\n    greeting = &quot;Good Morning&quot;;\n    customStyle.color = &quot;red&quot;;\n  } else if (currentTime &lt; 18) {\n    greeting = &quot;Good Afternoon&quot;;\n    customStyle.color = &quot;green&quot;;\n  } else {\n    greeting = &quot;Good Night&quot;;\n    customStyle.color = &quot;blue&quot;;\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot; style={customStyle}&gt;\n      {greeting}\n    &lt;/h1&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Provided stylesheet</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">.heading {\n  font-size: 50px;\n  font-weight: bold;\n  border-bottom: 5px solid black;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Time-of-day greeting</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A blank React app with a styles.css that already contains a .heading class.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show a single h1 whose message and colour depend on the local hour.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Render a lone h1 (no wrapper div is needed) into the root element.</li><li>Get the current hour with new Date().getHours().</li><li>Choose the text: before 12 'Good morning', 12 to 18 'Good afternoon', otherwise evening/night, and insert it in the h1 with curly braces.</li><li>Apply the provided heading class with className.</li><li>Make the text red in the morning, green in the afternoon and blue at night using an inline style object.</li><li>Test each branch by temporarily faking the date.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> At about 4:55 PM the page shows a large, bold, green 'Good afternoon' with a black line under it; at 10 AM it is red and says morning; at 7 PM it is blue.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const currentTime = new Date().getHours();\nconst customStyle = { color: &quot;&quot; };\nlet greeting = &quot;&quot;;\nif (currentTime &lt; 12) { greeting = &quot;Good Morning&quot;; customStyle.color = &quot;red&quot;; }\nelse if (currentTime &lt; 18) { greeting = &quot;Good Afternoon&quot;; customStyle.color = &quot;green&quot;; }\nelse { greeting = &quot;Good Night&quot;; customStyle.color = &quot;blue&quot;; }\n// &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot; style={customStyle}&gt;{greeting}&lt;/h1&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>The brief says 'evening' for 6 PM to midnight but the solution wording says 'Good night'; either is accepted, just be consistent.</li><li>Reference repo diverges slightly: it treats 4 AM to noon as morning and uses var, a default of black and a fragment wrapper; the 0-4 AM hours therefore fall into the evening branch.</li><li>Conditions like 'greater than 12' skip exactly 12 o'clock; use less-than checks in an else-if chain to avoid gaps.</li><li>The style attribute needs an object, so a string such as &quot;red&quot; will not work.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The greeting is computed once per render, so it will not update as time passes without a re-render.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l288", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces React function components: a function with a PascalCase name that returns JSX, used as a custom tag. Shows splitting a Heading and a List out of one file into separate .jsx modules with default export and import, then the App component and components folder layout.</p>\n<h3>Why components</h3>\n<ul><li>Long single files are hard to read; components split a big page into small, focused pieces.</li><li>A component can be reused wherever the same markup is needed, e.g. two List components without rewriting the ul and li tags.</li><li>Real components (heading with wrapper div, nav bar, styles) are much larger than the tiny examples used here.</li></ul>\n<h3>Writing a function component</h3>\n<ul><li>A component is a JavaScript function that returns JSX: function Heading() { return &lt;h1&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;; }.</li><li>The name must be PascalCase (every word capitalised). React uses the capital first letter to tell a custom component from a built-in HTML tag; lowercase names are treated as DOM elements.</li><li>Use it like a tag: &lt;Heading /&gt;. When React reaches that tag it calls the function and puts the returned elements where the tag was.</li><li>Components without children should use a self-closing tag with a space before the slash (&lt;Heading /&gt;). This follows the Airbnb React/JSX style guide, a commonly cited convention reference; a linter may auto-fix it. The compiler does not care, it is for readability.</li><li>Do not add parentheses in the export (export default Heading), since that would call the function immediately instead of handing it over.</li></ul>\n<h3>One component per file (ES6 modules)</h3>\n<ul><li>Put each component in its own file named after it with the .jsx extension (Heading.jsx). Convention: the entry file (index.js / main.jsx) stays plain JS, components use .jsx.</li><li>Older setups required import React from &quot;react&quot; at the top of every component file so JSX could be compiled; the lecture does this each time. (Extra) With the modern JSX transform used by Vite and React 17+, this import is no longer needed, which is why the reference repo omits it.</li><li>At the bottom of the component file: export default Heading;. In the file that uses it: import Heading from &quot;./Heading&quot;; (relative path starting with ./).</li><li>File extensions are optional in the import path in the sandbox; the bundler resolves Heading to Heading.jsx.</li><li>Being the default export means the importer can pick any local name, but keeping it the same as the component is standard. (Extra)</li></ul>\n<h3>The App component and project structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Typical apps keep the entry file nearly empty: it only renders &lt;App /&gt; into the root element.</li><li>App.jsx defines the top of the component tree, returning a div containing &lt;Heading /&gt; and &lt;List /&gt;, and is exported as default.</li><li>A component must return one root element (here a div, or a fragment &lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;).</li><li>As the number of components grows, put them in a components folder (src/components) and update relative import paths accordingly, e.g. ./components/App. Larger apps further group by feature such as login or register screens.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">src/components/Heading.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Heading() {\n  return &lt;h1&gt;My Favourite Foods&lt;/h1&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default Heading;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">src/components/List.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function List() {\n  return (\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Bacon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Jamon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Noodles&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default List;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">src/App.jsx composing the two</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Heading from &quot;./components/Heading&quot;;\nimport List from &quot;./components/List&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Heading /&gt;\n      &lt;List /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">src/main.jsx entry point (Vite)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { StrictMode } from 'react'\nimport { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client'\nimport App from './App.jsx'\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById('root')).render(\n  &lt;StrictMode&gt;\n    &lt;App /&gt;\n  &lt;/StrictMode&gt;\n)</pre>\n<h2>The task: Extract a List component</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Sandbox where Heading has been extracted already, and the h1 plus ul still sit in the entry file.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Create a List component in its own file and use it in place of the inline ul.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create List.jsx inside src (use the new-file button, not new-folder).</li><li>Add the React import if the setup requires it, then define function List() returning the ul with its three li items.</li><li>Add export default List at the bottom.</li><li>In the entry/App file delete the ul markup, write &lt;List /&gt; as a self-closing tag, and import List from &quot;./List&quot;.</li><li>Optionally restructure so the entry file only renders &lt;App /&gt;, with App.jsx holding Heading and List inside a div, and move everything into a components folder, fixing the import paths.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The page looks identical to before (heading and list), but the markup lives in three small files.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// List.jsx\nfunction List() {\n  return (\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Bacon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Jamon&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;Noodles&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default List;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>A lowercase component name (heading) is treated as an HTML tag and renders nothing useful.</li><li>Writing export default Heading() calls the function instead of exporting it.</li><li>Forgetting to import a component gives a 'List is not defined' error.</li><li>Moving files into a folder breaks relative import paths until they are updated.</li><li>The new-folder and new-file buttons in the sandbox sit side by side; choose the correct one.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l289", "<p class=\"summary\">Practice in refactoring: take the time-of-day greeting code, which sits entirely in the entry file, and split it into a Heading component and an App component in separate .jsx files, leaving the entry file to just render App.</p>\n<h3>Target structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Entry file (index.js / main.jsx): only imports App and renders &lt;App /&gt;.</li><li>App.jsx: the root of the component tree. Imports Heading and returns &lt;Heading /&gt;. A wrapping div is normally used when there are several children; with a single child it can be omitted.</li><li>Heading.jsx: holds the greeting logic (hour lookup, greeting text, colour style object) and the h1.</li></ul>\n<h3>How the move works</h3>\n<ul><li>Move the logic that decides the greeting and style, not only the h1 markup, into the Heading module, since the h1 depends on those variables.</li><li>Wrap the markup in function Heading() { ... return (&lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot; style={customStyle}&gt;{greeting}&lt;/h1&gt;); } and export it with export default Heading.</li><li>Whether the logic sits above the function (computed once when the module loads) or inside it (recomputed on each render) both work for this demo. (Extra) Inside the function is the better habit, since the value then refreshes when the component re-renders; the reference repo puts it at module level.</li><li>In App.jsx import Heading from the Heading file, and in the entry file import App from &quot;./App&quot;. If the files later move into a components folder, the path becomes ./components/App.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Heading.jsx (reference repo style, logic inside the component for clarity)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Heading() {\n  const currentTime = new Date().getHours();\n  const customStyle = { color: &quot;&quot; };\n  let greeting;\n\n  if (currentTime &lt; 12) {\n    greeting = &quot;Good Morning&quot;;\n    customStyle.color = &quot;red&quot;;\n  } else if (currentTime &lt; 18) {\n    greeting = &quot;Good Afternoon&quot;;\n    customStyle.color = &quot;green&quot;;\n  } else {\n    greeting = &quot;Good Night&quot;;\n    customStyle.color = &quot;blue&quot;;\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot; style={customStyle}&gt;\n      {greeting}\n    &lt;/h1&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Heading;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Heading from &quot;./components/Heading&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return &lt;Heading /&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Split the greeting page into App and Heading</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A sandbox whose entry file contains the whole greeting logic and h1 (the result of the previous styling practice).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> End with the entry file rendering only &lt;App /&gt;, plus App.jsx and Heading.jsx.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Clear the logic and the h1 out of the entry file.</li><li>Create Heading.jsx, import React if the setup needs it, and define function Heading() containing the time logic and returning the styled h1.</li><li>Add export default Heading.</li><li>Create App.jsx importing Heading and returning &lt;Heading /&gt;, exported as default.</li><li>In the entry file import App and render &lt;App /&gt;.</li><li>Verify the page looks and behaves exactly as before.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Same greeting, colour and heading style as before the refactor, now from a two-level component tree.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx\nimport Heading from &quot;./components/Heading&quot;;\nfunction App() {\n  return &lt;Heading /&gt;;\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Leaving the greeting logic behind in the entry file leaves Heading with undefined variables.</li><li>Forgetting export default or the matching import gives a not-defined or missing-export error.</li><li>Relative paths must match where each file lives (./Heading vs ./components/Heading).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l290", "<p class=\"summary\">A focused look at ES6 modules: default exports, named exports, the matching import syntax, wildcard imports, and how this differs from Node's require. Demonstrated with a math.js module exporting pi, doublePi and triplePi into a React list.</p>\n<h3>Modules in general</h3>\n<ul><li>Any JavaScript file can be a module: it keeps its contents private unless it exports them, and other files pull in only what they need.</li><li>This is the mechanism behind import React from &quot;react&quot; and behind splitting React components across files; it is not React-specific.</li><li>math.js here is a plain JS file, not a component, so it uses the .js extension.</li></ul>\n<h3>Default export</h3>\n<ul><li>export default pi; exposes one main value. A file can have at most one default export.</li><li>The importer chooses the local name: import pi from &quot;./math.js&quot; or import PI from &quot;./math.js&quot; both receive the same value, as long as the code then uses the same name consistently.</li><li>The extension in the path is optional with bundlers like Babel/Vite; it was kept in the demo for visibility.</li></ul>\n<h3>Named exports</h3>\n<ul><li>Additional exports go in a braces list: export { doublePi, triplePi };. A file can have any number of these.</li><li>Import them with braces after the default import, separated by a comma: import PI, { doublePi, triplePi } from &quot;./math.js&quot;;.</li><li>Names inside the braces must exactly match the exported names; renaming on import requires the 'as' keyword (import { doublePi as double }). (Extra)</li><li>Exported functions are imported as functions: to display a result in JSX, call it, e.g. {doublePi()}.</li></ul>\n<h3>Wildcard import</h3>\n<ul><li>import * as pi from &quot;./math.js&quot;; collects every export into one namespace object, which must be given a name.</li><li>The object has the named functions as properties (pi.doublePi(), pi.triplePi()) and the default export under the property default (pi.default).</li><li>Style guides tend to discourage this: it hides which things are really used and loses the clarity of a clear default plus specific named imports.</li></ul>\n<h3>ES6 import/export versus Node require</h3>\n<ul><li>Before ES6 modules, Node code used const x = require(&quot;x&quot;). require is a Node feature, not part of the JavaScript language, and it does not work in browsers directly.</li><li>At the time of the lecture, browser support for ES6 modules was incomplete; Babel (compiling to older syntax) made ES6 safe in React apps. (Extra) Today all modern browsers support ES modules natively.</li><li>The instructor prefers import/export syntax for its clarity.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">math.js with one default export and two named exports</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const pi = 3.1415962;\n\nfunction doublePi() {\n  return pi * 2;\n}\n\nfunction triplePi() {\n  return pi * 3;\n}\n\nexport default pi;\nexport { doublePi, triplePi };</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Consuming the module in a component</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import PI, { doublePi, triplePi } from &quot;./math.js&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{PI}&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{doublePi()}&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{triplePi()}&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Wildcard version</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import * as pi from &quot;./math.js&quot;;\n\n// &lt;li&gt;{pi.default}&lt;/li&gt;\n// &lt;li&gt;{pi.doublePi()}&lt;/li&gt;\n// &lt;li&gt;{pi.triplePi()}&lt;/li&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting more than one export default in a file is a syntax error.</li><li>Named imports are case-sensitive and must match the export; a typo yields undefined or a missing-export error.</li><li>Writing {doublePi} instead of {doublePi()} in JSX tries to render the function itself rather than its result.</li><li>Omitting curly braces around named imports imports the default export instead.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l291", "<p class=\"summary\">Practice with named exports and imports: export four calculator functions from calculator.js and import them into the React app so a four-item list shows computed results. Solved both with named imports and with a wildcard import.</p>\n<h3>Exporting several functions</h3>\n<ul><li>calculator.js has add, multiply, subtract and divide and no default export, so they are exported as a named group: export { add, multiply, subtract, divide };.</li><li>Export the function itself, with no parentheses; adding parentheses would call it rather than export it.</li><li>Until the import is in place, the app reports errors such as 'add is not defined'.</li></ul>\n<h3>Importing them</h3>\n<ul><li>Named import: import { add, subtract, multiply, divide } from &quot;./calculator&quot;; The order inside the braces does not matter, only the exact names.</li><li>The source is a relative path to the file; the .js extension may be omitted.</li><li>Wildcard alternative: import * as Calculator from &quot;./calculator&quot;; then call Calculator.add(1, 2) etc. The namespace object holds one property per export.</li><li>Both approaches work and both appear in real code, so recognise each.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">calculator.js</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function add(n1, n2) {\n  return n1 + n2;\n}\n\nfunction multiply(n1, n2) {\n  return n1 * n2;\n}\n\nfunction subtract(n1, n2) {\n  return n1 - n2;\n}\n\nfunction divide(n1, n2) {\n  return n1 / n2;\n}\n\nexport { add, multiply, subtract, divide };</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx using named imports</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { add, multiply, subtract, divide } from &quot;./calculator&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{add(1, 2)}&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{multiply(2, 3)}&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{subtract(7, 2)}&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{divide(5, 2)}&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Wildcard variant</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import * as Calculator from &quot;./calculator&quot;;\n\n// &lt;li&gt;{Calculator.add(1, 2)}&lt;/li&gt;\n// &lt;li&gt;{Calculator.multiply(2, 3)}&lt;/li&gt;\n// &lt;li&gt;{Calculator.subtract(7, 2)}&lt;/li&gt;\n// &lt;li&gt;{Calculator.divide(5, 2)}&lt;/li&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Import the calculator functions</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A sandbox whose App renders four list items calling add, multiply, subtract and divide, plus calculator.js defining them without exporting, and a Final.png of the target.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the page render four calculated numbers by exporting and importing the functions.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open calculator.js and export all four functions as named exports.</li><li>In the file that renders the list, import add, multiply, subtract and divide with braces from the relative path of calculator.</li><li>Remove the 'not defined' errors and compare with Final.png.</li><li>Optionally redo it with import * as Calculator and prefix each call.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Four bullet points showing 3, 6, 5 and 2.5 (results of add(1,2), multiply(2,3), subtract(7,2), divide(5,2)).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { add, multiply, subtract, divide } from &quot;./calculator&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Exporting with parentheses (add()) calls the function instead of exporting it.</li><li>Names in the import braces must match the export names exactly.</li><li>With the wildcard form every call needs the namespace prefix.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l292", "<p class=\"summary\">Kicks off the section's capstone, the Keeper note-taking app. Part 1 asks for an App component that renders three child components (Header, Note, Footer), styled by matching the classes and element selectors already present in the supplied styles.css.</p>\n<h3>Starting project contents</h3>\n<ul><li>index.html is minimal: a &lt;title&gt;, a Google Fonts stylesheet link (McLaren for the logo, Montserrat for body text), a link to styles.css, and the usual empty &lt;div id=&quot;root&quot;&gt; that React renders into. (Extra) In the react-scripts starter the bundled index.js is injected automatically; the Vite version in the reference repo loads it explicitly with &lt;script type=&quot;module&quot; src=&quot;/src/main.jsx&quot;&gt;.</li><li>styles.css already holds every rule the finished app needs. The job is to write JSX whose element types and class names match those selectors, not to write CSS.</li><li>index.js contains the numbered challenge steps as comments.</li></ul>\n<h3>Working locally instead of in CodeSandbox</h3>\n<ul><li>Open the starting sandbox from the lecture resources and export it as a zip, then unzip it to get a normal project folder.</li><li>Drag the folder into VS Code (add to workspace); older practice folders can be removed from the workspace.</li><li>In a terminal, cd into the project folder (e.g. Downloads/keeper-app-part-1) and run npm install. That pulls in react, react-dom and react-scripts as listed in package.json.</li><li>Run npm start to launch the dev server and view the app at localhost:3000.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Newer Vite-based versions of the starter (as in the reference repo) use npm run dev instead, serve on localhost:5173, and have src/main.jsx as the entry file in place of index.js.</li></ul>\n<h3>What the finished Part 1 must contain</h3>\n<ul><li>A new React app with an App component that acts as the root of the tree.</li><li>Header component: renders a &lt;header&gt; element with the app name (Keeper, or any name you like) inside an &lt;h1&gt;.</li><li>Footer component: renders a &lt;footer&gt; element holding a &lt;p&gt; copyright line. The year must be computed with the JavaScript Date object, not typed as a fixed number.</li><li>Note component: a &lt;div&gt; containing an &lt;h1&gt; for the note title and a &lt;p&gt; for the body text.</li><li>The page should look like the sample finished app linked in the challenge. To get there, read styles.css and work out which classes and which HTML elements each rule targets.</li><li>Optional: restyle by editing styles.css, or delete it and write your own stylesheet from scratch.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference repo styles.css lives in public/ and is linked from index.html with href=&quot;/styles.css&quot;, so it applies globally without being imported into any component.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Shape of the component tree the challenge asks for (illustrative)</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">index.js  -&gt;  &lt;App /&gt;\n                ├── &lt;Header /&gt;  -&gt; &lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Keeper&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;\n                ├── &lt;Note /&gt;    -&gt; &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;&lt;h1/&gt;&lt;p/&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n                └── &lt;Footer /&gt;  -&gt; &lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Copyright ⓒ {year}&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The selectors in styles.css your JSX has to satisfy (excerpt)</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">header { background-color: #f5ba13; margin: auto -16px; padding: 16px 32px; }\nheader h1 { color: #fff; font-family: &quot;McLaren&quot;, cursive; font-weight: 200; }\nfooter { position: absolute; text-align: center; bottom: 0; width: 100%; height: 2.5rem; }\nfooter p { color: #ccc; }\n.note { background: #fff; border-radius: 7px; box-shadow: 0 2px 5px #ccc; padding: 10px; width: 240px; margin: 16px; float: left; }\n.note h1 { font-size: 1.1em; margin-bottom: 6px; }\n.note p { font-size: 1.1em; margin-bottom: 10px; white-space: pre-wrap; word-wrap: break-word; }</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Local setup commands (react-scripts starter)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd ~/Downloads/keeper-app-part-1\nnpm install   # installs react, react-dom, react-scripts\nnpm start     # dev server on http://localhost:3000</pre>\n<h2>The task: Keeper App Part 1: Header, Note and Footer components</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter sandbox (or exported local folder) with index.html, styles.css and an index.js listing the challenge steps as comments. No components exist yet.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Build an App component that renders Header, Note and Footer components so the page matches the sample Keeper app: yellow header bar with the app name, one white note card, and a grey copyright footer pinned to the bottom with the current year.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create the React app entry: in index.js import React and ReactDOM and render into document.getElementById('root').</li><li>Create App.jsx exporting an App function component; render &lt;App /&gt; from index.js.</li><li>Create Header.jsx that returns a &lt;header&gt; element wrapping an &lt;h1&gt; with the app name.</li><li>Create Footer.jsx that returns a &lt;footer&gt; element wrapping a &lt;p&gt; copyright message; compute the year with new Date().getFullYear() and insert it with curly braces.</li><li>Create Note.jsx that returns a &lt;div&gt; with an &lt;h1&gt; title and a &lt;p&gt; of content.</li><li>Import Header, Note and Footer into App and render them in that order.</li><li>Open styles.css, note which selectors target elements (header, header h1, footer, footer p) and which target a class (.note), and add className=&quot;note&quot; on the Note component's &lt;div&gt; so the card styling applies.</li><li>Compare with the sample app and adjust until it matches; then optionally customise the styles.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A yellow header strip with white McLaren-font title, a single white rounded card with a bold title and body text floated to the left, and light grey footer text centred at the bottom of the page showing the current year.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// Key parts of the reference solution (full walkthrough in the Part 1 Solution notes)\n// components/Header.jsx\nfunction Header() {\n  return (\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Keeper App&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Header;\n\n// components/Footer.jsx\nconst year = new Date().getFullYear();\nfunction Footer() {\n  return (\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Copyright © {year}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Footer;\n\n// components/Note.jsx\nfunction Note() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;This is the note title&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This is the note content&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Note;\n\n// App.jsx\nimport Header from &quot;./components/Header&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./components/Footer&quot;;\nimport Note from &quot;./components/Note&quot;;\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      &lt;Note /&gt;\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Hard-coding the year in the footer instead of computing it with the Date object.</li><li>Wrapping the Footer's text in a plain &lt;div&gt; or the Header's in a &lt;div&gt;: the CSS targets the header and footer elements themselves, so the styles won't apply.</li><li>Using class instead of className in JSX for the note card.</li><li>Forgetting npm install before npm start when running the exported project locally.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l293", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds Keeper App Part 1 step by step: an App root component in a components folder, then Header, Footer (with a computed year) and Note components. The main lessons are that the provided CSS only applies when JSX uses the exact elements it targets, and that attributes such as className must go on HTML elements inside a component, not on the custom component tag.</p>\n<h3>Start small and verify</h3>\n<ul><li>Begin with index.js rendering a plain &lt;h1&gt; into #root to prove the setup works before adding components.</li><li>Then swap that h1 for &lt;App /&gt; once App exists; seeing App's own placeholder text (e.g. 'Hello App') confirms the import and render are wired correctly.</li></ul>\n<h3>.jsx vs .js file extensions</h3>\n<ul><li>The challenge names files with .jsx. The Airbnb React style guide recommends .jsx so tools like Bitbucket or Atlassian products can recognise and highlight JSX syntax.</li><li>Functionally React does not care: components work fine in .js files. This is a style choice, and the lecture notes that many developers (including the instructor) now drop the x.</li><li>Rule: choose one convention for a project and use it consistently.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Vite-based projects (like the reference repo) do care: Vite expects JSX to live in .jsx files by default, so keep the x there.</li></ul>\n<h3>Component file boilerplate</h3>\n<ul><li>Keep components in a src/components folder. In this lecture App.jsx also lives there, so index.js imports it with import App from &quot;./components/App&quot; (extension omitted).</li><li>Every component file follows the same three steps: import React from &quot;react&quot;; define a function named in PascalCase that returns JSX; export default that function.</li><li>Use a default export because each file contains just one component.</li><li>Inside App, sibling component files are imported with a relative path from the same folder, e.g. import Header from &quot;./Header&quot;.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The import path's casing should match the file name exactly (./Header for Header.jsx). Some file systems are case-insensitive and let a mismatch slide locally, but Linux build servers will fail.</li></ul>\n<h3>Lowercase HTML elements vs PascalCase components</h3>\n<ul><li>In JSX, lowercase tags (&lt;header&gt;, &lt;footer&gt;, &lt;div&gt;) are native HTML elements; capitalised tags (&lt;Header /&gt;, &lt;Footer /&gt;) are your own components.</li><li>That capitalisation is how React tells the two apart, so a component named header would be treated as an HTML tag.</li><li>Custom components with no children are written as self-closing tags: &lt;Header /&gt;.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The App in the reference repo returns a fragment (&lt;&gt;...&lt;/&gt;) instead of a wrapping &lt;div&gt;. A fragment groups siblings without adding an extra DOM node; either satisfies JSX's single-root rule.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why the styling appears (or doesn't)</h3>\n<ul><li>styles.css in the public folder uses element selectors: header gets the yellow background (#f5ba13), negative side margin, padding and a box-shadow; header h1 gets white text in the McLaren font.</li><li>So the Header looks right immediately only because it returns a real &lt;header&gt; containing an &lt;h1&gt;. If the styles don't show, the JSX isn't using the elements the CSS targets.</li><li>footer has position: absolute, bottom: 0, width: 100% and text-align: center; footer p sets the light grey colour. Returning just a &lt;p&gt; from Footer leaves the text unstyled at the top. It must be wrapped in a &lt;footer&gt; element.</li><li>The note card uses a class selector (.note) plus descendant rules .note h1 and .note p, so the card only gets its white background, border-radius, box-shadow, padding, fixed 240px width and float: left once the wrapping div has that class.</li></ul>\n<h3>A dynamic copyright year</h3>\n<ul><li>Avoid hard-coding a year that goes stale; some developers campaign against static footer years (the site updateyourfooter.com collects snippets for many languages).</li><li>new Date() creates a Date for the current moment; .getFullYear() returns the four-digit year as a number.</li><li>Either store it first (const currentYear = new Date().getFullYear();) and insert {currentYear}, or put the whole expression inline inside JSX curly braces.</li><li>The © symbol can be pasted in directly (copy it from a search result).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Defining the year inside the component (as in the lecture) recalculates it on every render; defining it at module level (as in the reference repo) calculates it once when the file loads. Both give the right year for a normal page visit.</li></ul>\n<h3>HTML attributes belong on HTML elements</h3>\n<ul><li>Writing &lt;Note className=&quot;note&quot; /&gt; in App does nothing visible: the class never reaches the DOM.</li><li>Attributes like className, style or onClick only take effect on native elements. Put className=&quot;note&quot; on the &lt;div&gt; that Note returns.</li><li>className is JSX's name for the HTML class attribute.</li><li>Why this happens is explained in the next lecture on props: anything written on a custom component tag is passed in as a prop, not applied as an HTML attribute.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.js: render the App root (react-scripts / React 16-17 style used in the video)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ReactDOM from &quot;react-dom&quot;;\nimport App from &quot;./components/App&quot;;\n\nReactDOM.render(&lt;App /&gt;, document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;));</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">components/App.jsx: root of the component tree</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Header from &quot;./Header&quot;;\nimport Note from &quot;./Note&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./Footer&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      &lt;Note /&gt;\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">components/Header.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction Header() {\n  return (\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Keeper&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Header;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">components/Footer.jsx: year computed at render time</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction Footer() {\n  const currentYear = new Date().getFullYear();\n  return (\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Copyright © {currentYear}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Footer;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">components/Note.jsx: className goes on the div, not on &lt;Note /&gt;</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction Note() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;This is the note title&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This is the note content&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Note;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">What does NOT work</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// In App.jsx: the class is passed as a prop and never reaches the DOM\n&lt;Note className=&quot;note&quot; /&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Vite entry used in the reference repo (React 18+)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { StrictMode } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport { createRoot } from &quot;react-dom/client&quot;;\nimport App from &quot;./App.jsx&quot;;\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)).render(\n  &lt;StrictMode&gt;\n    &lt;App /&gt;\n  &lt;/StrictMode&gt;\n);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Keeper App Part 1: solution walkthrough</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The Part 1 starter: index.html with #root, Google Fonts and styles.css linked; an index.js with the challenge steps as comments.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> An App component that renders Header, Note and Footer, styled to match the sample app using the classes and element selectors in styles.css.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In index.js import React and ReactDOM and render a test &lt;h1&gt; into document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;) to confirm the project runs.</li><li>Create a components folder and add App.jsx: import React, define function App returning a div with a placeholder h1, export default App.</li><li>In index.js import App from &quot;./components/App&quot; and render &lt;App /&gt; in place of the test h1; check the placeholder text appears.</li><li>Add Header.jsx returning &lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Keeper&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;; import it into App and replace the placeholder with &lt;Header /&gt;. The yellow bar styling appears automatically.</li><li>Add Footer.jsx: compute const currentYear = new Date().getFullYear() and return a &lt;footer&gt; wrapping &lt;p&gt;Copyright © {currentYear}&lt;/p&gt;. Render &lt;Footer /&gt; under the header in App.</li><li>If the footer text sits unstyled at the top, check styles.css: the positioning rules target footer and the colour rule targets footer p, so the &lt;footer&gt; wrapper is required.</li><li>Add Note.jsx returning a &lt;div&gt; with an &lt;h1&gt; title and a &lt;p&gt; content line; render &lt;Note /&gt; between Header and Footer in App.</li><li>Read the .note rules in styles.css and add className=&quot;note&quot; to the div inside Note.jsx (not to &lt;Note /&gt; in App).</li><li>Compare against the sample app; then experiment by moving or changing elements to cement the ideas.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Yellow header with a white 'Keeper' title, a single white card with rounded corners and a soft shadow floated left beneath it, and the copyright line with the current year centred in light grey at the bottom of the page.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx (reference repo version, Vite project: App.jsx in src/, children in src/components/)\nimport Header from &quot;./components/Header&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./components/Footer&quot;;\nimport Note from &quot;./components/Note&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      &lt;Note /&gt;\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;\n\n// components/Header.jsx\nfunction Header() {\n  return (\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Keeper App&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Header;\n\n// components/Footer.jsx\nconst year = new Date().getFullYear(); // computed once when the module loads\n\nfunction Footer() {\n  return (\n    &lt;footer&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;Copyright © {year}. All rights reserved.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/footer&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Footer;\n\n// components/Note.jsx\nfunction Note() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;This is the note title&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;This is the note content&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Note;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Putting className (or style/onClick) on the custom component tag, e.g. &lt;Note className=&quot;note&quot; /&gt;. It is silently ignored; apply it to the div inside the component.</li><li>Returning a bare &lt;p&gt; from Footer: without the &lt;footer&gt; wrapper none of the footer positioning or colour rules match.</li><li>Writing the header text in a div instead of a &lt;header&gt; element, so the yellow bar never appears.</li><li>Naming a component in lowercase; React then treats it as an unknown HTML tag.</li><li>Hard-coding the year in the footer.</li><li>Mixing .js and .jsx conventions within one project.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo note: its Footer text has a small typo (&quot;All right reserved&quot;), corrected in these notes; the files in 36.16 and 36.17 are identical.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l294", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows why repeated JSX should become a reusable component and how props let each instance receive its own data. Compares props with fixed HTML attributes, then asks the learner to render a second contact card by passing different props.</p>\n<h3>The problem: repetitive JSX and a Card component with no inputs</h3>\n<ul><li>The starting contacts app hard-codes three people (h2 name, img, two p elements for phone and email) directly in the render call, which is long and repetitive. Repeated markup like this is a signal to extract a component.</li><li>Step one is to move one contact's markup into a function named Card (capitalised) that returns a wrapping div. The render call then uses &lt;Card /&gt; three times.</li><li>Result: three identical cards, all showing the first contact. A component with hard-coded content cannot be reused, because there is no way yet to give each instance its own data.</li></ul>\n<h3>Analogy: HTML attributes pass data into an element</h3>\n<ul><li>A plain &lt;input /&gt; placed in index.html (outside the root div) accepts attributes such as id, placeholder and value. placeholder shows grey hint text while empty; value pre-fills the field.</li><li>A React app does not own the whole page: the root div is just a mount point, and the rest of index.html stays ordinary HTML. This makes it easy to add React to an existing site.</li><li>In the console, document.getElementById('fName') returns the element object, and its attributes appear as properties (input.placeholder, input.value), which can be read or assigned.</li><li>HTML attributes come from a fixed, predefined list (documented on MDN). You cannot invent your own, e.g. a made-up attribute will not appear as a property on the element.</li></ul>\n<h3>Props: custom attributes for your own components</h3>\n<ul><li>Think of a component as a custom HTML element. The attributes you write on it in JSX are called properties, shortened to props, and you choose their names freely.</li><li>Syntax on the usage side: &lt;Card name=&quot;Beyonce&quot; /&gt;. Quoted strings pass text; use curly braces for anything that is a JavaScript expression (numbers, variables, objects).</li><li>React calls the component function with a single argument: an object holding every prop as a key/value pair. By convention the parameter is called props: function Card(props) { ... }.</li><li>Logging props shows an object such as { name: 'Beyonce' }, which confirms that the attributes are packaged into one object.</li><li>Inside the JSX that Card returns, read values with props.name, props.img, props.tel, props.email. These are JavaScript expressions in markup, so they must sit inside curly braces, including when used for an attribute like src={props.img}.</li><li>The names on the usage side and the property names read in the component must match exactly, and must be consistent across every &lt;Card /&gt; instance.</li><li>After this refactor Card contains no contact-specific data; it holds only structure (and any styling), while each call site supplies the content. Adding another contact is just another &lt;Card ... /&gt; with different prop values.</li></ul>\n<h3>Props on custom components are not HTML attributes</h3>\n<ul><li>className=&quot;my-style&quot; works on a built-in element like a div, turning the text red via the stylesheet rule.</li><li>Putting className on &lt;Card /&gt; does nothing to the output by itself: React treats it as just another prop called className and passes it in the props object. The component would have to read props.className and apply it to an inner element.</li><li>Rule of thumb: predefined lowercase attribute names belong to real HTML elements; on your own components any name is allowed but means nothing until the component uses it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mental model</h3>\n<ul><li>Using a component is like calling a function: the attributes become an object argument, the function uses it to fill in a template, and the returned element is placed in the DOM.</li><li>Attributes on HTML elements: fixed names, set by the browser spec. Props on components: names you invent, defined by your own function.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Card component driven by props (illustrative; the wrapping div keeps each card's elements grouped)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;img src={props.img} alt=&quot;avatar&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.tel}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.email}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Rendering two cards with different data</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;Card\n        name=&quot;Beyonce&quot;\n        img=&quot;https://blackhistorywall.files.wordpress.com/2010/02/picture-device-independent-bitmap-119.jpg&quot;\n        tel=&quot;+123 456 789&quot;\n        email=&quot;b@beyonce.com&quot;\n      /&gt;\n      &lt;Card\n        name=&quot;Jack Bauer&quot;\n        img=&quot;https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/625247595825246208/X3XLea04_400x400.jpg&quot;\n        tel=&quot;+987 654 321&quot;\n        email=&quot;jack@nowhere.com&quot;\n      /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Console comparison: element attributes as properties</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const input = document.getElementById('fName');\ninput.placeholder; // text from the placeholder attribute\ninput.value;       // current value, can be assigned too</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add a second contact card by reusing Card</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The Card component reads props.name, props.img, props.tel and props.email, and one Card is already rendered for the first contact.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Render a second Card below the first with a different name, image, phone number and email, without changing the Card function.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add a second self-closing &lt;Card /&gt; in the render/App markup, directly after the first.</li><li>Give it the same four props (name, img, tel, email) so they match what Card reads.</li><li>Fill in new values: a different name, an image URL, a phone number and an email.</li><li>Save and check that two cards appear with different details.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Optionally add a third Card (Chuck Norris in the reference data) to confirm the component scales without edits.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Two contact cards stacked vertically, each showing its own name, picture, phone and email, produced from one Card definition.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h2&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n      &lt;img src={props.img} alt=&quot;avatar&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.tel}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.email}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;Card name=&quot;Beyonce&quot; img=&quot;https://blackhistorywall.files.wordpress.com/2010/02/picture-device-independent-bitmap-119.jpg&quot; tel=&quot;+123 456 789&quot; email=&quot;b@beyonce.com&quot; /&gt;\n      {/* the new card: same four prop names, new values */}\n      &lt;Card name=&quot;Jack Bauer&quot; img=&quot;https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/625247595825246208/X3XLea04_400x400.jpg&quot; tel=&quot;+987 654 321&quot; email=&quot;jack@nowhere.com&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting curly braces around props.x inside JSX, so the literal text is shown instead of the value.</li><li>Prop names that differ between the call site and the component (img versus imgSrc) produce undefined and blank output.</li><li>Expecting className or other HTML attributes placed on a custom component to style it automatically.</li><li>Using a lowercase component name (&lt;card /&gt;); React treats it as an HTML tag.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Props are read-only; a component should not assign to props.x.</li><li>Reference repo quirk: the student's Card uses different prop names (imgSrc, mobNum, eMail), returns a fragment instead of a div, and stores the contacts in an object keyed 1-3 rendered with Object.values(Data).map. The names are a free choice as long as both sides match; the mapping is a later lesson.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l295", "<p class=\"summary\">A four-step practice project that turns a hard-coded contact card into a styled, reusable Card component driven by props, then fills three cards from a contacts data file. The solution walkthrough finishes with the observation that indexing the array by hand is tedious and that map will replace it later.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: apply the provided CSS classes</h3>\n<ul><li>styles.css already defines the needed classes; the job is to read it and attach the right ones in JSX using className (not class).</li><li>name class: goes on the h2 with the contact name; sets a larger font size, a colour and margin so it no longer touches the card edge.</li><li>circle-img class: goes on the img; gives it a round shape (border-radius 50%) plus a border. A second rule targets any img inside .card and is already applied automatically because the img sits inside the card div; this is why the image already had some positioning before the class was added.</li><li>info class: goes on both p elements (phone and email); changes colour and adds a small margin.</li><li>Comparing the running app against the finished demo and checking which CSS selector produces which effect is the recommended way to discover which class belongs where.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> How the rest of styles.css builds the card: .card is a rounded, shadowed teal box with position: relative and a fixed 200px height; .top is an absolutely positioned darker strip (top: 0, left: 0, height 100px, display: flex) holding the name and picture side by side; .name has flex: 1 so it fills the space left of the image; .bottom has margin-top: 120px to push the phone and email below the strip; .heading centres and enlarges the page title; body is fixed at 400px wide and centred with margin: 0 auto.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: extract a reusable Card component</h3>\n<ul><li>Everything inside the div with class card is the part that repeats. The outer wrapper div and the page h1 heading stay in App.</li><li>Create a new file inside a components folder (card.jsx in the lecture). In it: define a function component, return the copied card markup, and export it as the default export.</li><li>In App, import it by relative path (import Card from './components/Card' style), then use it as &lt;Card /&gt; with PascalCase and a self-closing tag.</li><li>Result after this step: the single Beyonce card renders exactly as before, but now through a component.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Name the file Card.jsx so its casing matches the import path ./components/Card; a lowercase card.jsx imported as Card works on macOS/Windows but fails on case-sensitive Linux builds.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: replace hard-coded values with props</h3>\n<ul><li>Look through the card markup for anything specific to a person: name, image URL, phone and email. Each becomes a prop on the &lt;Card /&gt; element, written like an HTML attribute (key=&quot;value&quot;), even though it ends up as an object.</li><li>Delete the hard-coded text from the component and read the values from the function argument instead. The parameter can have any name but props is the universal convention.</li><li>Mapping used: {props.name} in the h2, src={props.img} on the image, {props.tel} and {props.email} in the two info paragraphs. Curly braces are required so JSX evaluates them as JavaScript instead of printing text like props.name.</li><li>If a prop name is forgotten, look back at where the Card is used in App.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: use contacts.js as the data source</h3>\n<ul><li>contacts.js holds an array of objects, each with name, imgURL, phone and email keys, matching what a card needs. Own data can replace it.</li><li>Export the array so other files can import it, then import it in App (a named export with braces, or a default export without; the file in the repo uses a named export). Logging it shows an array of three objects.</li><li>Create one Card per contact and feed values with indexes: name={contacts[0].name}, img={contacts[0].imgURL} and so on, changing the index to 1 and 2 for the next two cards. The expression is JavaScript so it sits in curly braces.</li><li>This works but is repetitive. The upcoming lessons on map, filter and reduce show how to generate the cards from the array automatically.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Card component after steps 1 to 3 (cleaned from the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;top&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;h2 className=&quot;name&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;img className=&quot;circle-img&quot; src={props.img} alt=&quot;avatar&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;bottom&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;p className=&quot;info&quot;&gt;{props.tel}&lt;/p&gt;\n        &lt;p className=&quot;info&quot;&gt;{props.email}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Card;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Step 4 in App using index access (illustrative)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Card from &quot;./components/Card&quot;;\nimport { contacts } from &quot;./contacts&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[0].name} img={contacts[0].imgURL} tel={contacts[0].phone} email={contacts[0].email} /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[1].name} img={contacts[1].imgURL} tel={contacts[1].phone} email={contacts[1].email} /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[2].name} img={contacts[2].imgURL} tel={contacts[2].phone} email={contacts[2].email} /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">contacts.js (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const contacts = [\n  {\n    name: &quot;Beyonce&quot;,\n    imgURL: &quot;https://blackhistorywall.files.wordpress.com/2010/02/picture-device-independent-bitmap-119.jpg&quot;,\n    phone: &quot;+123 456 789&quot;,\n    email: &quot;b@beyonce.com&quot;\n  },\n  {\n    name: &quot;Jack Bauer&quot;,\n    imgURL: &quot;https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/625247595825246208/X3XLea04_400x400.jpg&quot;,\n    phone: &quot;+987 654 321&quot;,\n    email: &quot;jack@nowhere.com&quot;\n  },\n  {\n    name: &quot;Chuck Norris&quot;,\n    imgURL: &quot;https://i.pinimg.com/originals/e3/94/47/e39447de921955826b1e498ccf9a39af.png&quot;,\n    phone: &quot;+918 372 574&quot;,\n    email: &quot;gmail@chucknorris.com&quot;\n  }\n];\n\nexport { contacts };</pre>\n<h2>The task: Contact list app with props</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A CodeSandbox/Vite project where App.jsx holds one hard-coded, unstyled Beyonce card, plus styles.css and a contacts.js data file with three contacts.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Match the finished contact list: a heading and three styled, circular-avatar cards, all produced by one reusable Card component fed through props.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add className values in App.jsx so the layout matches the demo: name on the h2, circle-img on the img, info on both p elements.</li><li>Extract the card div into its own Card component file in a components folder, export it by default and import it into App.</li><li>Turn the name, image, phone and email into props on &lt;Card /&gt;, and read them via props.* inside the component.</li><li>Export the contacts array from contacts.js, import it in App and create three Cards using contacts[0], contacts[1] and contacts[2].</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Three cards, one per contact, each with name, round picture, phone and email, matching the finished version, with no hard-coded contact data left inside Card.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// components/Card.jsx\nfunction Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;top&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;h2 className=&quot;name&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;img className=&quot;circle-img&quot; src={props.img} alt=&quot;avatar&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;bottom&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;p className=&quot;info&quot;&gt;{props.tel}&lt;/p&gt;\n        &lt;p className=&quot;info&quot;&gt;{props.email}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Card;\n\n// App.jsx (lecture approach: one Card per array index)\nimport Card from &quot;./components/Card&quot;;\nimport { contacts } from &quot;./contacts&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[0].name} img={contacts[0].imgURL} tel={contacts[0].phone} email={contacts[0].email} /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[1].name} img={contacts[1].imgURL} tel={contacts[1].phone} email={contacts[1].email} /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[2].name} img={contacts[2].imgURL} tel={contacts[2].phone} email={contacts[2].email} /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;\n\n// (Extra) Preview of the later mapping lesson, which replaces the three lines above:\n// {contacts.map((c, i) =&gt; (\n//   &lt;Card key={i} name={c.name} img={c.imgURL} tel={c.phone} email={c.email} /&gt;\n// ))}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Using class instead of className in JSX.</li><li>Mismatch between exporting and importing (default export imported with braces, or named export imported without).</li><li>Wrong relative path in the import (./ versus ../) when the component or data file sits in a different folder.</li><li>Forgetting curly braces around props.* or contacts[0].* expressions.</li><li>Prop name differences between App and Card (img versus imgURL) leave fields blank.</li><li>Reference repo quirk: its ContactCard also contains the page h1 heading, so the heading repeats above every card, unlike the lecture where the heading stays in App. It names the props imgURL, phone and email, and loops with Object.values(contacts).map; Object.values is redundant on an array (contacts.map does the same), and mapping belongs to a later lesson, not this one.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l296", "<p class=\"summary\">Splits the contact card into smaller components (Avatar, then Detail) and introduces the React Developer Tools for viewing the component tree and the props each component receives. Props that fail to arrive are diagnosed by inspecting them in DevTools.</p>\n<h3>Why split into smaller components</h3>\n<ul><li>Parts of a card that could appear elsewhere (a circular, bordered picture) are good candidates for their own component. Any element or group you find yourself repeating with the same classes is a candidate too.</li><li>Thinking about the component tree up front pays off: small specialised components form a palette that can be reused and customised through props.</li></ul>\n<h3>Extracting Avatar and passing props down two levels</h3>\n<ul><li>Create Avatar.jsx with the usual boilerplate (function, return, default export) and move the img element into it. Import Avatar in the Card file and use &lt;Avatar /&gt; where the image was.</li><li>The app breaks at first: the moved img still reads a prop, but Avatar does not declare a props parameter and nothing is passed to it.</li><li>The data travels App to Card to Avatar. App passes the contact's image URL to Card as a prop; Card must then pass it again to Avatar as a prop; Avatar receives it through its own props parameter and uses it as src.</li><li>Each component only sees what its direct parent hands it. Prop names can differ between levels, but keep them consistent to avoid confusion.</li><li>With many props and layers, tracing names by hand gets confusing, which is where DevTools helps.</li></ul>\n<h3>React Developer Tools</h3>\n<ul><li>CodeSandbox has a built-in React DevTools panel, but it can be unreliable. The browser extension (Chrome Web Store, by Facebook/Meta; also available as a Firefox add-on) is preferred and is also needed for local development.</li><li>After installing, refresh the page. The extension icon colours itself to signal the build type: one colour for a development build, another for a production build. (Extra) Currently red/orange means a development build and blue means production; grey means no React was detected.</li><li>Open the normal browser DevTools; two extra tabs appear ((Extra) named Components and Profiler), with Components being the relevant one. It shows the React tree (App at the top, Cards under it, an Avatar inside each) rather than raw HTML.</li><li>Selecting a component shows its props with their values and which component rendered it, so you can read the exact prop names to use with props.something.</li><li>A picker (the component-select tool inside the Components tab, separate from the elements picker) lets you click on the page and jump to the matching component in the tree.</li><li>It also works on any React site, not just your own. On a production site with minified code, component names may be short and meaningless, but props such as image sources and alt text are still visible.</li><li>By default native elements (div, h2, p) are filtered out of the tree. A settings gear in the Components tab has a filter option to show them, but leaving it on keeps the tree tidy.</li><li>A source button opens the file that defines the selected component. Prop values can be edited live in the panel (for example pasting a different image URL) to experiment without changing code.</li></ul>\n<h3>Debugging with the tree</h3>\n<ul><li>If Avatar reads props.imgURL while the parent actually passes img, no picture appears. Selecting the component in DevTools reveals the real prop name (img), so the code is fixed to match.</li><li>General pattern: blank output means check the received props first, then compare names against what the code reads.</li></ul>\n<h3>Detail component (the challenge)</h3>\n<ul><li>The two p elements with class info are the same markup holding different text, so they become one Detail component rendered twice.</li><li>Detail takes one prop, named detailInfo in the lecture, and renders a single p with the info class showing it. In Card, &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.tel} /&gt; and &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.email} /&gt; replace the paragraphs.</li><li>After first extracting it, nothing appeared because the copied code still read props.tel; DevTools showed the prop was really detailInfo. Changing the component to props.detailInfo fixed it. Lesson: when pasting markup into a new component, rename the prop reads to match the new component's own props.</li></ul>\n<h3>Reusing a component elsewhere</h3>\n<ul><li>Because Avatar is self-contained, it can be dropped anywhere. Importing it in App and placing &lt;Avatar img=&quot;...&quot; /&gt; under the main heading adds a standalone picture at the top of the tree, styled the same, with only the required prop to supply.</li><li>The lesson closes by pointing out that hand-indexing contacts[0], contacts[1] is ugly and that the next lesson replaces it with mapping.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Avatar.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Avatar(props) {\n  return &lt;img className=&quot;circle-img&quot; src={props.img} alt=&quot;avatar&quot; /&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default Avatar;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Detail.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Detail(props) {\n  return &lt;p className=&quot;info&quot;&gt;{props.detailInfo}&lt;/p&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default Detail;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Card using both child components (reference repo structure, cleaned)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Avatar from &quot;./Avatar&quot;;\nimport Detail from &quot;./Detail&quot;;\n\nfunction Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;top&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;h2 className=&quot;name&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;Avatar img={props.img} /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;bottom&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.tel} /&gt;\n        &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.email} /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Card;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Avatar reused directly in App (excerpt of App's return)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Avatar from &quot;./components/Avatar&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;Avatar img=&quot;https://example.com/me.jpg&quot; /&gt;\n      {/* ...the Card elements follow here */}\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Extract Avatar, then build a Detail component</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The finished props-practice project: a Card with the circular img and two info paragraphs inline.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Split Card into Avatar and Detail child components, pass the correct props down, and use React DevTools to verify the props each component receives.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create Avatar.jsx, move the img into it, import it in Card and add a props parameter.</li><li>Pass the image URL from Card to Avatar as a prop and read it inside Avatar with the same name.</li><li>Install React Developer Tools, reload, open the Components tab and inspect the tree and each component's props.</li><li>Create Detail.jsx returning one p with class info and showing a single prop (detailInfo).</li><li>Replace the two paragraphs in Card with two Detail components, passing the phone and the email.</li><li>If nothing renders, select Detail in DevTools, read the prop name, and fix the mismatch in the component.</li><li>Optional: import Avatar into App and render it under the main heading with your own image URL.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The page looks identical to before the refactor, but the DevTools tree shows App, Card, Avatar and Detail components with the expected props.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// components/Avatar.jsx\nfunction Avatar(props) {\n  return &lt;img className=&quot;circle-img&quot; src={props.img} alt=&quot;avatar&quot; /&gt;;\n}\nexport default Avatar;\n\n// components/Detail.jsx\nfunction Detail(props) {\n  return &lt;p className=&quot;info&quot;&gt;{props.detailInfo}&lt;/p&gt;;\n}\nexport default Detail;\n\n// components/Card.jsx: receives props from App and hands them on\nimport Avatar from &quot;./Avatar&quot;;\nimport Detail from &quot;./Detail&quot;;\n\nfunction Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;top&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;h2 className=&quot;name&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;Avatar img={props.img} /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;bottom&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.tel} /&gt;\n        &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.email} /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Card;\n\n// App.jsx (still indexing the array by hand at this point)\nimport Card from &quot;./components/Card&quot;;\nimport Avatar from &quot;./components/Avatar&quot;;\nimport { contacts } from &quot;./contacts&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;Avatar img=&quot;https://example.com/me.jpg&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[0].name} img={contacts[0].imgURL} tel={contacts[0].phone} email={contacts[0].email} /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[1].name} img={contacts[1].imgURL} tel={contacts[1].phone} email={contacts[1].email} /&gt;\n      &lt;Card name={contacts[2].name} img={contacts[2].imgURL} tel={contacts[2].phone} email={contacts[2].email} /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Moving markup into a new component without adding the props parameter or passing the props from the parent.</li><li>Prop name mismatches (img vs imgURL, tel vs detailInfo) cause empty output, with no error.</li><li>Forgetting to refresh the page after installing the extension, so the Components tab stays empty.</li><li>Not importing the new component into its parent.</li><li>Reference repo quirk: its App renders &lt;Avatar imgURL=&quot;&quot; /&gt; with an empty URL as a placeholder demo (this shows a broken image, and React warns about an empty src; supply a real URL), uses Object.values(...).map with imgURL, phone and email props, and its Avatar reads props.imgURL rather than props.img, so naming differs from the lecture.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Component names in minified production builds appear as short letters, so DevTools is more readable on development builds.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l297", "<p class=\"summary\">Replaces three hand-written Card components with one Array.prototype.map() call that turns each object in contacts.js into a &lt;Card/&gt;. Introduces React's required, unique key prop for list items, and a mini debugging challenge showing that key is never passed through to props.</p>\n<h3>Why map components</h3>\n<ul><li>Hard-coding &lt;Card name={contacts[0].name} .../&gt;, &lt;Card name={contacts[1].name} .../&gt; and so on is repetitive and only works for a fixed number of items.</li><li>Mapping generates one component per array element, so the UI follows the data automatically: add an object to the array (e.g. later from a form) and a new card appears with no JSX changes.</li><li>contacts.js holds an array of objects that all share the same shape (id, name, imgURL, phone, email) but with different values. That uniform shape is what makes a single template function possible.</li></ul>\n<h3>Calling map() inside JSX</h3>\n<ul><li>Comment out the three hard-coded cards in App.jsx and write contacts.map(...) in their place, inside the wrapper div.</li><li>Inside JSX children, bare text is rendered literally, so contacts.map() must be wrapped in curly braces: {contacts.map(createCard)}. The braces switch JSX into JavaScript-expression mode.</li><li>map() takes a function as its argument (a callback). Passing a function into a function is a core idea of functional programming, and it can be nested many levels deep.</li><li>Pass the function by name with no parentheses: contacts.map(createCard). Writing createCard() would call it once immediately instead of handing it to map.</li><li>map() loops over the array, calls the callback once per element with that single element (one contact object, not the whole array) as its argument, and collects every return value into a new array.</li><li>React can render an array of elements directly, so the array of &lt;Card/&gt; elements returned by map() appears as a list of cards.</li></ul>\n<h3>The createCard callback</h3>\n<ul><li>Define function createCard(contact) above App. Its parameter is one contact object on each call.</li><li>It returns a &lt;Card/&gt; whose props are filled from that object: name={contact.name}, img={contact.imgURL}, tel={contact.phone}, email={contact.email}.</li><li>The prop names (name, img, tel, email) must match what the Card component reads from props; the object property names (imgURL, phone) come from contacts.js. The two sets of names do not have to be the same.</li><li>React DevTools (Components tab) confirms each Card now receives the expected props.</li></ul>\n<h3>The key prop</h3>\n<ul><li>After mapping, the console warns that each child in a list needs a unique key. Refreshing does not clear it, so it is a genuine React warning rather than a sandbox glitch.</li><li>React keeps a virtual DOM of the page; when components are produced in a loop it needs a stable identity for each one so it can update, reorder and re-render list items efficiently.</li><li>Fix: add key={contact.id} to the returned &lt;Card/&gt;. The ids in contacts.js (1, 2, 3) are unique, so they make good keys.</li><li>Rules: the prop must be spelled exactly key (lowercase); its value may be a string or a number; it must be unique among the sibling components generated by that loop.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Keys only need to be unique among siblings, not across the whole app. Using the array index as a key is a fallback that causes bugs when items are reordered, inserted or deleted, so prefer a real id from the data.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> During re-rendering React compares the old and new list element by element using the keys: a matching key means 'same item, update it', a new key means 'create', a missing key means 'remove'. This is why keys must be stable and unique.</li></ul>\n<h3>Debugging challenge: key is not readable inside the component</h3>\n<ul><li>Challenge: render each card's key (its id) in a &lt;p&gt; placed above the &lt;h2&gt; inside Card.</li><li>Trying {props.key} renders nothing and triggers a console warning saying key is not a prop and reading it gives undefined.</li><li>React DevTools shows why: the Card's props list contains name, img, tel and email but no key. React consumes key internally to order and track the list and strips it from props.</li><li>Solution: pass the same value again under a normal prop name, e.g. id={contact.id}, alongside key={contact.id}, then render {props.id} inside Card. It looks duplicated, but key is reserved for React and id is for your own use.</li><li>Takeaway: get used to React DevTools and console warnings for debugging; bugs are normal, and the tools show what props a component actually received.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">contacts.js data shape (each object needs a unique id for the key)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const contacts = [\n  { id: 1, name: &quot;Beyonce&quot;, imgURL: &quot;https://...&quot;, phone: &quot;+123 456 789&quot;, email: &quot;b@beyonce.com&quot; },\n  { id: 2, name: &quot;Jack Bauer&quot;, imgURL: &quot;https://...&quot;, phone: &quot;+987 654 321&quot;, email: &quot;jack@nowhere.com&quot; },\n  { id: 3, name: &quot;Chuck Norris&quot;, imgURL: &quot;https://...&quot;, phone: &quot;+918 372 574&quot;, email: &quot;gmail@chucknorris.com&quot; }\n];\n\nexport default contacts;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Before: repetitive hard-coded cards in App.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">&lt;Card name={contacts[0].name} img={contacts[0].imgURL} tel={contacts[0].phone} email={contacts[0].email} /&gt;\n&lt;Card name={contacts[1].name} img={contacts[1].imgURL} tel={contacts[1].phone} email={contacts[1].email} /&gt;\n&lt;Card name={contacts[2].name} img={contacts[2].imgURL} tel={contacts[2].phone} email={contacts[2].email} /&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">After: App.jsx maps every contact to a Card (sandbox layout: App.jsx and Card.jsx in src/components, contacts.js in src)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Card from &quot;./Card&quot;;\nimport contacts from &quot;../contacts&quot;;\n\nfunction createCard(contact) {\n  return (\n    &lt;Card\n      key={contact.id}   // for React only, not visible in props\n      id={contact.id}    // a normal prop the component can read\n      name={contact.name}\n      img={contact.imgURL}\n      tel={contact.phone}\n      email={contact.email}\n    /&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1 className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;My Contacts&lt;/h1&gt;\n      {contacts.map(createCard)}\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Card.jsx reading the id prop (props.key would be undefined)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Avatar from &quot;./Avatar&quot;;\nimport Detail from &quot;./Detail&quot;;\n\nfunction Card(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;card&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;top&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;p&gt;{props.id}&lt;/p&gt;\n        &lt;h2 className=&quot;name&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n        &lt;Avatar img={props.img} /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;bottom&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.tel} /&gt;\n        &lt;Detail detailInfo={props.email} /&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Card;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Render the key/id inside each Card (debugging mini-challenge)</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The contacts app from the React Props / DevTools lessons, with App.jsx now rendering cards via contacts.map(createCard) and key={contact.id} on each Card.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show each contact's id (1, 2, 3) in a paragraph above the name heading on every card.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add &lt;p&gt;{props.key}&lt;/p&gt; above the &lt;h2&gt; in Card and notice that nothing renders and the console warns that key is not a prop.</li><li>Open React DevTools, select a Card, and confirm key is missing from its props.</li><li>In createCard, add a second prop id={contact.id} next to key={contact.id}.</li><li>Change the paragraph in Card to &lt;p&gt;{props.id}&lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>Check that each card shows its number and the console has no key warnings.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Three cards, each with its id in a small paragraph above the name, and no warnings in the console.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx\nfunction createCard(contact) {\n  return (\n    &lt;Card\n      key={contact.id}\n      id={contact.id}\n      name={contact.name}\n      img={contact.imgURL}\n      tel={contact.phone}\n      email={contact.email}\n    /&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n// Card.jsx (top section)\n&lt;div className=&quot;top&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{props.id}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;h2 className=&quot;name&quot;&gt;{props.name}&lt;/h2&gt;\n  &lt;Avatar img={props.img} /&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing contacts.map(createCard) without curly braces inside JSX, which prints it as text.</li><li>Calling the callback (map(createCard())) instead of passing it.</li><li>Forgetting key, or using a value that repeats across items, which keeps the 'unique key' warning.</li><li>Misspelling key (Key, keys) so React does not recognise it.</li><li>Expecting props.key to work inside the child component; it is always undefined.</li><li>Mixing up prop names and data property names (img vs imgURL, tel vs phone).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo divergence: the student's contacts.js has no id fields, so key={contact.id} and id={contact.id} are undefined there and the key warning would remain; add id: 1, 2, 3 to each object. The repo also renames Card to ContactCard, uses props imgURL/phone instead of img/tel, uses a named export (export {contacts} with import { contacts } from &quot;./contacts.js&quot;) instead of a default export, and keeps App.jsx in src (so the path is ./contacts.js rather than ../contacts).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l298", "<p class=\"summary\">Practice challenge: build an 'emojipedia' card dictionary by extracting a repeated block of HTML into an Entry component, replacing the hard-coded text with props, and rendering one Entry per object in emojipedia.js with map(). The solution half shows how to break the problem into numbered steps and work through them.</p>\n<h3>Starting project</h3>\n<ul><li>Fork the CodeSandbox from the course resources (or use the local Vite version). public/ already has index.html with the Montserrat Google Font link, styles.css and the root div; index.js renders &lt;App/&gt;. Nothing in public needs changing.</li><li>App.jsx contains a long block of static HTML: an h1 with a span reading 'emojipedia', then a &lt;dl className=&quot;dictionary&quot;&gt; holding three near-identical &lt;div className=&quot;term&quot;&gt; blocks (Tense Biceps, Person With Folded Hands, Rolling On The Floor Laughing).</li><li>emojipedia.js holds a const array of three objects, each with id, emoji, name and meaning. It is not exported yet.</li><li>The supplied styles.css produces the finished card look, but the styling is optional; the point is the mapping.</li></ul>\n<h3>Description list elements: dl, dt, dd</h3>\n<ul><li>&lt;dl&gt; is a description list: a container for groups of terms and their descriptions, such as a glossary or dictionary.</li><li>&lt;dt&gt; is the term being described; &lt;dd&gt; is the description or detail for it. A dt/dd pair works rather like a key-value pair.</li><li>Grouping a dt and its dd in a &lt;div&gt; is allowed inside a dl, which is what the starter does with div.term so each pair can be styled as a card.</li><li>The emoji is in &lt;span className=&quot;emoji&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;...&quot;&gt; so screen readers announce a label instead of the raw character.</li></ul>\n<h3>Planning: split a big challenge into small steps</h3>\n<ul><li>The challenge comes without step comments, so write your own: break the end result into small tasks you can tick off.</li><li>Step 1: extract the repeated part (each div.term) into its own Entry component.</li><li>Step 2: find what differs between the repeats (emoji, name, meaning) and replace each with a prop.</li><li>Step 3a: export the emojipedia array and import it into App.</li><li>Step 3b: map over the array and render an Entry per object, with a key.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1: extracting Entry</h3>\n<ul><li>Create Entry.jsx with the usual component boilerplate (import React, function Entry, export default Entry) and import Entry into App.jsx.</li><li>Cut one whole &lt;div className=&quot;term&quot;&gt; out of App and return it from Entry; delete the other two copies but keep the surrounding &lt;dl className=&quot;dictionary&quot;&gt; in App.</li><li>Temporarily place &lt;Entry /&gt; three times inside the dl. The page shows three identical cards, which proves the extraction worked.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: props for the parts that change</h3>\n<ul><li>Choose clear prop names. The solution uses emoji, name and description.</li><li>In Entry, accept props and swap the hard-coded values for {props.emoji}, {props.name} and {props.description}, each in curly braces.</li><li>Test by passing hard-coded strings (copied from emojipedia.js) to the first &lt;Entry/&gt; only. That card fills in while the other two render empty, because no props reach them.</li><li>Writing out props for every Entry by hand would work but is exactly the repetition map() removes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: import the data and map it</h3>\n<ul><li>A value can only be imported if its file exports it: add export default emojipedia; at the bottom of emojipedia.js.</li><li>In App.jsx (inside the components folder in the sandbox) write import emojipedia from &quot;../emojipedia&quot;; the ../ steps up from components into src.</li><li>console.log(emojipedia) is a quick check that the array of three objects (id, emoji, name, meaning) arrived.</li><li>Delete the placeholder Entry elements and write {emojipedia.map(createEntry)} inside the dl, wrapped in curly braces so it runs as JavaScript.</li><li>createEntry(emojiTerm) receives one object per call and returns &lt;Entry key={emojiTerm.id} emoji={emojiTerm.emoji} name={emojiTerm.name} description={emojiTerm.meaning} /&gt;.</li><li>The key prop is required whenever components are produced by looping over an array; id is unique, so it is used.</li><li>Pasting one sample object as a comment next to createEntry is a handy reference while matching data fields to prop names.</li><li>Result: as many cards as objects in the array. Adding a new object (e.g. another emoji and its meaning) to emojipedia.js makes a new card appear with no extra JSX.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> map() is used here instead of forEach because map returns the new array of &lt;Entry/&gt; elements, which JSX can render directly; forEach returns undefined, so nothing would appear.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Starter markup for one card (repeated three times in the original App.jsx)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">&lt;dl className=&quot;dictionary&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;div className=&quot;term&quot;&gt;\n    &lt;dt&gt;\n      &lt;span className=&quot;emoji&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;Tense Biceps&quot;&gt;\n        💪\n      &lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;span&gt;Tense Biceps&lt;/span&gt;\n    &lt;/dt&gt;\n    &lt;dd&gt;\n      “You can do that!” or “I feel strong!” Arm with tense biceps. ...\n    &lt;/dd&gt;\n  &lt;/div&gt;\n  {/* ...two more div.term blocks... */}\n&lt;/dl&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">emojipedia.js: the data array, now exported</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const emojipedia = [\n  { id: 1, emoji: &quot;💪&quot;, name: &quot;Tense Biceps&quot;, meaning: &quot;...&quot; },\n  { id: 2, emoji: &quot;🙏&quot;, name: &quot;Person With Folded Hands&quot;, meaning: &quot;...&quot; },\n  { id: 3, emoji: &quot;🤣&quot;, name: &quot;Rolling On The Floor, Laughing&quot;, meaning: &quot;...&quot; }\n];\n\nexport default emojipedia;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Entry.jsx: the extracted, prop-driven card ((Extra) aria-label={props.name} replaces the starter's hard-coded 'Tense Biceps' label)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction Entry(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;term&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;dt&gt;\n        &lt;span className=&quot;emoji&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label={props.name}&gt;\n          {props.emoji}\n        &lt;/span&gt;\n        &lt;span&gt;{props.name}&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;/dt&gt;\n      &lt;dd&gt;{props.description}&lt;/dd&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Entry;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Emojipedia: map data to Entry components</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Forked sandbox with App.jsx holding three hard-coded div.term cards inside a dl, emojipedia.js with an unexported array of {id, emoji, name, meaning}, and finished styles.css/index.html.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Render the dictionary entirely from emojipedia.js so that adding an object to the array adds a card automatically.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Write your own step list before coding (extract, add props, import, map).</li><li>Create Entry.jsx, move one div.term into it and return it; import Entry into App.</li><li>Remove the other hard-coded cards, keeping the dl wrapper in App.</li><li>Replace the emoji, name and meaning text in Entry with {props.emoji}, {props.name} and {props.description}.</li><li>Add export default emojipedia; to emojipedia.js and import it in App with the correct relative path.</li><li>Write createEntry(emojiTerm) returning an Entry with key, emoji, name and description (= emojiTerm.meaning).</li><li>Inside the dl, render {emojipedia.map(createEntry)}.</li><li>Add a fourth emoji object to the array to confirm a new card appears with no JSX changes.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Styled 'emojipedia' heading followed by one white card per array item (large emoji, teal name, description), with no key warning in the console.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Entry from &quot;./Entry&quot;;\nimport emojipedia from &quot;../emojipedia&quot;;\n\n// Called once per object in the emojipedia array\nfunction createEntry(emojiTerm) {\n  return (\n    &lt;Entry\n      key={emojiTerm.id}             // unique id for React's list tracking\n      emoji={emojiTerm.emoji}\n      name={emojiTerm.name}\n      description={emojiTerm.meaning} // prop name differs from data field\n    /&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;\n        &lt;span&gt;emojipedia&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;dl className=&quot;dictionary&quot;&gt;{emojipedia.map(createEntry)}&lt;/dl&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to export emojipedia before importing it (the import is undefined).</li><li>Wrong relative path: App.jsx sits in components, so the data file is ../emojipedia.</li><li>Leaving out the curly braces around emojipedia.map(...) in JSX.</li><li>Using a data field name as a prop name by mistake: the prop is description but the object field is meaning.</li><li>Moving the dl into Entry, which creates a separate description list per card instead of one list of terms.</li><li>Leaving aria-label hard-coded as 'Tense Biceps' for every card. (Extra) Use aria-label={props.name} so each emoji has the right accessible label.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo divergence: the student's App.jsx maps the array twice (once with an arrow function block and once with a concise arrow), so every card appears twice; delete one. It also uses key={index} instead of the id, passes the prop as meaning, and puts a &lt;dl&gt; inside each Entry. The lecture's version uses a named createEntry function, key={emojiTerm.id}, the description prop and a single dl in App.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l299", "<p class=\"summary\">Walks through the array methods map, filter, reduce, find and findIndex by comparing each with the equivalent forEach loop. Ends with a challenge: use map to turn the emojipedia array into an array of meaning strings capped at 100 characters.</p>\n<h3>map: transform every item into a new array</h3>\n<ul><li>map is called on an array and takes a callback. The callback receives each item in turn and whatever it returns becomes the item at the same position in a brand-new array.</li><li>The new array always has the same length as the original, and the original is left unchanged. The result must be captured in a variable (e.g. newNumbers) or it is lost.</li><li>The callback can be a named function passed by name (numbers.map(double)) or an anonymous function written inline. Both behave the same.</li><li>Compared with forEach: forEach returns nothing, so you must create an empty array first and push into it. map builds and returns the array itself, which is why it is shorter.</li><li>This is the method used in React to turn an array of data into an array of components.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The callback actually receives up to three arguments: the item, its index and the whole array, e.g. numbers.map((x, i) =&gt; x * i). The index is what people sometimes (riskily) use as a React key.</li></ul>\n<h3>filter: keep only items that pass a test</h3>\n<ul><li>filter also takes a callback; the callback returns true or false for each item. Items that return true are copied into the new array, others are dropped.</li><li>The result can be shorter than the input (or empty), and items keep their original order and values.</li><li>Example condition: return num &gt; 10 keeps only the larger numbers; flipping to num &lt; 10 keeps the small ones.</li><li>forEach equivalent: declare an empty array, then inside the loop use an if and push the items that match.</li></ul>\n<h3>reduce: collapse an array into one accumulated value</h3>\n<ul><li>The reduce callback receives two main parameters: the accumulator (the running result) and the current item. Whatever the callback returns becomes the accumulator for the next iteration.</li><li>Summing numbers: return accumulator + currentNumber. The result is a single value (a number here), not an array.</li><li>With no initial value supplied, the accumulator starts as the first array item and the first iteration's current item is the second one. Logging both inside the callback shows the running total growing each pass.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A second argument to reduce sets an explicit starting accumulator, e.g. numbers.reduce(fn, 0). It is safer for empty arrays, which otherwise throw an error.</li><li>forEach equivalent: a variable initialised to 0 outside the loop, with each pass adding the current item to it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> With the sample array [3, 56, 2, 48, 5] the trace is: 3+56=59, 59+2=61, 61+48=109, 109+5=114. Four callback calls for five items because the first item seeds the accumulator.</li></ul>\n<h3>find and findIndex: locate the first match</h3>\n<ul><li>find returns the first item for which the callback returns true. Unlike filter it stops looping as soon as a match is found, and it returns the item itself, not an array.</li><li>findIndex works the same but returns the position (zero-based) of the first match. With the sample array and the test num &gt; 10 it gives index 1.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If nothing matches, find returns undefined and findIndex returns -1.</li><li>Use MDN for the details and the browser-compatibility table of each method. (Extra) map, filter and reduce date from ES5 and run everywhere; find and findIndex were added in ES6 (ES2015), which only matters for very old browsers such as Internet Explorer.</li></ul>\n<h3>How to learn these</h3>\n<ul><li>Do not memorise every method. Understand what each is for (transform, select, accumulate, locate one, locate position) so you recognise when one fits, then look up the details in MDN.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge solution: truncated meanings with map</h3>\n<ul><li>Import the emojipedia array from ./emojipedia. It is an array of objects with id, emoji, name and meaning.</li><li>Because the goal is a new array with one output per entry, map is the right tool. The callback receives each emoji entry object and works on entry.meaning.</li><li>String.prototype.substring(start, end) returns the characters from start up to but not including end, so substring(0, 100) keeps the first 100 characters.</li><li>The callback must return the cut string. Forgetting the return (or using braces on an arrow function without return) gives an array of undefined values.</li><li>Truncating text like this keeps cards or boxes a uniform size in a layout.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">map vs forEach for doubling</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numbers = [3, 56, 2, 48, 5];\n\n// map builds the new array for you\nconst doubled = numbers.map(function (x) {\n  return x * 2;\n});\n\n// forEach needs a manual array and push\nconst doubled2 = [];\nnumbers.forEach(function (x) {\n  doubled2.push(x * 2);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">filter, reduce, find, findIndex</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const small = numbers.filter(function (num) {\n  return num &lt; 10;\n}); // [3, 2, 5]\n\nconst total = numbers.reduce(function (accumulator, currentNumber) {\n  return accumulator + currentNumber;\n}); // 114\n\nconst firstBig = numbers.find(function (num) {\n  return num &gt; 10;\n}); // 56\n\nconst firstBigIndex = numbers.findIndex(function (num) {\n  return num &gt; 10;\n}); // 1</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">reduce vs forEach for a total, with a trace and an explicit start value</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numbers = [3, 56, 2, 48, 5];\n\n// forEach: keep a running total yourself\nlet total = 0;\nnumbers.forEach(function (currentNumber) {\n  total += currentNumber;\n});\n\n// reduce: the return value becomes the next accumulator\nconst sum = numbers.reduce(function (accumulator, currentNumber) {\n  console.log(&quot;accumulator:&quot;, accumulator, &quot;current:&quot;, currentNumber);\n  return accumulator + currentNumber;\n});\n// logs 3/56, 59/2, 61/48, 109/5 -&gt; sum is 114\n\n// (Extra) explicit initial value: safe on empty arrays\nconst safeSum = [].reduce((acc, n) =&gt; acc + n, 0); // 0</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Challenge solution: 100-character meanings</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import emojipedia from &quot;./emojipedia&quot;;\n\nconst meaningsArray = emojipedia.map(function (emojiEntry) {\n  return emojiEntry.meaning.substring(0, 100);\n});\n\nconsole.log(meaningsArray);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Truncate emoji meanings with map</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter CodeSandbox (fork it) containing the numbers demo code and an emojipedia.js file exporting an array of three emoji objects with a long meaning string each.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Produce a new array holding only each entry's meaning text, cut to at most 100 characters, and log it.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Comment out the earlier number demos (keep them for reference).</li><li>Import the emojipedia array at the top of the file: import emojipedia from &quot;./emojipedia&quot;;</li><li>Decide which method fits: one output per input item means map.</li><li>Call emojipedia.map with a callback whose parameter (e.g. emojiEntry) is one emoji object.</li><li>Inside the callback read emojiEntry.meaning and call substring(0, 100) on it.</li><li>Return that string from the callback, store the result of map in a const such as meaningsArray, and console.log it.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The console shows an array of three strings, each exactly 100 characters long (the original meanings are 121, 155 and 169 characters).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const meaningsArray = emojipedia.map(function (emojiEntry) {\n  return emojiEntry.meaning.substring(0, 100);\n});\nconsole.log(meaningsArray);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reference repo diverges: its solution uses substring(0, 101) and an arrow function with braces but no return, so meaningsArray ends up full of undefined. Fix by returning the value and using 100.</li><li>Using map when you do not need the resulting array (use forEach for side effects only).</li><li>Expecting filter or find to return the same thing: filter gives an array, find gives a single item or undefined.</li><li>Calling reduce on an empty array without an initial value throws a TypeError.</li><li>Forgetting to store the result: none of these methods mutate the original array.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l300", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces ES6 arrow functions as shorter syntax for function expressions, with the rules for dropping parentheses, braces and return. The exercise converts the map/filter/reduce/find/findIndex callbacks and the React Entry-mapping callback to arrow form.</p>\n<h3>From function declaration to arrow function, step by step</h3>\n<ul><li>Start: a normal function, e.g. function square(x) { return x * x; }. Passed inline to map it can be anonymous (name removed) because the surrounding call already explains its purpose.</li><li>Arrow step 1: delete the function keyword and put =&gt; between the parameter list and the body: (x) =&gt; { return x * x; }.</li><li>The arrow token is the two characters = and &gt; written together with nothing between them. Splitting them with a space, or writing a different symbol, is a syntax error.</li><li>Arrow step 2: with exactly one parameter the parentheses around it are optional (x =&gt; ...). With zero or two-plus parameters, parentheses are required, e.g. (x, y) =&gt; x * y.</li><li>Arrow step 3: when the body is a single expression whose value is returned, drop the curly braces and the return keyword (implicit return): x =&gt; x * x. The semicolon inside the braces goes as well.</li><li>If the body needs several statements, keep the braces and an explicit return.</li><li>An arrow function can also be stored in a variable: const square = x =&gt; x * x; then called as square(4) like any other function.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> To implicitly return an object literal, wrap it in parentheses: id =&gt; ({ id: id }). Without them the braces are read as a function body and the result is undefined.</li></ul>\n<h3>When to use them</h3>\n<ul><li>Arrow functions are purely a shorter notation here; the result is identical to the longer form.</li><li>Very compact code can be harder for beginners or junior teammates to read, so adopt the style according to project and team comfort.</li><li>They are very common in React because map, filter and reduce are used constantly, so you need to be able to read them even if you prefer the long form.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Arrow functions are not identical to regular functions in every respect: they have no own this or arguments and cannot be used with new. That does not matter for these callbacks, but it does matter for object methods and class code later.</li></ul>\n<h3>Converting the array-method callbacks</h3>\n<ul><li>map: numbers.map(x =&gt; x * 2) (one parameter, single expression).</li><li>filter: numbers.filter(num =&gt; num &lt; 10).</li><li>reduce: has two parameters, so the parentheses stay: (accumulator, currentNumber) =&gt; accumulator + currentNumber.</li><li>find and findIndex follow the same one-parameter pattern: num =&gt; num &gt; 10.</li></ul>\n<h3>Converting the React mapping callback</h3>\n<ul><li>In App.jsx the named helper createEntry(emojiTerm) returned an Entry element with key, emoji, name and description props taken from the emoji object (description comes from the meaning field).</li><li>Move that function body inline into emojipedia.map(...), remove the function keyword and name, add =&gt;, and strip the parentheses around the single emojiTerm parameter.</li><li>The return statement and braces can go because the body is one JSX expression, even though the props are spread across several lines for readability.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Keep a pair of parentheses around the multi-line JSX when dropping return: emojiTerm =&gt; (&lt;Entry ... /&gt;). Without them a line break after =&gt; can break parsing; wrapping is the usual React convention.</li><li>The key prop must still be present on each mapped element; use the item's unique id.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Progressive shortening of a squaring callback</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">var numbers = [3, 56, 2, 48, 5];\n\n// 0. named function declaration\nfunction square(x) {\n  return x * x;\n}\n\n// 1. anonymous function passed to map\nnumbers.map(function (x) { return x * x; });\n\n// 2. arrow with braces and explicit return\nnumbers.map((x) =&gt; { return x * x; });\n\n// 3. single parameter, implicit return\nnumbers.map(x =&gt; x * x); // [9, 3136, 4, 2304, 25]\n\n// arrow stored in a variable\nconst squareArrow = x =&gt; x * x;\n\n// two parameters need parentheses\nconst product = (x, y) =&gt; x * y;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Arrow versions of the array methods</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const doubled = numbers.map(x =&gt; x * 2);\nconst small = numbers.filter(num =&gt; num &lt; 10);\nconst total = numbers.reduce((accumulator, currentNumber) =&gt; accumulator + currentNumber);\nconst firstBig = numbers.find(num =&gt; num &gt; 10);\nconst firstBigIndex = numbers.findIndex(num =&gt; num &gt; 10);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx with an inline arrow callback (Vite layout from the reference repo: App.jsx in src; in the sandbox, where App.jsx is in src/components, import from &quot;./Entry&quot; and &quot;../emojipedia&quot;)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Entry from &quot;./components/Entry&quot;;\nimport emojipedia from &quot;./emojipedia&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;&lt;span&gt;emojipedia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;dl className=&quot;dictionary&quot;&gt;\n        {emojipedia.map(emojiTerm =&gt; (\n          &lt;Entry\n            key={emojiTerm.id}\n            emoji={emojiTerm.emoji}\n            name={emojiTerm.name}\n            description={emojiTerm.meaning}\n          /&gt;\n        ))}\n      &lt;/dl&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Convert callbacks to arrow functions</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Forked CodeSandbox with the commented numbers demos (map, filter, reduce, find, findIndex using anonymous functions) and an App.jsx that maps emojipedia through a createEntry function into Entry components.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Rewrite every callback as the shortest valid arrow function, in both the plain JS demos and the React code.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Comment out the earlier squaring example but keep it as reference.</li><li>Uncomment the map, filter, reduce, find and findIndex demos one at a time.</li><li>For each: delete the function keyword, add =&gt;, drop return and braces when the body is one expression, and drop the parameter parentheses when there is exactly one parameter (reduce keeps them).</li><li>In App.jsx, move the createEntry body into the emojipedia.map call as an arrow function returning the Entry element.</li><li>Check the page still renders every emoji entry.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Same console output and same rendered emoji list as before, with much shorter callbacks.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">{emojipedia.map(emojiTerm =&gt; (\n  &lt;Entry\n    key={emojiTerm.id}\n    emoji={emojiTerm.emoji}\n    name={emojiTerm.name}\n    description={emojiTerm.meaning}\n  /&gt;\n))}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing the arrow token with a space (= &gt;) or reversed (=&lt;); it must be = and &gt; joined as =&gt;.</li><li>Removing parentheses on a callback with two parameters such as reduce.</li><li>Dropping return while keeping the braces: the function then returns undefined.</li><li>Returning a multi-line JSX element without wrapping parentheses.</li><li>Cutting readability for teammates by shortening everything.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l301", "<p class=\"summary\">Second stage of the Keeper notes app: replace the single hard-coded Note with one Note per entry in the notes array, using props and map. The walkthrough finishes by shrinking the mapping callback into an arrow function.</p>\n<h3>Starting project</h3>\n<ul><li>The project continues Keeper part 1: Header renders a yellow bar with the 'Keeper' h1, Footer renders a copyright line with the current year from new Date().getFullYear(), and Note renders a div.note containing an h1 and a p.</li><li>notes.js contains four joke notes (Delegation, Loops, Arrays, Hardware vs. Software), each an object with key, title and content.</li><li>The challenge: render every object in notes.js as its own Note so that adding an object to the array adds a card with no JSX changes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> styles.css gives .note a white background, rounded corners, a shadow, a fixed 240px width and float: left, so the notes line up side by side and wrap.</li></ul>\n<h3>Making Note reusable with props</h3>\n<ul><li>Initially App renders one Note whose heading and paragraph text are written directly inside Note.</li><li>Give the Note element two props in App, title and content. The old hard-coded heading and paragraph text become their values for a first test.</li><li>In Note, the function parameter (props) is an object holding every attribute passed in. Insert values into JSX with curly braces: {props.title} inside the h1 and {props.content} inside the p.</li><li>The prop names used by the parent and child must match exactly. A typo on either side silently produces empty output; React DevTools shows the props received by each Note and helps spot mismatches.</li></ul>\n<h3>Getting the data in</h3>\n<ul><li>notes.js defines an array of note objects, each with key, title and content properties.</li><li>To use it elsewhere it needs a default export (export default notes) and App needs a matching import (import notes from &quot;./notes&quot;, adjusted for the relative path).</li><li>Logging the imported array is a quick check that the import works.</li></ul>\n<h3>Mapping notes to components</h3>\n<ul><li>Replace the single Note with a JavaScript expression in curly braces: notes.map(callback). The callback receives one note object and returns a Note element built from its fields (title={noteItem.title}, content={noteItem.content}).</li><li>Because it is JavaScript, the map expression must be wrapped in curly braces inside the JSX.</li><li>Every element produced by a list needs a unique key prop. The data already has a key field, so use that as the key; otherwise the console warns about missing keys.</li><li>Adding another object to notes.js makes another Note appear automatically, which is the point of mapping.</li><li>Refactor: move the named callback inside map, drop its name, convert to an arrow function and drop return and braces because the body is a single JSX expression. Behaviour stays the same.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The data field happens to be called key. Reading noteItem.key in App is fine because that is just an object property; what React strips is the key prop, so inside Note props.key would still be undefined.</li></ul>\n<h3>What comes later</h3>\n<ul><li>Upcoming parts add user input, forms, adding and deleting notes, and conditional rendering, which turn the static list into an interactive app.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Note component receiving props</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Note(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{props.title}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.content}&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Note;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Intermediate step: a named callback before the arrow-function refactor</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function createNote(noteItem) {\n  return (\n    &lt;Note\n      key={noteItem.key}\n      title={noteItem.title}\n      content={noteItem.content}\n    /&gt;\n  );\n}\n\n// inside App's JSX\n{notes.map(createNote)}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final App.jsx with an inline arrow callback (reference repo layout, App.jsx in src; key added, which the repo leaves out)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Header from &quot;./components/Header&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./components/Footer&quot;;\nimport Note from &quot;./components/Note&quot;;\nimport notes from &quot;./notes&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      {notes.map(noteItem =&gt; (\n        &lt;Note\n          key={noteItem.key}\n          title={noteItem.title}\n          content={noteItem.content}\n        /&gt;\n      ))}\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">notes.js shape</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const notes = [\n  { key: 1, title: &quot;Delegation&quot;, content: &quot;...&quot; },\n  { key: 2, title: &quot;Loops&quot;, content: &quot;...&quot; }\n];\n\nexport default notes;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Keeper app part 2: render every note</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter project (fork the CodeSandbox, or download it, run npm install, then npm start for the original Create React App version or npm run dev for the Vite version) with Header, Footer and Note components, a notes.js array of four notes, and an App rendering one Note with hard-coded text.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Render each object in the notes array as its own Note component, so editing notes.js changes the page dynamically.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In App, give the single &lt;Note /&gt; two props, title and content, using the old hard-coded heading and paragraph text as their values.</li><li>In Note.jsx, add the props parameter and replace the hard-coded text with {props.title} inside the h1 and {props.content} inside the p.</li><li>Open React DevTools, select Note and check that title and content appear in its props (a typo shows up as a missing or empty prop).</li><li>Add export default notes; at the bottom of notes.js (if not already there).</li><li>Import it in App: import notes from &quot;../notes&quot;; when App.jsx is in src/components (sandbox), or &quot;./notes&quot; when App.jsx is in src (Vite repo). Optionally console.log(notes) to check.</li><li>Replace the single Note with {notes.map(createNote)}, where createNote(noteItem) returns &lt;Note title={noteItem.title} content={noteItem.content} /&gt;.</li><li>Add key={noteItem.key} to the returned Note and check the console warning is gone.</li><li>Refactor: move the callback inline as noteItem =&gt; (&lt;Note ... /&gt;), dropping the function keyword, the name, return and the braces.</li><li>Add a fifth object (with key: 5) to notes.js and confirm a new card appears.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The Keeper page shows the header, one card per note (four with the starter data) and the footer, with each card's title and text coming from notes.js.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">{notes.map(noteItem =&gt; (\n  &lt;Note\n    key={noteItem.key}\n    title={noteItem.title}\n    content={noteItem.content}\n  /&gt;\n))}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reference repo diverges: it passes the id as a prop named id and never sets key, so React's missing-key warning appears. Use key={noteItem.key}.</li><li>Prop-name typos between parent and child give blank text rather than an error.</li><li>Forgetting the curly braces around notes.map in JSX renders the code as text or fails.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The key prop is not readable inside Note via props, so pass a separate prop if the child needs the id.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l302", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds a login screen that shows either a greeting or a login form depending on an isLoggedIn flag. Starts with an if/else helper function, refactors the form into reusable Login and Input components, then replaces the function with an inline ternary and finally the &amp;&amp; short-circuit pattern.</p>\n<h3>The goal: show different components for different state</h3>\n<ul><li>Typical use: a logged-out visitor sees a login form, a logged-in user sees a greeting or dashboard. The same App decides which one to render from a single boolean.</li><li>Start from the provided CodeSandbox (fork it). It has one App component with a heading and a hard-coded form. A module-level variable isLoggedIn (false at first) stands in for the result of authentication.</li><li>Rule of thumb: check one value, render one of several components.</li></ul>\n<h3>First approach: a helper function with if/else</h3>\n<ul><li>Write a function (e.g. renderConditionally) that tests isLoggedIn. If true it returns the h1 greeting element, otherwise it returns the form markup.</li><li>Call it inside the JSX with curly braces and parentheses: {renderConditionally()}. The braces switch from JSX to JavaScript, and the parentheses actually invoke the function.</li><li>Comparing with === true is redundant because isLoggedIn is already a boolean; writing if (isLoggedIn) is enough. Flip the variable between true and false and the page updates to show the other branch.</li><li>It works but is verbose for something done constantly in React, which motivates the shorter forms below.</li></ul>\n<h3>Single responsibility: extract Login and Input components</h3>\n<ul><li>Each component should do one job (single responsibility principle). The inline form markup is moved out of App.</li><li>Challenge in the lecture: create a Login component holding the whole form, and an Input component for each field, configured with props so it can be reused.</li><li>Login.jsx: import React, define function Login returning the form, export default Login. In App, import Login from './Login' and render &lt;Login /&gt;.</li><li>Input.jsx: returns a single &lt;input&gt;. Because the two inputs differ in type and placeholder, those become props: &lt;Input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;Username&quot; /&gt; and &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Password&quot; /&gt;.</li><li>Inside Input, read props.type and props.placeholder in curly braces: &lt;input type={props.type} placeholder={props.placeholder} /&gt;.</li><li>The page looks identical afterwards, but responsibilities are split: Input = one field, Login = whole form, App = which one is shown.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why an if statement cannot go inside JSX braces</h3>\n<ul><li>Curly braces in JSX accept only expressions (anything that resolves to a single value). An if/else, a loop or a switch is a statement and causes a syntax error if pasted in.</li><li>So the conditional logic needs to be rewritten as an expression to live inline where the output is rendered.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ternary operator</h3>\n<ul><li>Syntax: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse. The ? and : separate three expressions; because all three are expressions, the whole thing is one expression and legal inside JSX.</li><li>Applied here: {isLoggedIn ? &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt; : &lt;Login /&gt;}. Read it as 'is isLoggedIn true? then the h1, else the Login component'.</li><li>The condition can be the boolean itself; no need for == true.</li><li>Toggling the variable swaps which component appears, with no helper function needed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Rendering nothing: null in the false branch</h3>\n<ul><li>Sometimes you only want something when a condition holds. Demo: const currentTime = new Date().getHours(), which returns the hour 0-23.</li><li>{currentTime &gt; 12 ? &lt;h1&gt;Why are you still working?&lt;/h1&gt; : null} shows the heading after noon and nothing before. React renders nothing for null.</li><li>Testing trick: temporarily construct a Date for a fixed time (e.g. new Date(2019, 11, 1, 9)) to try morning and evening cases.</li></ul>\n<h3>The &amp;&amp; (AND) short-circuit pattern</h3>\n<ul><li>In JavaScript, a &amp;&amp; b only evaluates b when a is truthy. If a is falsy, evaluation stops and the result is a. This is called short-circuit evaluation.</li><li>In JSX: {currentTime &gt; 12 &amp;&amp; &lt;h1&gt;Why are you still working?&lt;/h1&gt;}. When the left side is true the right side (the element) is evaluated and rendered; when false it is skipped and React renders nothing for false.</li><li>It is equivalent to the ternary with null in the else branch, just shorter. Using it is optional; you will see it very often in real-world React code.</li><li>When reading such code, the intent is not to test that both sides are true, but to render the right side only if the left side is true.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> currentTime &gt; 12 is false during the 12:00-12:59 hour (getHours() returns 12), so the message actually appears from 13:00; use &gt;= 12 if noon itself should count.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The element on the right of &amp;&amp; or inside a ternary branch can span several lines; wrap it in parentheses, e.g. {cond &amp;&amp; ( &lt;div&gt;...&lt;/div&gt; )}, so the JSX parses cleanly.</li></ul>\n<h3>Extra notes</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Beware of numbers on the left: {count &amp;&amp; &lt;p/&gt;} renders a literal 0 when count is 0, because 0 is a valid renderable value. Use count &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; ... instead.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> true, false, null and undefined are all ignored by React when rendering, which is why both null and a false &amp;&amp; result show nothing.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Approach 1: helper function with if/else (after the form has been extracted into Login)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Login from &quot;./Login&quot;;\n\nvar isLoggedIn = false;\n\nfunction renderConditionally() {\n  if (isLoggedIn) {\n    return &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;;\n  } else {\n    return &lt;Login /&gt;;\n  }\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  return &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;{renderConditionally()}&lt;/div&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Input.jsx and Login.jsx: reusable Input driven by props, used twice inside Login</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// Input.jsx\nfunction Input(props) {\n  return &lt;input type={props.type} placeholder={props.placeholder} /&gt;;\n}\nexport default Input;\n\n// Login.jsx\nimport Input from &quot;./Input&quot;;\n\nfunction Login() {\n  return (\n    &lt;form className=&quot;form&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;Username&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Password&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Login;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Approach 2: inline ternary</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Login from &quot;./Login&quot;;\n\nvar isLoggedIn = false;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      {isLoggedIn ? &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt; : &lt;Login /&gt;}\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Ternary with null vs &amp;&amp; short-circuit</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const currentTime = new Date().getHours(); // 0-23\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      {/* ternary, nothing in the false branch */}\n      {currentTime &gt; 12 ? &lt;h1&gt;Why are you still working?&lt;/h1&gt; : null}\n\n      {/* same result with &amp;&amp; */}\n      {currentTime &gt; 12 &amp;&amp; &lt;h1&gt;Why are you still working?&lt;/h1&gt;}\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Refactor the login flow into components and inline conditionals</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Forked CodeSandbox with a single App file containing an h1 greeting and a hard-coded login form, plus an isLoggedIn variable.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show the greeting when isLoggedIn is true and the login form when false, using reusable components and inline conditional expressions.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add var isLoggedIn = false; at the top of App.jsx (outside the component) to stand in for an authentication result.</li><li>Write renderConditionally(): if (isLoggedIn) return the &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt;, else return the form markup. Call it inside the container div as {renderConditionally()} (braces to enter JavaScript, parentheses to call it). Flip the variable to true/false to check both branches.</li><li>Create Login.jsx: a function Login that returns the whole &lt;form className=&quot;form&quot;&gt; with the two inputs and the submit button; export default Login. In App.jsx import Login from &quot;./Login&quot; and return &lt;Login /&gt; from the else branch.</li><li>Create Input.jsx: a function Input(props) returning &lt;input type={props.type} placeholder={props.placeholder} /&gt;; export it. In Login, import it and replace the two raw inputs with &lt;Input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;Username&quot; /&gt; and &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Password&quot; /&gt;. The page should look exactly as before.</li><li>Delete renderConditionally and put the ternary straight into the JSX: {isLoggedIn ? &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt; : &lt;Login /&gt;}. Toggle isLoggedIn again to confirm.</li><li>Add const currentTime = new Date().getHours(); and render &lt;h1&gt;Why are you still working?&lt;/h1&gt; only when currentTime &gt; 12, first with a ternary whose false branch is null, then with the &amp;&amp; form.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Greeting when isLoggedIn is true, Username/Password form when false; the after-noon message appears only when the hour is greater than 12.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx (final ternary version)\nimport Login from &quot;./Login&quot;;\n\nvar isLoggedIn = false;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      {isLoggedIn ? &lt;h1&gt;Hello&lt;/h1&gt; : &lt;Login /&gt;}\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;\n\n// Login.jsx\nimport Input from &quot;./Input&quot;;\n\nfunction Login() {\n  return (\n    &lt;form className=&quot;form&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;Username&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Password&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Login&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Login;\n\n// Input.jsx\nfunction Input(props) {\n  return &lt;input type={props.type} placeholder={props.placeholder} /&gt;;\n}\nexport default Input;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Pasting an if/else into JSX braces gives a syntax error because it is a statement.</li><li>Forgetting the parentheses when calling the helper function: {renderConditionally} passes the function itself instead of its result.</li><li>Confusing &amp;&amp; in JSX with a 'both must be true' check; it is a render-if guard.</li><li>The reference repo names the form component LoginForm and adds a small Button component (type and text props), differing from the lecture's Login/Input split; it also writes isLoggedIn == true in the ternary (redundant) and leaves the old helper function commented out (with a misspelt name). The notes' solution uses the lecture naming.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l303", "<p class=\"summary\">Practice exercise: turn one login form into either a Login or a Register form depending on a userIsRegistered variable that must stay in App. Solved by passing the flag down as a prop, using a ternary for the button text and &amp;&amp; for the optional Confirm Password input.</p>\n<h3>Scenario</h3>\n<ul><li>A variable userIsRegistered lives in App and models the result of checking credentials: true means the user exists (show Login), false means they do not (show Register).</li><li>Register mode needs an extra Confirm Password input (type the password twice); Login mode shows only Username and Password.</li><li>Constraint: the variable must not be moved out of App, so the Form component needs to receive it another way.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1: pass the flag as a prop</h3>\n<ul><li>In App, render &lt;Form isRegistered={userIsRegistered} /&gt;. The prop name is chosen freely; the value is the variable inside curly braces.</li><li>Conditional rendering in React is very often driven by props handed down from a parent.</li><li>In Form, accept props and read props.isRegistered.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Data only flows downward: Form can read props.isRegistered but cannot change userIsRegistered in App. Changing it from a child comes later with state and callback props.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: button text with a ternary</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside the button's children use curly braces: {props.isRegistered ? &quot;Login&quot; : &quot;Register&quot;}.</li><li>Without the braces the expression is shown as plain text instead of evaluated.</li><li>Toggling the variable in App between true and false flips the label.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Only the text node inside &lt;button&gt; needs to change, so the ternary can return plain strings; the element itself stays the same in both modes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: Confirm Password only when registering</h3>\n<ul><li>Use &amp;&amp; so the input is rendered only when the user is not registered: {!props.isRegistered &amp;&amp; &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Confirm Password&quot; /&gt;}.</li><li>The first version compares explicitly (props.isRegistered === false &amp;&amp;); the shorter form negates with !, which flips a boolean and reads as 'is not registered'.</li><li>A ternary with null in the false branch gives the same result but is longer; &amp;&amp; is the most compact option.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> When the right-hand JSX after &amp;&amp; spans more than one line, wrap it in parentheses so the expression is parsed as one unit.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> ! converts any value to the opposite boolean, so !props.isRegistered is true when the prop is false (and also when it is missing/undefined).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">App passes the flag down</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Form from &quot;./Form&quot;;\n\nvar userIsRegistered = true;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Form isRegistered={userIsRegistered} /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Form.jsx: ternary for the button text, &amp;&amp; for the extra input</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import Input from &quot;./Input&quot;;\n\nfunction Form(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;form className=&quot;form&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;Username&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Password&quot; /&gt;\n      {!props.isRegistered &amp;&amp; (\n        &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Confirm Password&quot; /&gt;\n      )}\n      &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;\n        {props.isRegistered ? &quot;Login&quot; : &quot;Register&quot;}\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Form;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Login / Register form switch</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Forked sandbox with App holding userIsRegistered and a Form component that always renders username, password, confirm password and a Register button.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Without moving userIsRegistered, show Login text and hide Confirm Password when it is true; show Register and the Confirm Password field when it is false.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In App.jsx leave var userIsRegistered = true; where it is and render &lt;Form isRegistered={userIsRegistered} /&gt;.</li><li>In Form.jsx accept props in the function signature (function Form(props)).</li><li>Replace the button's fixed text with {props.isRegistered ? &quot;Login&quot; : &quot;Register&quot;} inside the &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;.</li><li>Wrap the Confirm Password input in {!props.isRegistered &amp;&amp; ( ... )} so it only renders when the user is not registered (first try props.isRegistered === false &amp;&amp;, then shorten it with !).</li><li>Flip userIsRegistered in App between true and false and check both screens.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> true: Username, Password, Login button. false: Username, Password, Confirm Password, Register button.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx\nimport Form from &quot;./Form&quot;;\n\nvar userIsRegistered = true;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Form isRegistered={userIsRegistered} /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default App;\n\n// Form.jsx\nimport Input from &quot;./Input&quot;;\n\nfunction Form(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;form className=&quot;form&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;Username&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Password&quot; /&gt;\n      {!props.isRegistered &amp;&amp; (\n        &lt;Input type=&quot;password&quot; placeholder=&quot;Confirm Password&quot; /&gt;\n      )}\n      &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;\n        {props.isRegistered ? &quot;Login&quot; : &quot;Register&quot;}\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/form&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default Form;\n\n// Input.jsx\nfunction Input(props) {\n  return &lt;input type={props.type} placeholder={props.placeholder} /&gt;;\n}\nexport default Input;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting curly braces around the conditional, so it appears as literal text.</li><li>Trying to move the variable instead of passing it as a prop.</li><li>The reference repo puts the components in src/components, uses a Button component and switches the whole &lt;Button text=...&gt; in a ternary rather than just the text, and renders the Username field as a raw &lt;input&gt; while the others use &lt;Input&gt;. The notes' solution follows the lecture's simpler version.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l304", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces state as the thing a React UI is derived from, and contrasts declarative (describe the UI per state) with imperative (DOM-manipulating) code using a strike-through to-do example. Ends by showing that a plain variable change does not re-render, which leads to hooks.</p>\n<h3>UI as a function of state</h3>\n<ul><li>Core React idea: UI = f(state). The component describes what to show for each value of some data, and the screen follows when that data changes.</li><li>Analogy kept short: ice and water are the same thing shown differently depending on a temperature value.</li><li>Example app: a to-do item whose text is struck through once done. The state is a boolean isDone, false initially, switched to true when the user clicks the item.</li></ul>\n<h3>Declarative version in React</h3>\n<ul><li>Start with a component rendering &lt;p&gt;Buy milk&lt;/p&gt;.</li><li>Add inline styling through the style attribute, which takes a JavaScript object: style={{ textDecoration: &quot;line-through&quot; }} (outer braces = JSX expression, inner braces = object literal).</li><li>Extract the object into a constant, e.g. const strikeThrough = { textDecoration: &quot;line-through&quot; }, so the JSX stays readable.</li><li>Make it conditional with a ternary: style={isDone ? strikeThrough : null}. Null means no style applied.</li><li>Setting isDone to true in the source shows the struck text, false shows plain text. The code declares how the UI looks under each condition; this is declarative programming.</li></ul>\n<h3>Imperative version in plain JavaScript</h3>\n<ul><li>The approach used earlier in the course: fetch the element with document.getElementById and assign to its style.textDecoration property directly.</li><li>Add a button with an onClick handler calling a function (strike) that applies line-through, and a second button/function (unStrike) that resets the property.</li><li>This is imperative: you explicitly grab an element and tell it what to do at each change. It achieves the same visual result but you must manage every transition by hand.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why changing a plain variable is not enough</h3>\n<ul><li>In the declarative component, wiring buttons to functions that set isDone = true / false looks like it should work, but the page does not update.</li><li>A component's output is produced when it renders; changing a normal variable afterwards does not trigger React to render again, so the displayed elements stay as they were.</li><li>React needs to be told about the change through its own mechanism. Hooks are functions that let a component tie into React state (read it and change it); the next lecture covers the most common one, useState.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The entry file renders App once with root.render(&lt;App /&gt;). After that React only calls App again (re-renders) when that component's state, its props or a parent changes; a module-level var is none of those, so the click handlers run but the JSX is never re-evaluated.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Even if App did re-run, a plain variable declared inside the component would be re-created with its initial value on every render, so it cannot remember anything between renders. State solves both problems.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Declarative styling driven by a variable (still static, no hook yet)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const strikeThrough = { textDecoration: &quot;line-through&quot; };\nvar isDone = false;\n\nfunction App() {\n  return &lt;p style={isDone ? strikeThrough : null}&gt;Buy milk&lt;/p&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Imperative equivalent: grab the element and set its style directly</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// &lt;button onclick=&quot;strike()&quot;&gt;Strike&lt;/button&gt;  &lt;button onclick=&quot;unStrike()&quot;&gt;Undo&lt;/button&gt;\nfunction strike() {\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;).style.textDecoration = &quot;line-through&quot;;\n}\n\nfunction unStrike() {\n  document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;).style.textDecoration = null; // clears the inline style\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Reference repo state of this lesson: variable-based buttons that do NOT update the UI</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">var isDone = false;\nfunction strike() { isDone = true; }\nfunction unStrike() { isDone = false; }\n\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;p style={isDone ? { textDecoration: &quot;line-through&quot; } : null}&gt;Buy milk&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={strike}&gt;Change to strike through&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={unStrike}&gt;Change back&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Expecting a normal variable assignment to refresh the screen; it will not re-render.</li><li>Mixing up the two brace pairs in style={{ ... }}.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Writing CSS property names with hyphens inside the style object: React expects camelCase (textDecoration, backgroundColor).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l305", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds a counter to introduce the useState hook: why a changing variable or repeated ReactDOM.render is not the answer, how useState returns a [value, setter] pair, how array destructuring names them, and how the setter triggers a re-render. Ends with a small challenge to add a decrement button.</p>\n<h3>Starting point and the naive attempts</h3>\n<ul><li>Start: a div with an h1 (hard-coded number) and a plus button, all rendered from the entry file. Goal: each click increases the number.</li><li>Step 1: replace the literal with a variable (count = 0) displayed with {count}. Nothing visible changes.</li><li>Step 2: attach behaviour with onClick. React's JSX uses the camelCase form of the HTML attribute and takes a function reference in braces: &lt;button onClick={increase}&gt;. Test with a console.log inside increase; it logs on every click.</li><li>Step 3: count++ inside increase. The console shows the number rising, but the h1 does not change, because JSX elements are descriptions rendered by ReactDOM; changing a variable does not redraw them.</li><li>Brute force fix: call the root render again inside increase. It works, but it re-renders everything, repeats code and is wasteful. Hooks are the intended solution. (Extra) In the older ReactDOM.render API this meant repeating ReactDOM.render(...); with React 18's createRoot it would be calling root.render(...) again.</li></ul>\n<h3>Preparing: hooks live in function components</h3>\n<ul><li>Rule: hooks may only be called inside a function component (or another hook), so the UI is moved into function App() { ... return (&lt;div&gt;...&lt;/div&gt;); } in its own file.</li><li>The entry file shrinks to rendering &lt;App /&gt; into the root; the old repeated render calls are deleted.</li></ul>\n<h3>What useState returns</h3>\n<ul><li>useState comes from the react package. Either React.useState(...) or a named import: import { useState } from &quot;react&quot; (a non-default export, as in the earlier import/export lesson).</li><li>Called with an argument, it returns an array of two items: [currentValue, setterFunction]. The argument is the initial state, used only on the first render. With no argument the first item is undefined.</li><li>Accessing the value as state[0] works but is unreadable.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Rules of hooks: call them at the top level of the component, never inside if statements, loops or nested functions, so React sees them in the same order on every render.</li></ul>\n<h3>Array destructuring to name the pair</h3>\n<ul><li>Destructuring unpacks an array by position into named variables: const [red, green, blue] = rgb; (demo with an RGB colour array, where rgb[0] means nothing but red is self-explanatory).</li><li>Applied to hooks: const [count, setCount] = useState(0). First name holds the current value; second name is the function that updates it.</li><li>By convention the setter is named set + the capitalised state name (setCount).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Destructuring is covered more fully in a later lesson; here the pattern just needs to be memorised.</li></ul>\n<h3>Updating state</h3>\n<ul><li>Call the setter with the new value. setCount(12) on click sets the count to 12 and keeps resetting it to 12 on later clicks, so the new value must be derived from the current one.</li><li>Counter: function increase() { setCount(count + 1); }.</li><li>Flow: App renders, useState supplies the initial value, {count} appears in the h1, click runs increase, setCount stores the new value, and React re-renders the component so the h1 shows it. Only the parts of the DOM that changed are updated, which is more efficient than re-rendering manually.</li><li>Challenge solution: a minus button wired to decrease, which calls setCount(count - 1).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> count is a const that holds the value for this particular render. setCount does not change it in place; it schedules a new render in which useState returns the updated number.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> When the next value depends on the previous one, the updater form setCount(prev =&gt; prev + 1) is the safer pattern (it always uses the latest value, e.g. when several updates happen in one click).</li></ul>\n<h3>Recap and study advice</h3>\n<ul><li>State gives interactivity: the UI reflects the state, and calling the setter is how you change it.</li><li>Suggested practice: delete the component and rewrite it from memory until the useState syntax is familiar.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Attempt that logs but does not update the UI</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">var count = 0;\nfunction increase() {\n  count++;\n  console.log(count); // rises, h1 stays the same\n}\n// &lt;h1&gt;{count}&lt;/h1&gt; &lt;button onClick={increase}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Array destructuring demo</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const rgb = [9, 132, 227];\nconst [red, green, blue] = rgb;\nconsole.log(blue); // 227</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished counter with useState</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  function increase() {\n    setCount(count + 1);\n  }\n\n  function decrease() {\n    setCount(count - 1);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{count}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={decrease}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={increase}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Counter with increase and decrease buttons</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Forked sandbox where the whole app (div, h1 with a hard-coded number, plus button) sits in the entry file.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> A counter that goes up with + and down with -, using useState.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create App.jsx with function App() returning the div (className &quot;container&quot;), the h1 and the + button; export default App. In the entry file render &lt;App /&gt; into the root and delete the old repeated render code.</li><li>Add import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;; at the top of App.jsx.</li><li>Inside App, before the return, write const [count, setCount] = useState(0); (0 is the starting value).</li><li>Replace the hard-coded number with {count} inside the h1.</li><li>Write function increase() { setCount(count + 1); } inside App and attach it with &lt;button onClick={increase}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt; (pass the function, do not call it).</li><li>Challenge: add &lt;button onClick={decrease}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt; and function decrease() { setCount(count - 1); }. Click both buttons to check the h1 updates.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Starts at 0, plus increments, minus decrements, with the screen updating each time.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx\nimport { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  function increase() {\n    setCount(count + 1);\n  }\n\n  function decrease() {\n    setCount(count - 1);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{count}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={decrease}&gt;-&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={increase}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;\n\n// main.jsx (entry file)\nimport { createRoot } from &quot;react-dom/client&quot;;\nimport App from &quot;./App&quot;;\n\ncreateRoot(document.getElementById(&quot;root&quot;)).render(&lt;App /&gt;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing onClick={increase()} (calls immediately) instead of onClick={increase}.</li><li>Mutating a normal variable (count++) and expecting a refresh.</li><li>Calling hooks outside a function component.</li><li>Setting a fixed value such as setCount(12) rather than building the new value from count.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Expecting count to hold the new value on the line right after setCount(count + 1); it only changes on the next render.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l306", "<p class=\"summary\">Practice with useState: show the current time in an h1 and refresh it when a Get Time button is clicked, then extend it so the clock ticks every second using setInterval. The walkthrough keeps the time in state and updates it through the setter from a handler.</p>\n<h3>The challenge</h3>\n<ul><li>Part 1: an h1 initially holds placeholder text and a Get Time button sits below. The time string comes from new Date().toLocaleTimeString(). The h1 must show the time at load, and a click must refresh it to the current time.</li><li>Part 2 (bonus): make the h1 update every second without any click, using setInterval.</li><li>setInterval(fn, ms) calls fn repeatedly every ms milliseconds. Pass the function first and the delay second; leaving out the delay makes it fire as fast as possible and can freeze the sandbox. If that happens, close and reopen the tab; the code is saved.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> toLocaleTimeString() formats the time using the browser's locale (e.g. 2:05:09 PM in en-US, 14:05:09 in en-GB); it returns a string, which is what gets stored in state.</li></ul>\n<h3>Part 1 walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside App, compute const now = new Date().toLocaleTimeString(); and log it once to see the format (e.g. 14:05:09, depending on locale). The starter's demo console.log can then be removed.</li><li>Create state for it: const [time, setTime] = useState(now). The initial value is the time when the component first renders. Editors usually auto-import useState from react.</li><li>Use {time} in the h1 in place of the placeholder.</li><li>Write function updateTime() and set it as the button's onClick: &lt;button onClick={updateTime}&gt;.</li><li>Inside updateTime build a fresh value (const newTime = new Date().toLocaleTimeString()) and call setTime(newTime). Names are free; their position in the destructured array is what matters (first = value, second = updater).</li><li>Each click changes the state and React re-renders the h1 with the new time.</li></ul>\n<h3>Part 2 walkthrough</h3>\n<ul><li>Call setInterval(updateTime, 1000) so the same update function runs every second. The state changes each tick, so the h1 refreshes continuously.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Calling setInterval directly in the component body registers a new timer on every render; each tick sets state, which re-renders and adds yet another timer, so the number of timers keeps growing (the reference repo does the same). The idiomatic approach is useEffect(() =&gt; { const id = setInterval(updateTime, 1000); return () =&gt; clearInterval(id); }, []), which starts one timer after the first render and clears it when the component unmounts.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Pass a function reference, not updateTime(), to setInterval or onClick.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution for both parts (lecture approach; setInterval in the component body accumulates timers, see the Extra note)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const now = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n  const [time, setTime] = useState(now);\n\n  function updateTime() {\n    const newTime = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n    setTime(newTime);\n  }\n\n  setInterval(updateTime, 1000);\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{time}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={updateTime}&gt;Get Time&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Same ticking clock with useEffect and cleanup</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState, useEffect } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [time, setTime] = useState(new Date().toLocaleTimeString());\n\n  useEffect(() =&gt; {\n    const id = setInterval(() =&gt; setTime(new Date().toLocaleTimeString()), 1000);\n    return () =&gt; clearInterval(id);\n  }, []);\n\n  return &lt;h1&gt;{time}&lt;/h1&gt;;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Clock with Get Time button and live ticking</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Sandbox whose App renders an h1 with placeholder text and a Get Time button; comments describe the two-part challenge and include a commented-out sayHi() example that logs every second via setInterval(sayHi, 1000).</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show the current time, refresh it on button click, then make it tick every second.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Inside App, get the time string with const now = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();.</li><li>Import useState from react and create const [time, setTime] = useState(now); then render {time} in the h1 instead of the placeholder.</li><li>Write function updateTime() { const newTime = new Date().toLocaleTimeString(); setTime(newTime); } inside App.</li><li>Attach it to the button: &lt;button onClick={updateTime}&gt;Get Time&lt;/button&gt;. Click and check the h1 jumps to the current time.</li><li>Optional: uncomment the sayHi example to see setInterval(fn, 1000) log once per second, then remove it.</li><li>Bonus: add setInterval(updateTime, 1000); so the h1 refreshes every second without clicking (always pass the delay as the second argument).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The h1 shows the time on load, changes when the button is pressed, and with part 2 changes every second.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const now = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n  const [time, setTime] = useState(now);\n\n  function updateTime() {\n    const newTime = new Date().toLocaleTimeString();\n    setTime(newTime);\n  }\n\n  setInterval(updateTime, 1000); // part 2 (lecture approach)\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{time}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={updateTime}&gt;Get Time&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Omitting the interval delay (or mixing the argument order) in setInterval.</li><li>Calling setInterval in the render body creates a new timer on every render, so timers pile up and the number of state updates grows each second; in real apps start the timer once with useEffect and clear it with clearInterval.</li><li>Using the stale const now as the update value instead of computing a fresh time.</li><li>The reference solution keeps two separate state variables (time for the button, currtime for the ticking clock) with two handlers and two h1s, unlike the lecture's single shared state and update function.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l307", "<p class=\"summary\">A deep dive into ES6 destructuring: array destructuring by position, object destructuring by key, renaming, default values, nested objects, and a custom useAnimals function that mimics how useState returns [value, setter]. It ends by setting a challenge: destructure a nested cars array to fill an HTML table in React.</p>\n<h3>Array destructuring (by position)</h3>\n<ul><li>Square brackets on the left of the assignment declare the variables: const [cat, dog] = animals; takes item 0 into cat and item 1 into dog.</li><li>It is a shorter way of writing cat = animals[0]; dog = animals[1].</li><li>Because matching is positional, the variable names are free choice: [c, d] would hold the same objects.</li><li>Names declared this way share the file's normal scope rules: re-declaring cat elsewhere in the same scope throws an error saying the identifier has already been declared.</li><li>Typical use: data arrives from an API, a module or a framework (React's useState) and you log it first to see its shape, then unpack it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Object destructuring (by key)</h3>\n<ul><li>Curly braces on the left: const { name, sound } = cat; creates variables name and sound equal to cat.name and cat.sound.</li><li>Unlike arrays, matching is by property name. A name that does not exist as a key (e.g. animalSound) just yields undefined.</li><li>You may pull out only the properties you need; the rest of the object is ignored.</li><li>Rename while destructuring with a colon: const { name: catName, sound: catSound } = cat. After that only catName and catSound exist; name and sound are not created. Handy for terse or unclear API field names.</li><li>Default values use equals: const { name = &quot;Fluffy&quot;, sound = &quot;Purr&quot; } = cat. The default is used only when the property is undefined (for example the key was deleted from the data). Useful when remote data has gaps and the page should not break.</li></ul>\n<h3>Nested destructuring</h3>\n<ul><li>If cat has a feedingRequirements property that itself is an object { food, water }, you can destructure it in the same statement: const { name, sound, feedingRequirements: { food, water } } = cat.</li><li>Instead of giving the nested key an alias, you open another pair of braces and list the inner keys. Afterwards food and water are plain variables (2 and 3 in the demo).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The nested key itself (feedingRequirements) is not created as a variable when you do this; add it separately if you also need the whole object.</li><li>The same pattern works for arrays inside objects, e.g. coloursByPopularity: [firstColour] takes the first array item.</li></ul>\n<h3>How useState-style returns work</h3>\n<ul><li>A function can return an array of two things, a value and a function. The demo useAnimals(animal) returns [animal.name, () =&gt; console.log(animal.sound)].</li><li>Calling it and destructuring the result: const [animal, makeSound] = useAnimals(cat); gives animal === &quot;cat&quot; and makeSound() logs &quot;meow&quot;.</li><li>useState follows the same shape: it returns [currentValue, setterFunction], and you choose the names when destructuring. This is why array destructuring is used there.</li><li>Exporting multiple things from data.js: animals stays the default export and useAnimals is added as a named export, so the import becomes import animals, { useAnimals } from &quot;./data&quot;; (default name outside the braces, named exports inside).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Hooks such as useState are named with a use prefix by convention; useAnimals borrows the prefix only to show the shape, it is not a real React hook.</li></ul>\n<h3>The challenge set at the end</h3>\n<ul><li>practice.js exports a cars array of two objects; each has model, coloursByPopularity (array) and speedStats (object with topSpeed, zeroToSixty).</li><li>The App already renders a table using certain variable names; those lines must not be changed. Only destructuring code is added so the Brand, Top Speed and Top Colour cells fill with model, speedStats.topSpeed and the first colour.</li><li>Console warnings about table markup come from how the simple table is structured in React and are unrelated to the task.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">data.js exports and the matching import in index.js / App.jsx</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// data.js\nconst animals = [\n  { name: &quot;cat&quot;, sound: &quot;meow&quot;, feedingRequirements: { food: 2, water: 3 } },\n  { name: &quot;dog&quot;, sound: &quot;woof&quot; }\n];\n\nfunction useAnimals(animal) {\n  return [\n    animal.name,\n    function () {\n      console.log(animal.sound);\n    }\n  ];\n}\n\nexport default animals;\nexport { useAnimals };\n\n// index.js / App.jsx\nimport animals, { useAnimals } from &quot;./data&quot;;\nconsole.log(animals); // inspect the shape before destructuring</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Array and object destructuring side by side (each line is an alternative; names must not repeat in one scope)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const animals = [\n  { name: &quot;cat&quot;, sound: &quot;meow&quot;, feedingRequirements: { food: 2, water: 3 } },\n  { name: &quot;dog&quot;, sound: &quot;woof&quot; }\n];\n\nconst [cat, dog] = animals;                       // by position\nconst { name, sound } = cat;                      // by key\nconst { name: catName, sound: catSound } = cat;   // rename\nconst { name: n = &quot;Fluffy&quot; } = cat;               // default if undefined\nconst { feedingRequirements: { food, water } } = cat; // nested</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">A homemade useState lookalike</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function useAnimals(animal) {\n  return [animal.name, () =&gt; console.log(animal.sound)];\n}\n\nconst [animal, makeSound] = useAnimals(cat);\nconsole.log(animal); // &quot;cat&quot;\nmakeSound();         // &quot;meow&quot;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Destructure the cars array for a table</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> CodeSandbox starter with practice.js (cars array of Honda Civic and Tesla Model 3 objects with nested coloursByPopularity array and speedStats object) and an index/App file with commented-out table code under a CHALLENGE line.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Uncomment the challenge code and, without editing it, add destructuring so the table shows each car's model, top speed and most popular colour.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Uncomment the code below the CHALLENGE line in the app file; do not edit those lines.</li><li>Import cars from practice.js (it is the default export): import cars from &quot;./practice&quot;;</li><li>Optionally console.log(cars) to see the shape: an array of two objects, each with model, coloursByPopularity (array) and speedStats (object).</li><li>Destructure the array by position into two variables: const [honda, tesla] = cars; this makes honda.model and tesla.model work.</li><li>From each car object, destructure speedStats and open braces after a colon to reach topSpeed, renaming it with another colon (topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed / topSpeed: teslaTopSpeed).</li><li>From each car object, destructure coloursByPopularity and open square brackets after a colon to take the first item as hondaTopColour / teslaTopColour.</li><li>Check the table renders all three cells per row and the undefined-variable errors are gone (the table-markup warnings may remain).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> A table with rows for Tesla Model 3 (150, red) and Honda Civic (140, black).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import cars from &quot;./practice&quot;;\n\nconst [honda, tesla] = cars;\n\n// one statement per car; object pattern mixes an array sub-pattern and an object sub-pattern\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [hondaTopColour],\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed },\n} = honda;\n\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [teslaTopColour],\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: teslaTopSpeed },\n} = tesla;\n\n// used by the unchanged table code, e.g.\n// &lt;td&gt;{tesla.model}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{teslaTopSpeed}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{teslaTopColour}&lt;/td&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Re-declaring a destructured name already used in the same scope causes an identifier-already-declared error.</li><li>Misspelling a key when destructuring an object silently gives undefined instead of an error.</li><li>Renaming with a colon removes the original name; using the old variable afterwards fails.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Defaults only apply when the value is undefined, not for null or empty strings.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's table has only two &lt;th&gt; cells (Brand, Top Speed) although each row has three &lt;td&gt; cells; add a Top Colour header if you want the columns labelled.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Putting &lt;tr&gt; directly inside &lt;table&gt; (no &lt;tbody&gt;) is what produces the React validateDOMNesting console warnings; wrapping rows in &lt;thead&gt;/&lt;tbody&gt; silences them.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l308", "<p class=\"summary\">Walkthrough of the cars destructuring challenge: unpack the array into honda and tesla, then dig into each object for the renamed top speed and the first colour. Each added line removes one more undefined-variable error from the table code.</p>\n<h3>Step 1: import and inspect the data</h3>\n<ul><li>practice.js needs a default export of cars; the app file imports it below the other imports.</li><li>Logging cars first shows an array of two objects with nested objects and arrays, similar to a real API response.</li><li>Working out which variable names the (unchangeable) table code expects tells you what to create: honda, tesla, hondaTopSpeed, teslaTopSpeed, hondaTopColour, teslaTopColour.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: destructure the array</h3>\n<ul><li>const [honda, tesla] = cars; assigns item 0 and item 1 by position.</li><li>This alone makes honda.model and tesla.model resolve, so the model column renders and the related errors disappear.</li><li>honda now holds the whole Honda Civic object.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: nested object for top speed</h3>\n<ul><li>topSpeed sits inside the speedStats object, so destructure speedStats from honda and open a second pair of braces after a colon to reach inside it.</li><li>Inside, pick topSpeed and rename it with another colon, e.g. topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed. zeroToSixty can be pulled out in the same braces too, though the table does not use it.</li><li>Repeat for tesla with teslaTopSpeed. The top speed column now fills in for both rows.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If zeroToSixty is destructured from both cars without renaming, the second const zeroToSixty clashes with the first; rename one (zeroToSixty: teslaZeroToSixty) or leave it out.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: nested array for top colour</h3>\n<ul><li>coloursByPopularity is an array, so after the colon use square brackets: coloursByPopularity: [hondaTopColour] takes the first element and names it.</li><li>Same again for tesla. The table now shows black for Honda and red for Tesla.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The walkthrough builds the speed and colour patterns as separate statements; the reference repo merges them into one statement per car, which works because a single object pattern may mix array and object sub-patterns.</li></ul>\n<h3>Rule of thumb</h3>\n<ul><li>Mirror the shape of the data in the pattern: braces where the data is an object, brackets where it is an array, a colon to step one level deeper or to rename.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Built up step by step as separate statements (equivalent to the merged final version)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import cars from &quot;./practice&quot;;\n\nconst [honda, tesla] = cars;\n\nconst {\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed },\n} = honda;\nconst {\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: teslaTopSpeed },\n} = tesla;\n\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [hondaTopColour],\n} = honda;\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [teslaTopColour],\n} = tesla;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final destructuring for the challenge (merged form, as in the reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import cars from &quot;./practice&quot;;\n\nconst [honda, tesla] = cars;\n\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [hondaTopColour],\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed },\n} = honda;\n\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [teslaTopColour],\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: teslaTopSpeed },\n} = tesla;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Solution to the cars destructuring challenge</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The previous lecture's sandbox with the challenge table code uncommented and practice.js holding the cars array.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the table render model, top speed and top colour for both cars purely by adding destructuring.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Confirm practice.js ends with export default cars; and import it in the app file: import cars from &quot;./practice&quot;;</li><li>console.log(cars) to inspect the nesting, and list the variable names the table code uses (honda, tesla, hondaTopSpeed, teslaTopSpeed, hondaTopColour, teslaTopColour).</li><li>Destructure [honda, tesla] from cars; the model column now renders.</li><li>Destructure speedStats: { topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed } from honda and the same with teslaTopSpeed from tesla; the top speed column fills in.</li><li>Destructure coloursByPopularity: [hondaTopColour] from honda and coloursByPopularity: [teslaTopColour] from tesla; the colour column fills in.</li><li>Optionally merge each car's two patterns into a single const statement.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Table rows show Tesla Model 3 / 150 / red and Honda Civic / 140 / black with no undefined-variable errors.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import cars from &quot;./practice&quot;;\n\nconst [honda, tesla] = cars;\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [hondaTopColour],\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: hondaTopSpeed },\n} = honda;\nconst {\n  coloursByPopularity: [teslaTopColour],\n  speedStats: { topSpeed: teslaTopSpeed },\n} = tesla;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the colon-then-braces (or brackets) means you get the nested object itself, not the inner value.</li><li>Names must match the keys exactly (coloursByPopularity, British spelling) or the result is undefined.</li><li>Do not edit the provided table code; create the variables it expects.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l309", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows how to attach event handlers to JSX elements (onClick, onMouseOver, onMouseOut), update state from them with useState, and combine that with inline styles and a ternary to build a button that turns black on hover and white when the mouse leaves.</p>\n<h3>Attaching handlers in JSX</h3>\n<ul><li>HTML event attributes become camelCase props in JSX: onclick becomes onClick, onmouseover becomes onMouseOver, onmouseout becomes onMouseOut.</li><li>The value is a JavaScript expression in curly braces that references a function: onClick={handleClick}. Pass the function itself, not a call (handleClick without parentheses), so React runs it when the event fires rather than at render.</li><li>A first test handler that only logs to the console proves the wiring; it fires each time the event occurs.</li><li>W3Schools' HTML event attributes list is a handy catalogue of other events to react to; remember to camelCase the name in JSX.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> React passes every handler a synthetic event object as its first argument (handleClick(event)); this lecture does not need it, the forms lecture uses event.target.value.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> onMouseOver/onMouseOut also fire when the pointer moves between child elements inside the target; onMouseEnter/onMouseLeave fire only once on entering and leaving, which is often what a hover effect wants.</li></ul>\n<h3>Changing what is rendered from an event</h3>\n<ul><li>Heading text that must change is stored in state: const [headingText, setHeadingText] = useState(&quot;Hello&quot;). The name pair (value, setValue) is the usual convention.</li><li>In the handler call setHeadingText(&quot;Submitted&quot;). React re-renders the component and the h1 {headingText} shows the new value.</li><li>useState has to be imported from react before use: import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;; (or just import { useState } from &quot;react&quot; with the newer JSX transform used by Vite).</li><li>The argument to useState is the initial value used on first render only.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hover colour change: state + inline style + ternary</h3>\n<ul><li>Inline style in JSX takes an object with camelCase keys inside double braces: style={{ backgroundColor: &quot;black&quot; }} (outer braces for the JS expression, inner for the object).</li><li>A second state value tracks hover: const [isMouseOver, setMouseOver] = useState(false). It starts false because the pointer is not over the button on load.</li><li>handleMouseOver calls setMouseOver(true); handleMouseOut calls setMouseOver(false).</li><li>The style picks its colour with a ternary: backgroundColor: isMouseOver ? &quot;black&quot; : &quot;white&quot;. Every state change triggers a re-render that recomputes this.</li><li>Without onMouseOut the button would turn black and never revert; both events are needed.</li><li>This brings together conditional rendering, inline styling, event props and state.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The setter name setMouseOver does not have to mirror the state name exactly (isMouseOver); only the [value, setter] order from useState matters.</li></ul>\n<h3>What comes next</h3>\n<ul><li>The following lecture uses the same input element to handle form events, so the input here is just a placeholder for now.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished App: click changes heading, hover changes button colour (from reference repo, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [headingText, setHeadingText] = useState(&quot;Hello&quot;);\n  const [isMouseOver, setMouseOver] = useState(false);\n\n  function handleClick() {\n    setHeadingText(&quot;Submitted&quot;);\n  }\n  function handleMouseOver() {\n    setMouseOver(true);\n  }\n  function handleMouseOut() {\n    setMouseOver(false);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{headingText}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;What's your name?&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;button\n        style={{ backgroundColor: isMouseOver ? &quot;black&quot; : &quot;white&quot; }}\n        onClick={handleClick}\n        onMouseOver={handleMouseOver}\n        onMouseOut={handleMouseOut}\n      &gt;\n        Submit\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Hover-to-black button</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> CodeSandbox starter: App with an h1 saying Hello, a text input with a name placeholder and a Submit button.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> On clicking Submit the heading changes to Submitted; hovering the button turns its background black and leaving turns it white again.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add onClick={handleClick} to the button; first just console.log in it to check wiring.</li><li>Create headingText state (initial &quot;Hello&quot;), render it in the h1 and make handleClick call setHeadingText(&quot;Submitted&quot;).</li><li>Add onMouseOver with handleMouseOver; log to confirm it fires on entering the button.</li><li>Create isMouseOver state (initial false); handleMouseOver sets it to true.</li><li>Apply style={{ backgroundColor: isMouseOver ? &quot;black&quot; : &quot;white&quot; }} to the button.</li><li>Add onMouseOut with handleMouseOut that sets isMouseOver back to false.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> White button turns black under the cursor, back to white when the cursor leaves; clicking changes the heading to Submitted.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [headingText, setHeadingText] = useState(&quot;Hello&quot;);\n  const [isMouseOver, setMouseOver] = useState(false);\n\n  function handleClick() { setHeadingText(&quot;Submitted&quot;); }\n  function handleMouseOver() { setMouseOver(true); }\n  function handleMouseOut() { setMouseOver(false); }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{headingText}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; placeholder=&quot;What's your name?&quot; /&gt;\n      &lt;button\n        style={{ backgroundColor: isMouseOver ? &quot;black&quot; : &quot;white&quot; }}\n        onClick={handleClick}\n        onMouseOver={handleMouseOver}\n        onMouseOut={handleMouseOut}\n      &gt;\n        Submit\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing onClick={handleClick()} calls the function during render instead of on click.</li><li>Using lowercase attribute names (onclick, onmouseover) instead of camelCase.</li><li>Forgetting the double braces for the inline style object.</li><li>Misspelling the event name (onMouseOut) so the revert never fires.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> For plain colour-on-hover, CSS :hover would be simpler; this exercise is about practising state and events.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l310", "<p class=\"summary\">Covers reading input values in React via onChange and event.target.value, storing them in state, controlled components, delaying the heading update until Submit, and using a form's onSubmit with event.preventDefault() to stop the page refresh.</p>\n<h3>onChange and the event object</h3>\n<ul><li>Inputs accept an onChange prop; the handler runs on every change, i.e. each keystroke.</li><li>React passes the handler an event object. event.target is the element that fired it, so event.target.value is the current text, and event.target.placeholder / event.target.type expose other attributes.</li><li>Logging only the fact that a change happened is not useful; reading event.target.value is what matters.</li></ul>\n<h3>Storing the typed value in state</h3>\n<ul><li>const [name, setName] = useState(&quot;&quot;) holds the text, starting empty. Import useState from react.</li><li>handleChange calls setName(event.target.value). Rendering &lt;h1&gt;Hello {name}&lt;/h1&gt; then updates live as the user types.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The handler reads the value synchronously from event.target, so read it inside the handler, not in a delayed callback.</li></ul>\n<h3>Controlled components</h3>\n<ul><li>In plain HTML the input element tracks its own value. In React the recommended approach is to set the input's value prop to the state variable (value={name}) so state is the single source of truth.</li><li>An input whose displayed value comes from state and whose changes go back into state through onChange is a controlled component. The lecture points to the React docs on controlled components.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If you set value without onChange, the field becomes read-only and React warns.</li></ul>\n<h3>Updating the heading only on Submit</h3>\n<ul><li>Use two state values: name (updates on each keystroke) and headingText (starts empty, shown in the h1).</li><li>A button onClick handler, handleClick, runs setHeadingText(name), copying the current typed value into the heading at that moment.</li><li>Inside handlers write the variable name plainly: setHeadingText(name). Writing { name } there would create an object instead of the string; console.log both to see the difference.</li></ul>\n<h3>Real form elements and preventDefault</h3>\n<ul><li>Wrapping the input and button in a &lt;form&gt; is the normal HTML pattern, and the button gets type=&quot;submit&quot; to mark its role. (Extra) A button inside a form already defaults to type submit; being explicit helps when a form has several buttons.</li><li>Clicking it makes the browser submit the form (a GET or POST request) which reloads the page and wipes React state, so the heading flashes the new value and then resets.</li><li>Put the handler on the form's onSubmit prop instead of the button's onClick. It receives the event, and calling event.preventDefault() at the end of the handler cancels the page reload.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Pressing Enter inside the input also fires the form's onSubmit, which a bare button onClick would not catch.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Form with controlled input and delayed heading update (reference repo plus value={name}, which the repo omits)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [name, setName] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [headingText, setHeadingText] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    setName(event.target.value);\n  }\n\n  function handleClick(event) {\n    setHeadingText(name);\n    event.preventDefault();\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Hello {headingText}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;form onSubmit={handleClick}&gt;\n        &lt;input\n          onChange={handleChange}\n          type=&quot;text&quot;\n          placeholder=&quot;What's your name?&quot;\n          value={name}\n        /&gt;\n        &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Show the name only after Submit</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The Hello heading, text input and Submit button from the events lecture, plus the live-updating name state.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Typing should not change the heading; clicking Submit (or submitting the form) copies the typed name into the heading without a page refresh.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Give the input onChange={handleChange}; write handleChange(event) and console.log(event.target.value) to see the text on each keystroke.</li><li>Create const [name, setName] = useState(&quot;&quot;) and call setName(event.target.value) inside handleChange.</li><li>Add value={name} to the input so it is a controlled component.</li><li>Add a second state value headingText initialised to an empty string and render it after Hello in the h1 (instead of name).</li><li>Add onClick={handleClick} to the button and make handleClick call setHeadingText(name) (the plain variable, not { name }).</li><li>Wrap the input and button in a &lt;form&gt; and give the button type=&quot;submit&quot;; notice the heading flashes and the page reloads.</li><li>Move the handler to the form: &lt;form onSubmit={handleClick}&gt;, accept event as a parameter and call event.preventDefault() so the page does not reload.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The heading reads Hello until Submit; then it reads Hello followed by the typed name, and the page does not refresh.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [name, setName] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [headingText, setHeadingText] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    setName(event.target.value);   // runs on every keystroke\n  }\n\n  function handleClick(event) {\n    setHeadingText(name);          // copy typed text into the heading\n    event.preventDefault();        // stop the browser's form submit/reload\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Hello {headingText}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;form onSubmit={handleClick}&gt;\n        &lt;input\n          onChange={handleChange}\n          type=&quot;text&quot;\n          placeholder=&quot;What's your name?&quot;\n          value={name}\n        /&gt;\n        &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot;&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>The reference repo's input omits value={name}, so it is uncontrolled; the lecture recommends adding it (added in the snippet above).</li><li>Using { name } in a setter call stores an object, not the text.</li><li>Forgetting preventDefault inside a form handler reloads the page and resets all state.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Setting value without onChange freezes the input and React logs a warning.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l311", "<p class=\"summary\">Compares the older class-based way of writing React components, which was needed for state, with function components plus hooks. Uses the same counter built both ways so legacy class code is recognisable, while recommending hooks for new code.</p>\n<h3>Class components: structure</h3>\n<ul><li>Write class App extends React.Component instead of function App(). The class is not called with parentheses and must extend Component from the react module.</li><li>The JSX to display goes in a render() method that returns it. Output is identical to the function version.</li><li>A class component is used in JSX exactly like a function component, e.g. &lt;ClassComponent /&gt;.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference repo App itself is a class whose render() returns &lt;ClassComponent /&gt;; swapping it to &lt;FunctionalComponent /&gt; shows the identical counter.</li></ul>\n<h3>State in classes</h3>\n<ul><li>Historically state required a class: the constructor calls super() and sets this.state = { count: 0 }.</li><li>Read state through this.state.count in render().</li><li>Update with the inherited this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 }), called from the increase() method. (Extra) Class setState merges the given keys into the existing state object, unlike the useState setter, which replaces the value.</li><li>Handler methods are bound in the constructor (this.increase = this.increase.bind(this)); this binding is part of the boilerplate. (Extra) Without it, this is undefined inside increase when the button calls it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why hooks replaced them</h3>\n<ul><li>Class code carries extra boilerplate, repeated this references and binding, and reusing stateful logic between components is awkward.</li><li>The React team introduced hooks (2018) and recommends them for new code. Hooks work only in function components, not classes, but classes and function components can coexist in one app.</li><li>With useState the counter becomes: const [count, setCount] = useState(0); the handler calls setCount(count + 1); the JSX uses count directly. No this, no binding, less code.</li><li>Takeaway: you may meet this.state and this.setState in older tutorials and codebases; they achieve what useState does.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Counter as a class component</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nclass ClassComponent extends React.Component {\n  constructor() {\n    super();\n    this.state = { count: 0 };\n    this.increase = this.increase.bind(this);\n  }\n\n  increase() {\n    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\n  }\n\n  render() {\n    return (\n      &lt;div&gt;\n        &lt;h1&gt;{this.state.count}&lt;/h1&gt;\n        &lt;button onClick={this.increase}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    );\n  }\n}\n\nexport default ClassComponent;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Same counter with a function component and useState</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction FunctionalComponent() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n\n  function increase() {\n    setCount(count + 1);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{count}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={increase}&gt;+&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default FunctionalComponent;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to bind class methods so this is undefined inside the handler.</li><li>Trying to call useState inside a class component; hooks are function-only.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Directly mutating this.state instead of calling setState does not trigger a re-render.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l312", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds a first-name / last-name greeting form, first with two separate state variables and handlers, then refactors to one object in state. Explains why setting only one key wipes the other, how to fix it with the functional updater form using the previous value, and tidies the handler with destructuring.</p>\n<h3>Version 1: one state variable per input</h3>\n<ul><li>Create fName and lName with separate useState(&quot;&quot;) calls (initial empty strings) and show both after Hello in the h1.</li><li>Each input gets value={...} (controlled) and its own onChange handler that reads event.target.value and calls the matching setter.</li><li>It works and is good practice, but needs duplicated functions and consts for data that really belongs together, such as a contact's names.</li></ul>\n<h3>Version 2: an object in state</h3>\n<ul><li>useState can hold an object: const [fullName, setFullName] = useState({ fName: &quot;&quot;, lName: &quot;&quot; }).</li><li>Read values as fullName.fName and fullName.lName in the h1 and in the inputs' value props.</li><li>Both inputs share one handleChange function. To know which input fired, give every input a name attribute (&quot;fName&quot;, &quot;lName&quot;) and read event.target.name. The names must match the object keys.</li><li>While debugging it can help to temporarily remove the value props. With value fixed to state that never changes, a controlled input shows nothing typed. Restore them afterwards.</li></ul>\n<h3>The bug: replacing instead of merging</h3>\n<ul><li>Calling setFullName({ fName: newValue }) replaces the whole state object. Typing in the last-name field then drops fName, and vice versa.</li><li>The useState setter does not merge keys, so the complete new object has to be supplied. (Extra) This differs from class components, whose this.setState merges.</li><li>The React DevTools (Components tab, hooks section; works better in a popped-out window than inside CodeSandbox) show the object losing a key on each keystroke.</li></ul>\n<h3>The fix: functional update with previous value</h3>\n<ul><li>Pass a function to the setter instead of a value: setFullName(prevValue =&gt; { ... }). React calls it with the latest stored state.</li><li>Return a new object that keeps the other key from prevValue and takes the new one: if name is fName return { fName: value, lName: prevValue.lName }; else if name is lName return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: value }.</li><li>React remembers state across re-renders, which is what makes prevValue available.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A compact equivalent is return { ...prevValue, [name]: value }, using the spread operator and computed key, which the next lectures cover.</li></ul>\n<h3>Destructure the event, and never read it inside the updater</h3>\n<ul><li>Replace the two consts with const { value, name } = event.target; at the top of handleChange.</li><li>Do not touch event.target inside the updater function. React gives handlers a synthetic event wrapper, and by the time the updater runs the event data may have been released, causing a warning in the console about synthetic events being reused.</li><li>Always extract what you need from the event first, then use those plain variables inside the setter. (Extra) Newer React versions (17+) no longer pool events, but extracting first remains the safest habit.</li><li>After the refactor the handler only needs the two variables name and value; the earlier intermediate consts (newValue, inputName) can be deleted.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Version 1: separate state per input (the approach later replaced)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [fName, setFName] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [lName, setLName] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n\n  function updateFName(event) {\n    setFName(event.target.value);\n  }\n  function updateLName(event) {\n    setLName(event.target.value);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;Hello {fName} {lName}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;form&gt;\n        &lt;input onChange={updateFName} value={fName} placeholder=&quot;First Name&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;input onChange={updateLName} value={lName} placeholder=&quot;Last Name&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;button&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Object state with functional update (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [fullName, setFullName] = useState({\n    fName: &quot;&quot;,\n    lName: &quot;&quot;,\n  });\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    const { value, name } = event.target;\n    setFullName((prevValue) =&gt; {\n      if (name === &quot;fName&quot;) {\n        return { fName: value, lName: prevValue.lName };\n      } else if (name === &quot;lName&quot;) {\n        return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: value };\n      }\n    });\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;\n        Hello {fullName.fName} {fullName.lName}\n      &lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;form&gt;\n        &lt;input name=&quot;fName&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={fullName.fName} placeholder=&quot;First Name&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;input name=&quot;lName&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={fullName.lName} placeholder=&quot;Last Name&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;button&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The buggy version that loses the other key</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">if (name === &quot;fName&quot;) {\n  setFullName({ fName: value });   // lName is gone now\n} else if (name === &quot;lName&quot;) {\n  setFullName({ lName: value });   // fName is gone now\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Live greeting from first and last name</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> CodeSandbox starter with an h1 and a form containing two inputs (first name, last name) and a Submit button.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Typing a first name shows it in the heading after Hello; typing a last name adds to it without erasing the first name, and vice versa.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Try it first with separate state variables and handlers for each input.</li><li>Refactor to a single fullName object state with fName and lName keys.</li><li>Add name attributes to the inputs matching the keys and point both onChange props at handleChange.</li><li>Make the inputs controlled with value={fullName.fName} / value={fullName.lName}.</li><li>Inside handleChange destructure value and name from event.target.</li><li>Call setFullName with a function that returns a new object using prevValue for the untouched key.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The heading updates live for both names and neither field clears the other.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function handleChange(event) {\n  const { value, name } = event.target;\n  setFullName((prevValue) =&gt; {\n    if (name === &quot;fName&quot;) {\n      return { fName: value, lName: prevValue.lName };\n    } else if (name === &quot;lName&quot;) {\n      return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: value };\n    }\n  });\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Setting only the changed key replaces the entire state object, losing the others.</li><li>Input name attributes that do not exactly match the comparison strings (or object keys) leave the handler returning undefined.</li><li>Reading event.target inside the updater function can trigger the synthetic-event warning; destructure first.</li><li>Controlled inputs with value tied to unchanged state appear frozen while debugging.</li><li>The reference solution keeps the old two-variable version commented out; it is not needed.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l313", "<p class=\"summary\">A challenge and walkthrough: finish a form component whose state is an object with three fields (fName, lName, email) so each input updates only its own field. The long if/else-if version of the updater is built deliberately, to be shortened in the next lecture with the spread operator.</p>\n<h3>The challenge setup</h3>\n<ul><li>App.jsx is provided with state renamed to <code>contact</code> and its setter <code>setContact</code>; the initial value is an object with fName, lName and email, all empty strings.</li><li>Three inputs and a Submit button sit inside a form. Typing in the first two inputs should fill the h1 greeting; typing in the third should fill a new paragraph beneath the h1.</li><li>Concepts to reuse from earlier lessons: object state, updating from the previous state, controlled form inputs, and event handling.</li><li>Use the React Developer Tools Components tab to watch the state object change while typing; it is the quickest way to spot a field being wiped or not updating.</li></ul>\n<h3>Wiring up the change handler</h3>\n<ul><li>Give every input the same <code>onChange={handleChange}</code>. One shared function serves all three inputs. (Tip from the lecture: in VS Code, Option-click places several cursors so the identical attribute is typed once.)</li><li>handleChange receives the synthetic event. Destructure only what is needed: <code>const { name, value } = event.target;</code>. The <code>name</code> attribute on each input (fName, lName, email) tells the handler which field fired; <code>value</code> is the text currently typed.</li><li>Each input needs a <code>name</code> attribute matching the corresponding key in the state object; the comparison in the handler depends on that.</li></ul>\n<h3>Updating only the changed field</h3>\n<ul><li>Call <code>setContact</code> with a callback that receives the previous state (named prevValue in the solution). Logging the argument is a good way to confirm its shape if unsure.</li><li>Inside the callback, branch on <code>name</code>: if it is fName, return a full new object with the new <code>value</code> for fName while copying lName and email from prevValue; likewise for lName and for email.</li><li>Every branch must return a complete object with all three keys. If a key is omitted, that field disappears from state, because the useState setter replaces the whole value rather than merging object properties.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> This differs from class components, where this.setState shallow-merges the object you pass into the existing state.</li><li>This works but repeats near-identical code in three places, which is the motivation for the spread-operator version in the next lecture.</li></ul>\n<h3>Controlled inputs and display</h3>\n<ul><li>Add a <code>value</code> attribute to each input bound to the state: <code>contact.fName</code>, <code>contact.lName</code>, <code>contact.email</code>. This makes the inputs controlled: React state is the single source of truth, and what appears in the field is always what is stored.</li><li>The h1 renders <code>Hello {contact.fName} {contact.lName}</code> and the paragraph renders <code>{contact.email}</code> as JSX expressions that update on every re-render triggered by setContact.</li><li>Verify in DevTools: expanding the App component's hooks shows the contact object; typing in one input changes just that key.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Handler with explicit branches (from the reference solution, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">const [contact, setContact] = useState({ fName: &quot;&quot;, lName: &quot;&quot;, email: &quot;&quot; });\n\nfunction handleChange(event) {\n  const { name, value } = event.target;\n  setContact((prevValue) =&gt; {\n    if (name === &quot;fName&quot;) {\n      return { fName: value, lName: prevValue.lName, email: prevValue.email };\n    } else if (name === &quot;lName&quot;) {\n      return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: value, email: prevValue.email };\n    } else if (name === &quot;email&quot;) {\n      return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: prevValue.lName, email: value };\n    }\n  });\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Markup: shared handler, name and value on each input</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">&lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;h1&gt;Hello {contact.fName} {contact.lName}&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;p&gt;{contact.email}&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;form&gt;\n    &lt;input name=&quot;fName&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={contact.fName} placeholder=&quot;First Name&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;input name=&quot;lName&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={contact.lName} placeholder=&quot;Last Name&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;input name=&quot;email&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={contact.email} placeholder=&quot;Email&quot; /&gt;\n    &lt;button&gt;Submit&lt;/button&gt;\n  &lt;/form&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Changing Complex State: contact form</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> App.jsx with a <code>contact</code> object state (fName, lName, email), setContact, and a form with three inputs plus an h1 and a paragraph that are not yet connected.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Typing in each input updates only its own property in state and shows the first and last name in the h1 and the email in the paragraph beneath it.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add <code>onChange={handleChange}</code> to all three inputs and define handleChange.</li><li>Destructure <code>name</code> and <code>value</code> from <code>event.target</code> inside the handler.</li><li>Call setContact with a function of the previous state.</li><li>Branch on the input name and return a complete new object that replaces just the changed field and copies the other two from the previous state.</li><li>Add <code>value</code> attributes (contact.fName, contact.lName, contact.email) so the inputs are controlled.</li><li>Render contact.fName and contact.lName in the h1 and contact.email in the paragraph.</li><li>Check the result with React DevTools.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each input can be typed in independently; the greeting and email text update live and no field is cleared when another changes.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function handleChange(event) {\n  const { name, value } = event.target;\n  setContact((prevValue) =&gt; {\n    if (name === &quot;fName&quot;) {\n      return { fName: value, lName: prevValue.lName, email: prevValue.email };\n    } else if (name === &quot;lName&quot;) {\n      return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: value, email: prevValue.email };\n    } else if (name === &quot;email&quot;) {\n      return { fName: prevValue.fName, lName: prevValue.lName, email: value };\n    }\n  });\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Omitting a key in a returned object removes it from state because state is replaced, not merged.</li><li>Forgetting the <code>name</code> attribute on an input, or a name that does not match the state key and the if-branch string.</li><li>Forgetting the <code>value</code> attribute leaves the input uncontrolled and out of sync with state.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The final else-if has no plain else, so an unexpected name would make the updater return undefined and wipe state.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reading event.target inside the updater callback can fail in older React versions due to event pooling; destructure first, as done here.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l314", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces the ES6 spread operator (...) for arrays and objects, then uses it together with computed property names to collapse the long if/else state updater from the previous challenge into a few lines.</p>\n<h3>Spread with arrays</h3>\n<ul><li><code>...arr</code> written inside an array literal expands the array into its individual elements at that position. It replaces looping and pushing each item.</li><li>Position matters: <code>[...citrus, &quot;Apple&quot;]</code> puts the citrus items first, <code>[&quot;Apple&quot;, ...citrus, &quot;Banana&quot;]</code> inserts them between Apple and Banana, and putting it last appends them.</li><li>The spread creates a new array; the source array is not modified.</li><li>Demo: citrus = Lime, Lemon, Orange spread into a fruits array of Apple, Banana, Coconut; logging fruits shows a single flat array.</li></ul>\n<h3>Spread with objects</h3>\n<ul><li><code>...obj</code> inside an object literal copies all of that object's key-value pairs into the new object, alongside any other properties written next to it.</li><li>Example: spreading a fullName object (fName, lName) into a user object with id and username yields one flat object with four keys.</li><li>Contrast with listing the object by name (<code>{ fullName, id }</code>): that nests it under a <code>fullName</code> key instead of merging its keys.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If the same key appears twice, the later one wins, so put overrides after the spread. This is why the state updater puts the new field last.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The spread is shallow: nested objects are copied by reference.</li></ul>\n<h3>Applying it to the handleChange updater</h3>\n<ul><li>All the if/else-if branches can be deleted. Return a new object that spreads the previous state and then adds the changed field.</li><li>Everything unchanged is carried over by <code>...prevValue</code>; the single trailing key-value pair overwrites just the field that changed.</li><li>First attempt <code>{ ...prevValue, name: value }</code> does not work: JavaScript treats <code>name</code> as the literal key &quot;name&quot;, so state gains an unwanted <code>name</code> property and the real field never updates. The destructured <code>name</code> variable is flagged as unused.</li><li>Using <code>event.target.name</code> as the key also fails, since any text before the colon in an object literal is read as a string key, not an expression.</li><li>Fix: wrap the key in square brackets, <code>[name]: value</code>. This computed property name syntax evaluates the expression and uses its result as the key. Before ES6 the equivalent was creating an empty object and assigning <code>obj[key] = value</code>.</li><li>It is available here because Babel (via React's tooling) handles modern syntax.</li></ul>\n<h3>Going shorter, and why not to</h3>\n<ul><li>With only one returned object the <code>return</code> and outer braces can be dropped, but the object literal must be wrapped in parentheses: <code>(prev) =&gt; ({ ...prev, [name]: value })</code>. Otherwise the braces are parsed as a function body.</li><li>That makes the updater two lines, but the instructor prefers the explicit return version because stacked braces and parentheses are harder to read. Rule: shorten code only while the meaning stays clear.</li><li>Practice recommendation: experiment with it before the next challenge.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Spread in arrays</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const citrus = [&quot;Lime&quot;, &quot;Lemon&quot;, &quot;Orange&quot;];\nconst fruits = [&quot;Apple&quot;, ...citrus, &quot;Banana&quot;, &quot;Coconut&quot;];\nconsole.log(fruits); // [&quot;Apple&quot;,&quot;Lime&quot;,&quot;Lemon&quot;,&quot;Orange&quot;,&quot;Banana&quot;,&quot;Coconut&quot;]</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Spread in objects versus nesting</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const fullName = { fName: &quot;James&quot;, lName: &quot;Bond&quot; };\nconst merged = { ...fullName, id: 1, username: &quot;jamesbond007&quot; }; // flat: fName, lName, id, username\nconst nested = { fullName, id: 1, username: &quot;jamesbond007&quot; };    // fullName key holds an object</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Final handleChange using spread and a computed key (reference repo)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function handleChange(event) {\n  const { name, value } = event.target;\n  setContact((prevValue) =&gt; {\n    return {\n      ...prevValue,\n      [name]: value,\n    };\n  });\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Terse form (works, less readable)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">setContact((prevValue) =&gt; ({ ...prevValue, [name]: value }));</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Writing <code>name: value</code> instead of <code>[name]: value</code> creates a literal key called name.</li><li>Forgetting parentheses around an object returned from an arrow function with an implicit return.</li><li>Placing the spread after the new field, which makes the old value overwrite the new one.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Spread copies are shallow, so nested objects still share references.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l315", "<p class=\"summary\">Challenge to build a to-do list in React: track the input text in state, append it to an items array with the spread operator when Add is clicked, render the array as list items with map, and clear the input afterwards. The walkthrough builds it in four stages.</p>\n<h3>The challenge</h3>\n<ul><li>Final behaviour: type an item such as 'Buy milk', click Add, and it appears as a bullet in a list. Multiple items accumulate.</li><li>Three requirements in the starter's comment: (1) save the input's text in state as it changes, (2) on Add, push the current text into an array, (3) render the array as <code>li</code> elements inside the <code>ul</code>.</li><li>It combines everything so far: useState, controlled inputs, events, map, and the spread operator from the previous lesson.</li></ul>\n<h3>Stage 1: track the input text</h3>\n<ul><li>Declare <code>const [inputText, setInputText] = useState(&quot;&quot;)</code>. The initial value is an empty string because the field starts blank. Remember to import useState.</li><li>Add <code>value={inputText}</code> to the input so it is controlled and always mirrors state.</li><li>Add <code>onChange={handleChange}</code>. With only one input there is no need to read <code>name</code> or destructure; <code>const newValue = event.target.value; setInputText(newValue);</code> is enough.</li><li>The previous state is not needed here because the new value replaces the old one outright (the whole text is simply what the user has typed).</li><li>Check with React DevTools: the state starts as an empty string and updates with each keystroke.</li></ul>\n<h3>Stage 2: items array and rendering</h3>\n<ul><li>Create a second state: <code>const [items, setItems] = useState([])</code>, initially an empty array, so no bullets show at first.</li><li>Inside the <code>ul</code>, use curly braces to embed a JavaScript expression that maps over items: for each <code>todoItem</code> return <code>&lt;li&gt;{todoItem}&lt;/li&gt;</code>. A singular name for the callback parameter keeps array and element distinct.</li><li>If the map expression is not wrapped in braces inside JSX, the editor shows an 'unexpected token' because it is read as text, not code.</li><li>Because the arrow function returns a single expression, <code>return</code> and its braces can be dropped, leaving an implicit-return arrow with parentheses around the JSX.</li></ul>\n<h3>Stage 3: Add button and spread</h3>\n<ul><li>Attach <code>onClick={addItem}</code> to the button. An inline arrow would also work, but a named function is better for reuse.</li><li>Inside addItem call <code>setItems</code> with a function of the previous items and return a new array: <code>[...prevItems, inputText]</code>. The spread copies existing items and the new text goes at the end. State is never mutated with push.</li><li>The explicit return form is kept because it reads better and is short anyway.</li><li>DevTools check: two hooks, a string and an array; after clicking Add the array has the new item at index 0, and the state change re-renders the map output, adding an <code>li</code>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Stage 4: clear the input</h3>\n<ul><li>After updating items, call <code>setInputText(&quot;&quot;)</code> in addItem. Since the input is controlled by inputText, this empties the field ready for the next item.</li><li>Next lesson preview: split the app into separate components and pass state between parent and child.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Complete to-do list component (reference repo, trimmed)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [inputText, setInputText] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    const newValue = event.target.value;\n    setInputText(newValue);\n  }\n\n  function addItem() {\n    setItems((prevItems) =&gt; {\n      return [...prevItems, inputText];\n    });\n    setInputText(&quot;&quot;);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;&lt;h1&gt;To-Do List&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;form&quot;&gt;\n        &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={inputText} /&gt;\n        &lt;button onClick={addItem}&gt;&lt;span&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;div&gt;\n        &lt;ul&gt;\n          {items.map((todoItem) =&gt; (\n            &lt;li&gt;{todoItem}&lt;/li&gt;\n          ))}\n        &lt;/ul&gt;\n      &lt;/div&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: To-do list with state and spread</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter project (fork) with App.jsx containing the To-Do List markup (input, Add button, empty ul) and comment instructions; styles supplied.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Typing and clicking Add appends items to a list that renders as bullets, with the input cleared after each add.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create <code>inputText</code> state with initial empty string and bind it to the input via <code>value</code>.</li><li>Add an onChange handler that stores event.target.value in state.</li><li>Create <code>items</code> state with initial empty array.</li><li>Map items to <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> elements inside the ul.</li><li>Add an onClick handler to the Add button that sets items to a new array of the previous items plus the current inputText.</li><li>Reset inputText to an empty string after adding.</li><li>Inspect both hooks in React DevTools to verify.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each Add click adds one bullet containing the typed text and empties the input.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function addItem() {\n  setItems((prevItems) =&gt; {\n    return [...prevItems, inputText];\n  });\n  setInputText(&quot;&quot;);\n}\n\n// in JSX\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  {items.map((todoItem) =&gt; (\n    &lt;li&gt;{todoItem}&lt;/li&gt;\n  ))}\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Using items.push and then calling setItems with the same array mutates state; React compares by reference, so it may not re-render. Always return a new array (spread, concat).</li><li>Forgetting braces around the map expression in JSX.</li><li>Forgetting to import useState.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference solution omits a <code>key</code> prop on each li, which React warns about; use a key (ideally a stable id, index as a last resort).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference lets empty strings be added; guard against blank input if desired.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l316", "<p class=\"summary\">Splits the to-do list into a ToDoItem child component, shows that props are read-only, gives the child its own state to strike text through on click, then replaces that with a pattern where the parent passes a callback prop so a child can delete itself from the parent's array using an index id and filter.</p>\n<h3>Extracting ToDoItem</h3>\n<ul><li>Real apps are divided into components (heading, input area, each list row). Here the <code>li</code> is pulled out into <code>components/ToDoItem.jsx</code>.</li><li>The component file imports React, defines a function named after the file, returns an <code>li</code> and exports it as default.</li><li>In App.jsx, import it (<code>import ToDoItem from &quot;./components/ToDoItem&quot;</code>) and inside items.map render <code>&lt;ToDoItem ... /&gt;</code> as a self-closing tag instead of the raw <code>li</code>.</li><li>Each rendered item receives its text via a prop (the lecture names it <code>text</code>), and the child reads it as <code>props.text</code> inside curly braces. Behaviour is identical to before; only the structure changed.</li><li>At this stage ToDoItem is stateless: it just displays read-only props.</li></ul>\n<h3>Props are read-only</h3>\n<ul><li>Props cannot be reassigned by the child. An input inside ToDoItem whose onChange tries <code>props = &quot;new Thing&quot;</code> does nothing visible.</li><li>A child that needs to change over time uses its own state via useState instead.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge A: strike-through with local state</h3>\n<ul><li>Task: clicking an item crosses it out; clicking again uncrosses it. Needs inline styling, a ternary, event handling and state.</li><li>Step 1: confirm inline style works with a style object, <code>style={{ textDecoration: &quot;line-through&quot; }}</code>. The property name is camelCase and the value a string; the outer braces are JSX expression, the inner are the object.</li><li>Step 2: add <code>onClick={handleClick}</code> to the div (the li would work too).</li><li>Step 3: <code>const [isDone, setIsDone] = useState(false)</code>; items start not done. In handleClick call <code>setIsDone((prevValue) =&gt; !prevValue)</code>; negating is simpler than if/else.</li><li>Step 4: <code>textDecoration: isDone ? &quot;line-through&quot; : &quot;none&quot;</code>.</li><li>Limitation: this state is local to one item. The parent cannot know about it, which leads to the next problem.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge B: child deletes itself from the parent's array</h3>\n<ul><li>The goal is to remove the clicked item from the App's items array (with Buy milk, Buy eggs, Buy bacon, clicking eggs removes only eggs). The strike-through state, handleClick and inline style are removed first.</li><li>Key idea: parents can pass functions as props. In App, define <code>deleteItem</code> and pass it as a prop (named <code>onChecked</code>) on each ToDoItem. The child triggers it with <code>props.onChecked</code>, which runs the function that lives in App.</li><li>To identify which item was clicked, pass an <code>id</code>. The map callback gets a second argument, the element's index, so use <code>items.map((todoItem, index) =&gt; ...)</code>. With two parameters the arrow function needs parentheses.</li><li>Each mapped component needs a <code>key</code>; the lecture uses the index for both key and id, noting that React advises against index keys in general but that it is acceptable for this example. (Extra) In real apps give each item a stable unique id, e.g. generated with crypto.randomUUID() or the uuid package, and use it for both key and id.</li><li>In React DevTools the ToDoItem props show id, text and onChecked (the function).</li><li>Gotcha: writing <code>onClick={props.onChecked(props.id)}</code> calls the function immediately on render and logs for every item. Instead wrap it: <code>onClick={() =&gt; { props.onChecked(props.id); }}</code> so it only runs on click.</li><li>deleteItem(id) calls <code>setItems</code> with the previous array filtered: <code>prevItems.filter((item, index) =&gt; index !== id)</code>. filter's callback receives the element and its index; returning true keeps the element, so everything except the clicked index remains.</li><li>Verification: add three items, click the second; its id 1 is sent to deleteItem, and the filter keeps indexes 0 and 2.</li></ul>\n<h3>Closing advice</h3>\n<ul><li>This nests functions inside functions, typical of functional style in React; the instruction is to experiment (different filter conditions, array of objects instead of strings) rather than memorise.</li><li>Data flows down through props; events flow up by calling callbacks the parent passed down.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">First step: stateless ToDoItem that only displays its text prop</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// components/ToDoItem.jsx\nimport React from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction ToDoItem(props) {\n  return &lt;li&gt;{props.text}&lt;/li&gt;;\n}\n\nexport default ToDoItem;\n\n// App.jsx\nimport ToDoItem from &quot;./components/ToDoItem&quot;;\n// ...inside the return\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  {items.map((todoItem) =&gt; (\n    &lt;ToDoItem text={todoItem} /&gt;\n  ))}\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">ToDoItem.jsx: calls the parent's callback with its id on click</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">export default function ToDoItem(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div onClick={() =&gt; { props.onChecked(props.id); }}&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{props.text}&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx: delete by index and render children with id and callback</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function deleteItem(id) {\n  setItems((prevItems) =&gt; {\n    return prevItems.filter((item, index) =&gt; {\n      return index !== id;\n    });\n  });\n}\n\n&lt;ul&gt;\n  {items.map((todoItem, index) =&gt; (\n    &lt;ToDoItem key={index} id={index} onChecked={deleteItem} text={todoItem} /&gt;\n  ))}\n&lt;/ul&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Local state version (Challenge A) inside ToDoItem</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction ToDoItem(props) {\n  const [isDone, setIsDone] = useState(false);\n  function handleClick() {\n    setIsDone((prevValue) =&gt; !prevValue);\n  }\n  return (\n    &lt;div onClick={handleClick}&gt;\n      &lt;li style={{ textDecoration: isDone ? &quot;line-through&quot; : &quot;none&quot; }}&gt;{props.text}&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\nexport default ToDoItem;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Component tree: ToDoItem extraction, strike-through, then delete</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The to-do list from the previous lesson (single App component) in a forked sandbox with an empty components folder.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Move the li into a ToDoItem component, then (a) make clicking strike through a todo with its own state and finally (b) make clicking remove it from the parent's items array.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Create components/ToDoItem.jsx that returns an li showing props.text and exports it by default.</li><li>Import it in App.jsx and render it inside items.map, passing the item string as a prop.</li><li>Challenge A: add isDone state (false), a click handler that toggles it, and a ternary on the inline textDecoration style.</li><li>Remove the Challenge A code.</li><li>Define deleteItem(id) in App and pass it as an onChecked prop, along with id from the map's index parameter and a key.</li><li>In ToDoItem call props.onChecked(props.id) inside an arrow function on click so it does not run during render.</li><li>In deleteItem use setItems with prevItems.filter to drop the element whose index equals id.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Adding several items and clicking one removes just that one from the list; with Challenge A active, a click toggles a line through the text.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx (key parts)\nfunction deleteItem(id) {\n  setItems((prevItems) =&gt; prevItems.filter((item, index) =&gt; index !== id));\n}\n{items.map((todoItem, index) =&gt; (\n  &lt;ToDoItem key={index} id={index} onChecked={deleteItem} text={todoItem} /&gt;\n))}\n\n// ToDoItem.jsx\nexport default function ToDoItem(props) {\n  return (\n    &lt;div onClick={() =&gt; { props.onChecked(props.id); }}&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;{props.text}&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling the callback in the attribute (<code>onClick={props.onChecked(props.id)}</code>) runs it at render time instead of on click.</li><li>Trying to assign to props; they are read-only.</li><li>Using the array index as a key breaks down if items can be reordered or removed (ids shift); use unique ids when possible.</li><li>Forgetting parentheses around two arrow-function parameters in map.</li><li>Reference repo quirks: it imports from &quot;./components/todoItem&quot; (lowercase t, which fails on case-sensitive file systems when the file is ToDoItem.jsx), names the prop <code>todoItem</code> instead of <code>text</code>, and has no key; the note's code corrects these. It also contains only the final delete version, not Challenge A.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Wrapping each li in a div (as the lecture and reference do, to hang the onClick on) produces a div as a direct child of ul, which is invalid HTML. Putting onClick on the li itself avoids this.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l317", "<p class=\"summary\">Challenge and walkthrough: the to-do list's input and Add button have been extracted into an InputArea component and the app is broken. The fix keeps the typed text in InputArea's own state while the items array stays in App, and passes the text up through an onAdd callback prop.</p>\n<h3>The starting situation</h3>\n<ul><li>The starter has InputArea (input plus button only) already rendered inside App, but the old handlers and state were left in App, so the compiler shows many errors.</li><li>Goal: behave exactly as before (add items, click to delete) with the new component tree: App at the top, one InputArea, and many ToDoItem children.</li><li>Rules in the starter comments: do not edit ToDoItem.jsx and do not move the input/button back into App.jsx.</li><li>Hints: decide where the input's state should live, and how the typed value gets back to addItem in App.</li></ul>\n<h3>Deciding where state lives</h3>\n<ul><li>The raw text in the input matters only to InputArea until Add is pressed, so <code>inputText</code> state and <code>handleChange</code> move into InputArea. Local state keeps App simpler.</li><li>The items array stays in App: both InputArea (to add) and the ToDoItems (to display and delete) need to influence it, so it belongs at the common ancestor.</li><li>When code is copied between files by hand, the editor will not add the <code>useState</code> import automatically; add <code>import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;</code> yourself or the error 'useState is not defined' appears.</li></ul>\n<h3>Passing the text up with a callback prop</h3>\n<ul><li>In App.jsx, render <code>&lt;InputArea onAdd={addItem} /&gt;</code>: the prop name is arbitrary, the value is the addItem function.</li><li>InputArea receives <code>props</code> and calls <code>props.onAdd(...)</code> on button click. If confused about which props arrive, inspect the component in React DevTools.</li><li>addItem now takes a parameter: <code>function addItem(inputText) { setItems((prev) =&gt; [...prev, inputText]); }</code>, so it is told which text to add rather than reading state from App.</li><li>The click handler in InputArea must be an arrow function wrapping the call so the text can be passed as an argument: <code>onClick={() =&gt; { props.onAdd(inputText); setInputText(&quot;&quot;); }}</code>. Passing <code>props.onAdd(inputText)</code> directly would run at render.</li><li>Clearing the field moves here too: after calling onAdd, <code>setInputText(&quot;&quot;)</code> resets the local state, since the value has already been handed up.</li><li>Data flow summary: InputArea owns the draft text, App owns the list, the callback sends data up, and the list is passed back down to ToDoItem children via props.</li></ul>\n<h3>Retained from previous lesson</h3>\n<ul><li>deleteItem(id) with filter and the ToDoItem calling <code>props.onChecked(props.id)</code> are unchanged.</li><li>Next: using these ideas in the third iteration of the Keeper app.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">InputArea.jsx (reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction InputArea(props) {\n  const [inputText, setInputText] = useState(&quot;&quot;);\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    const newValue = event.target.value;\n    setInputText(newValue);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;form&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;input onChange={handleChange} type=&quot;text&quot; value={inputText} /&gt;\n      &lt;button\n        onClick={() =&gt; {\n          props.onAdd(inputText);\n          setInputText(&quot;&quot;);\n        }}\n      &gt;\n        &lt;span&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default InputArea;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx (reference solution, challenge comments removed): items-only state and addItem with a parameter</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import React, { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport ToDoItem from &quot;./components/ToDoItem&quot;;\nimport InputArea from &quot;./components/InputArea&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\n\n  function addItem(inputText) {\n    setItems((prevItems) =&gt; [...prevItems, inputText]);\n  }\n\n  function deleteItem(id) {\n    setItems((prevItems) =&gt; prevItems.filter((item, index) =&gt; index !== id));\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;container&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;div className=&quot;heading&quot;&gt;&lt;h1&gt;To-Do List&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/div&gt;\n      &lt;InputArea onAdd={addItem} /&gt;\n      &lt;ul&gt;\n        {items.map((todoItem, index) =&gt; (\n          &lt;ToDoItem key={index} id={index} text={todoItem} onChecked={deleteItem} /&gt;\n        ))}\n      &lt;/ul&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Extracted InputArea component</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> App.jsx renders <code>&lt;InputArea /&gt;</code> but still has the old inputText state and handlers (broken); InputArea.jsx has the input and button markup only; ToDoItem.jsx is finished and must not change.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Make the app work as before, with the input state in InputArea and the items array in App.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Move <code>inputText</code> state, its setter and handleChange from App into InputArea, importing useState.</li><li>Bind the input's value to inputText and onChange to handleChange.</li><li>Pass <code>onAdd={addItem}</code> from App to InputArea.</li><li>Change addItem in App to accept the new text as an argument and append it with the spread operator.</li><li>In InputArea make the button's onClick an arrow function that calls props.onAdd(inputText) and then setInputText(&quot;&quot;).</li><li>Remove unused state from App and test: add several items, then delete one by clicking it.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Typing then clicking Add inserts a new ToDoItem and empties the input; clicking an item removes it.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// InputArea button\n&lt;button onClick={() =&gt; { props.onAdd(inputText); setInputText(&quot;&quot;); }}&gt;\n  &lt;span&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;\n&lt;/button&gt;\n\n// App.jsx\n&lt;InputArea onAdd={addItem} /&gt;\nfunction addItem(inputText) {\n  setItems((prevItems) =&gt; [...prevItems, inputText]);\n}</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Missing useState import after moving code by copy and paste.</li><li>Passing <code>props.onAdd(inputText)</code> directly as onClick, which executes during render.</li><li>Leaving the clear-input call in App, where it can no longer reach InputArea's state.</li><li>Not giving addItem a parameter, so it reads a variable that no longer exists in App.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's App.jsx already contains the completed solution along with the challenge comments; the starter state is not preserved there.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l318", "<p class=\"summary\">Final functional step of the Keeper app: users can add notes (title and content captured in state, passed up to App, stored in an array and rendered with map) and delete them (a callback prop plus filter by id). The lecture first splits the challenge into sub-steps, then walks through the full solution.</p>\n<h3>Challenge breakdown</h3>\n<ul><li>Two features: (1) add a note and (2) delete a note. On Add, the inputs should clear and the new note appears next to existing ones; on DELETE, that note disappears.</li><li>Add feature sub-steps: keep title and content in state inside CreateArea; pass the finished note up to App; store notes in an array held in App state; render one Note component per array item.</li><li>Delete feature sub-steps: Note triggers a callback prop; the delete function lives in App and uses filter(); each Note receives an id that it hands back when deleting.</li><li>Everything needed was taught earlier (state, props, events, spread, map, filter), so the lecture is mostly about combining them.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1: track title and content in CreateArea</h3>\n<ul><li>Use a single state object instead of two separate constants: const [note, setNote] = useState({ title: '', content: '' }). One object means fewer lines and one setter.</li><li>Make the inputs controlled: value={note.title} on the input and value={note.content} on the textarea, so the displayed text always comes from state.</li><li>Give both elements an onChange that points to the same handleChange function.</li><li>Give each field a name attribute ('title' and 'content') that matches the keys in the state object. This is what lets one handler serve both fields.</li><li>Inside handleChange, destructure the event first: const { name, value } = event.target, then use name and value inside the setter callback.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Grabbing name/value up front matters most in React 16 and earlier, where synthetic events were pooled and their fields were nulled before a deferred updater ran; since React 17 there is no pooling, but extracting the values first is still the clearer habit.</li><li>Update with the functional form of the setter: setNote(prevNote =&gt; ({ ...prevNote, [name]: value })). The spread copies the existing title and content, and the computed key [name] overwrites only the field being typed in (the brackets turn the variable's value into the key).</li><li>Check with React DevTools: typing in each field should show the CreateArea state object update.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: send the note up to App</h3>\n<ul><li>Add onClick={submitNote} to the Add button.</li><li>A button inside a form submits it by default, which reloads the page (visible as a flash). Call event.preventDefault() inside submitNote to stop that.</li><li>In App, define addNote(newNote) and give it to CreateArea as a prop, here named onAdd: &lt;CreateArea onAdd={addNote} /&gt;. The prop name is arbitrary.</li><li>In CreateArea, props.onAdd(note) invokes App's addNote and passes the current note object as the argument. This child-to-parent pattern works by passing a function down and calling it with data.</li><li>Verify by console.log(newNote) inside addNote before wiring up state.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: store notes in an array and render them</h3>\n<ul><li>In App add const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]) (import useState from react). Start with an empty array so no sample note shows until the user adds one.</li><li>addNote appends with the spread operator: setNotes(prevNotes =&gt; [...prevNotes, newNote]). The callback must return the new array.</li><li>Replace the hardcoded sample Note with {notes.map(...)} (curly braces so JSX evaluates the expression). For each noteItem, return &lt;Note title={noteItem.title} content={noteItem.content} /&gt;.</li><li>Result: each Add click creates a new object in the array and a new Note card on screen.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: delete via callback prop and filter</h3>\n<ul><li>In Note, add onClick={handleClick} on the DELETE button. A named handler is used for readability; an inline arrow would work as well.</li><li>In App create deleteNote(id) and pass it to each Note as onDelete={deleteNote}. DevTools will show it under the Note's props.</li><li>Note's handleClick calls props.onDelete(props.id). Initially a console.log confirms the callback fires.</li><li>deleteNote uses setNotes(prevNotes =&gt; prevNotes.filter((noteItem, index) =&gt; index !== id)). filter keeps items for which the callback returns true, and its second parameter is the item's index, so everything except the matching index survives.</li><li>The id comes from map: its callback also receives (noteItem, index). Pass id={index} to Note and also key={index}. Note then sends props.id back on click.</li><li>Because key and id are just the index, they are not stable identifiers; she treats it as the simplest option and mentions uuid or timestamps as alternatives.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 5: clear the form after adding</h3>\n<ul><li>In submitNote, after props.onAdd(note), call setNote({ title: '', content: '' }) to reset to the initial object.</li><li>Since the input and textarea are controlled by note.title and note.content, they empty out and show their placeholders again.</li></ul>\n<h3>Data flow summary</h3>\n<ul><li>Down: App passes onAdd to CreateArea and title/content/id/onDelete to each Note.</li><li>Up: CreateArea calls onAdd(note); Note calls onDelete(id). State for the list lives in App because both siblings need it; state for the in-progress note lives in CreateArea because only it needs it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> This is 'lifting state up': shared data goes to the nearest common parent, children communicate by calling functions received as props.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> setNotes and setNote updates are asynchronous; reading notes right after calling setNotes still gives the old array. The functional updater (prev =&gt; ...) guarantees each update builds on the latest state.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">App.jsx: notes array state, add/delete handlers, rendering with map</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Header from &quot;./components/Header&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./components/Footer&quot;;\nimport Note from &quot;./components/Note&quot;;\nimport CreateArea from &quot;./components/CreateArea&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]);\n\n  function addNote(newNote) {\n    setNotes((prevNotes) =&gt; [...prevNotes, newNote]);\n  }\n\n  function deleteNote(id) {\n    setNotes((prevNotes) =&gt;\n      prevNotes.filter((noteItem, index) =&gt; index !== id)\n    );\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      &lt;CreateArea onAdd={addNote} /&gt;\n      {notes.map((noteItem, index) =&gt; (\n        &lt;Note\n          key={index}\n          id={index}\n          title={noteItem.title}\n          content={noteItem.content}\n          onDelete={deleteNote}\n        /&gt;\n      ))}\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">CreateArea.jsx: controlled inputs, handleChange, submitNote</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\n\nfunction CreateArea(props) {\n  const [note, setNote] = useState({ title: &quot;&quot;, content: &quot;&quot; });\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    const { name, value } = event.target;\n    setNote((prevNote) =&gt; ({ ...prevNote, [name]: value }));\n  }\n\n  function submitNote(event) {\n    props.onAdd(note);\n    setNote({ title: &quot;&quot;, content: &quot;&quot; });\n    event.preventDefault();\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;form&gt;\n        &lt;input name=&quot;title&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={note.title} placeholder=&quot;Title&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;textarea name=&quot;content&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={note.content} placeholder=&quot;Take a note...&quot; rows=&quot;3&quot; /&gt;\n        &lt;button onClick={submitNote}&gt;Add&lt;/button&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default CreateArea;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Note.jsx: delete callback</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">function Note(props) {\n  function handleClick() {\n    props.onDelete(props.id);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{props.title}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.content}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={handleClick}&gt;DELETE&lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Note;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Keeper App Part 3: add and delete notes</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Keeper starter project (Header, Footer, Note, CreateArea components with styling). App renders one static sample Note; CreateArea has a form with an input, a textarea and an Add button but no logic. Comments in App.jsx list the challenge.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Implement adding notes (state, pass to App, store in array, render each as a Note) and deleting notes (callback with id and filter), matching the demo behaviour.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>In CreateArea, create a note state object {title, content} and bind the input and textarea value to it.</li><li>Add name attributes and a shared handleChange that updates the right key using destructuring, spread and [name].</li><li>Add an Add-button onClick (submitNote) that calls event.preventDefault().</li><li>Add addNote in App and pass it to CreateArea as onAdd; call props.onAdd(note) from submitNote.</li><li>Add notes state (empty array) in App; in addNote append the new note with the spread operator.</li><li>Replace the static Note with notes.map(...) rendering a Note per item with title and content props.</li><li>Add onClick/handleClick to the Note DELETE button that calls props.onDelete(props.id).</li><li>Create deleteNote(id) in App using filter to drop the item whose index equals id, and pass it as onDelete.</li><li>Pass key={index} and id={index} from map to each Note.</li><li>Reset note state to empty strings after a successful add.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Typing a title and text then clicking Add shows a new card and empties the form; clicking DELETE on a card removes only that card; the page never reloads.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// App.jsx (CreateArea.jsx and Note.jsx are in code_examples)\nimport { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport Header from &quot;./components/Header&quot;;\nimport Footer from &quot;./components/Footer&quot;;\nimport Note from &quot;./components/Note&quot;;\nimport CreateArea from &quot;./components/CreateArea&quot;;\n\nfunction App() {\n  const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]);\n\n  function addNote(newNote) {\n    setNotes((prevNotes) =&gt; [...prevNotes, newNote]);\n  }\n\n  function deleteNote(id) {\n    setNotes((prevNotes) =&gt;\n      prevNotes.filter((noteItem, index) =&gt; index !== id)\n    );\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;Header /&gt;\n      &lt;CreateArea onAdd={addNote} /&gt;\n      {notes.map((noteItem, index) =&gt; (\n        &lt;Note\n          key={index}\n          id={index}\n          title={noteItem.title}\n          content={noteItem.content}\n          onDelete={deleteNote}\n        /&gt;\n      ))}\n      &lt;Footer /&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default App;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting event.preventDefault() on the Add button makes the page reload and the state vanish.</li><li>Returning nothing from the setNotes callback (a missing return in a braced arrow) sets state to undefined.</li><li>Forgetting the name attributes on the inputs, so [name] is undefined/empty in handleChange.</li><li>Omitting the key prop on mapped Notes triggers a React warning.</li><li>Forgetting curly braces around the map call in JSX so it prints as text.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Index-based ids misbehave if the list is reordered or filtered elsewhere; for production use a unique id per note.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo calls props.onAdd before preventDefault and uses != in the filter; behaviour is the same here, but !== is the safer comparison.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l319", "<p class=\"summary\">Short article warning that the next video uses the old Material UI v4 package names; the current MUI v5 packages and import paths must be used instead.</p>\n<h3>Use MUI v5 packages, not the ones in the video</h3>\n<ul><li>The video installs the legacy @material-ui/core 4.6.1 and @material-ui/icons 4.5.1. These are superseded.</li><li>Add four dependencies to package.json instead: @mui/material, @mui/icons-material (both ^5.15.11), plus the styling engine @emotion/react (^11.11.4) and @emotion/styled (^11.11.0). Run npm install afterwards.</li><li>Imports change: icons come from @mui/icons-material/&lt;Name&gt;, components from @mui/material (named imports such as { Fab } and { Zoom }) rather than per-file default imports.</li><li>Using the new names avoids console warnings and errors, and matches the downloadable finished projects.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> MUI v5 needs Emotion as its default styling engine, which is why the two @emotion packages are listed.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">package.json dependencies to add</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">&quot;@mui/icons-material&quot;: &quot;^5.15.11&quot;,\n&quot;@mui/material&quot;: &quot;^5.15.11&quot;,\n&quot;@emotion/react&quot;: &quot;^11.11.4&quot;,\n&quot;@emotion/styled&quot;: &quot;^11.11.0&quot;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Imports per file (new style)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">// CreateArea.jsx\nimport AddIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Add&quot;;\nimport { Fab } from &quot;@mui/material&quot;;\nimport { Zoom } from &quot;@mui/material&quot;;\n\n// Header.jsx\nimport HighlightIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Highlight&quot;;\n\n// Note.jsx\nimport DeleteIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Delete&quot;;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Old style shown in the video (do not use)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import AddIcon from &quot;@material-ui/icons/Add&quot;;\nimport Fab from &quot;@material-ui/core/Fab&quot;;\nimport Zoom from &quot;@material-ui/core/Zoom&quot;;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Copying the old @material-ui imports from the video causes dependency or peer-version errors with current React.</li><li>Forgetting the @emotion packages gives a missing-module error when MUI components render.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l320", "<p class=\"summary\">Polishes the Keeper app by installing Material UI, swapping text buttons for icon components, using a Fab button with a Zoom transition, collapsing the create form until the textarea is clicked, and adding a patterned background. Closes by noting React alone does not persist data.</p>\n<h3>Starting point and why to use the provided project</h3>\n<ul><li>The styled demo has icons, an expanding note form, a hover effect on the add button and a patterned page background.</li><li>The supplied starter differs from the previous lesson: styles.css has new rules and the form in CreateArea carries className='create-note'. Without both, the Fab renders grey and misplaced. Copy the stylesheet and add the class if you continue your own copy.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In the reference styles.css the form, input/textarea and button rules are written as form.create-note, form.create-note input/textarea and form.create-note button. The Fab renders a real &lt;button&gt; element, so form.create-note button is what makes it yellow, round and absolutely positioned at the bottom-right of the form.</li></ul>\n<h3>Installing Material UI</h3>\n<ul><li>Material UI (MUI) is a library of pre-built React components following Google's Material Design, so appearance and some behaviour come ready-made.</li><li>The video uses two packages, core (components) and icons. Locally, install them with npm; in a CodeSandbox, use Add Dependency.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Per the preceding note lecture, use current packages: npm install @mui/material @mui/icons-material @emotion/react @emotion/styled. The reference repo uses MUI v7 with React 19.</li><li>Advantage over Bootstrap or icon fonts: each component bundles its markup, styles and logic as JSX, so it behaves like any other component you write.</li></ul>\n<h3>Icons as components</h3>\n<ul><li>Find an icon in the MUI icon search page, copy its import line, and render it as a self-closing component.</li><li>Note.jsx: import DeleteIcon and put &lt;DeleteIcon /&gt; inside the button instead of the word DELETE. The button element and its onClick stay unchanged.</li><li>CreateArea: import AddIcon and use &lt;AddIcon /&gt; in place of the text Add. Icons are SVG, so they scale cleanly, unlike a typed plus character.</li><li>Header.jsx: place &lt;HighlightIcon /&gt; inside the h1 next to the title text.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fab and Zoom</h3>\n<ul><li>Replace the plain button with MUI's Fab (floating action button), a circular button with a built-in colour change on hover. Swap the tag only: &lt;Fab onClick={submitNote}&gt;&lt;AddIcon /&gt;&lt;/Fab&gt;. Component names are capitalised, which is how JSX tells them apart from HTML tags.</li><li>Zoom is a transition wrapper. Wrap the Fab in &lt;Zoom&gt; and control it with the boolean in prop: true makes the child animate in, false hides it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Zoom requires a single child that can hold a ref; Fab qualifies.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge: expand the form on click (conditional rendering)</h3>\n<ul><li>Desired behaviour: initially only a one-row textarea shows. Clicking it reveals the title input, grows the textarea to three rows and zooms in the Fab.</li><li>Add const [isExpanded, setExpanded] = useState(false).</li><li>Define expand() that calls setExpanded(true) and attach it as onClick on the textarea.</li><li>Render the title input only when expanded, using either a ternary with null or the &amp;&amp; short-circuit form: {isExpanded &amp;&amp; &lt;input ... /&gt;}.</li><li>Make rows depend on state: rows={isExpanded ? 3 : 1}.</li><li>Pass the state to Zoom: &lt;Zoom in={isExpanded}&gt; instead of a fixed true.</li></ul>\n<h3>Background pattern</h3>\n<ul><li>transparenttextures.com offers tileable transparent PNG patterns. The lecture picks one called cubes.</li><li>Copy the pattern's URL and add background-image: url(...) to the body rule in styles.css, keeping the solid background colour (#eee in the reference) so the translucent pattern shows over it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Persistence caveat</h3>\n<ul><li>Refreshing the page loses all notes because React is only the front end and keeps state in browser memory.</li><li>Saving notes would need a backend and database, such as Node with MongoDB from earlier modules. (Extra) localStorage is a lightweight alternative for a client-only app.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">CreateArea.jsx with expanding form, Fab and Zoom (MUI v5+ imports)</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport AddIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Add&quot;;\nimport { Fab, Zoom } from &quot;@mui/material&quot;;\n\nfunction CreateArea(props) {\n  const [isExpanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const [note, setNote] = useState({ title: &quot;&quot;, content: &quot;&quot; });\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    const { name, value } = event.target;\n    setNote((prev) =&gt; ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));\n  }\n\n  function submitNote(event) {\n    props.onAdd(note);\n    setNote({ title: &quot;&quot;, content: &quot;&quot; });\n    event.preventDefault();\n  }\n\n  function expand() {\n    setExpanded(true);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;form className=&quot;create-note&quot;&gt;\n        {isExpanded &amp;&amp; (\n          &lt;input name=&quot;title&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={note.title} placeholder=&quot;Title&quot; /&gt;\n        )}\n        &lt;textarea\n          name=&quot;content&quot;\n          onClick={expand}\n          onChange={handleChange}\n          value={note.content}\n          placeholder=&quot;Take a note...&quot;\n          rows={isExpanded ? 3 : 1}\n        /&gt;\n        &lt;Zoom in={isExpanded}&gt;\n          &lt;Fab onClick={submitNote}&gt;\n            &lt;AddIcon /&gt;\n          &lt;/Fab&gt;\n        &lt;/Zoom&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default CreateArea;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Note.jsx with DeleteIcon inside the delete button</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import DeleteIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Delete&quot;;\n\nfunction Note(props) {\n  function handleClick() {\n    props.onDelete(props.id);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div className=&quot;note&quot;&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;{props.title}&lt;/h1&gt;\n      &lt;p&gt;{props.content}&lt;/p&gt;\n      &lt;button onClick={handleClick}&gt;\n        &lt;DeleteIcon /&gt;\n      &lt;/button&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Note;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Header.jsx with HighlightIcon before the title</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import HighlightIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Highlight&quot;;\n\nfunction Header() {\n  return (\n    &lt;header&gt;\n      &lt;h1&gt;\n        &lt;HighlightIcon /&gt;\n        Keeper\n      &lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;/header&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default Header;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">styles.css: body background colour plus the cubes pattern; form rules scoped to .create-note</p><pre data-lang=\"css\">body {\n  background: #eee;\n  background-image: url(&quot;https://www.transparenttextures.com/patterns/cubes.png&quot;);\n  padding: 0 16px;\n}\n\n/* the new starter scopes the form rules to the class */\nform.create-note {\n  position: relative;\n  width: 480px;\n  margin: 30px auto 20px auto;\n  background: #fff;\n  padding: 15px;\n  border-radius: 7px;\n}\n\nform.create-note button {\n  position: absolute;   /* pins the Fab to the form's bottom-right edge */\n  right: 18px;\n  bottom: -18px;\n  background: #f5ba13;  /* Keeper yellow */\n  color: #fff;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  width: 36px;\n  height: 36px;\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: Make the create-note form expand on click</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Keeper app with working add/delete, MUI installed, form className 'create-note', Fab wrapped in Zoom with in set to true, title input and 3-row textarea always visible.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Show only a one-row textarea at first; on click, show the title input, make the textarea 3 rows and zoom in the Fab.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open CreateArea.jsx (form already has className=&quot;create-note&quot;, Fab wrapped in &lt;Zoom in={true}&gt;).</li><li>Add const [isExpanded, setExpanded] = useState(false) at the top of the component.</li><li>Write function expand() { setExpanded(true); } and attach it to the textarea with onClick={expand}.</li><li>Wrap the title input in {isExpanded &amp;&amp; ( &lt;input name=&quot;title&quot; ... /&gt; )} so it is only rendered after the click (a ternary returning null works too).</li><li>Change the textarea's rows attribute from the fixed &quot;3&quot; to rows={isExpanded ? 3 : 1}.</li><li>Change &lt;Zoom in={true}&gt; to &lt;Zoom in={isExpanded}&gt; so the Fab animates in only when the form expands.</li><li>In Header.jsx import HighlightIcon from @mui/icons-material/Highlight and render &lt;HighlightIcon /&gt; inside the h1 before the text.</li><li>In styles.css add background-image: url(&quot;https://www.transparenttextures.com/patterns/cubes.png&quot;) to the body rule.</li><li>Test: reload, confirm only one row shows, click it, type a title and note, click the yellow + button and confirm the note card appears with a delete icon.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Page loads with a compact note box; clicking it expands the box with title field and animated yellow add button; adding a note still works.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"jsx\">import { useState } from &quot;react&quot;;\nimport AddIcon from &quot;@mui/icons-material/Add&quot;;\nimport { Fab, Zoom } from &quot;@mui/material&quot;;\n\nfunction CreateArea(props) {\n  const [isExpanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);\n  const [note, setNote] = useState({ title: &quot;&quot;, content: &quot;&quot; });\n\n  function handleChange(event) {\n    const { name, value } = event.target;\n    setNote((prev) =&gt; ({ ...prev, [name]: value }));\n  }\n\n  function submitNote(event) {\n    props.onAdd(note);\n    setNote({ title: &quot;&quot;, content: &quot;&quot; });\n    event.preventDefault();\n  }\n\n  function expand() {\n    setExpanded(true);\n  }\n\n  return (\n    &lt;div&gt;\n      &lt;form className=&quot;create-note&quot;&gt;\n        {isExpanded &amp;&amp; (\n          &lt;input name=&quot;title&quot; onChange={handleChange} value={note.title} placeholder=&quot;Title&quot; /&gt;\n        )}\n        &lt;textarea\n          name=&quot;content&quot;\n          onClick={expand}\n          onChange={handleChange}\n          value={note.content}\n          placeholder=&quot;Take a note...&quot;\n          rows={isExpanded ? 3 : 1}\n        /&gt;\n        &lt;Zoom in={isExpanded}&gt;\n          &lt;Fab onClick={submitNote}&gt;\n            &lt;AddIcon /&gt;\n          &lt;/Fab&gt;\n        &lt;/Zoom&gt;\n      &lt;/form&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  );\n}\n\nexport default CreateArea;</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting the create-note className or updated stylesheet leaves a grey, mispositioned button.</li><li>Using the old @material-ui imports from the video with a modern React setup; use @mui/* paths.</li><li>Capitalisation: Fab not fab, otherwise React treats it as an unknown HTML element.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The form never collapses again in this version: isExpanded only ever goes from false to true.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> MUI's Fab renders &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot;&gt;, so unlike a plain &lt;button&gt; inside a form it does not submit the form by itself; event.preventDefault() in submitNote is now just a safety net. Keep onClick={submitNote} on the Fab itself, not on Zoom.</li><li>Expecting notes to survive a refresh.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l321", "<p class=\"summary\">Advice for students who want to turn what they learn into their own product: identify the core feature, ship a minimal first version and iterate from user feedback.</p>\n<h3>Building your own product</h3>\n<ul><li>Write down every feature you imagine, then trim the list to the one thing that makes the product different.</li><li>Build the smallest usable version first rather than the final grand vision. Each stage should already be usable, instead of a half-built big project nobody can use.</li><li>Overly ambitious mash-up ideas tend to stall; starting small keeps motivation up.</li><li>Release early and collect feedback from real users on what works, what is missing and what hurts the experience, then improve in cycles. This loop is presented as essential for success.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Starting with a huge feature set and never reaching a usable release.</li></ul>");
